/* Specline — specification library. Every record carries its category (c). */
const SPECS = {

extension: { name:"House Extension", region:"England",
 cats:["Planning & Permitted Development","General Notes","Site Preparation","Structure","Foundations","Ground Floors","External Walls","Additional Notes for Walls","Roofs","Internal Works","Means of Escape & Fire Regs","Windows & Doors","Ventilation","Condensation (C2)","Drainage","Services","Conservation of Fuel & Power","Renewable Energy"],
 buildups:[
  {g:"FD", c:"Foundations", t:"Trench Fill Foundation", u:"Wall + 300 wide, 1000 deep",
   tgt:"To Approved Document A Section 2E and BS 8004:2015 (+A1:2020) — width from Table 10 for the load and subsoil, or the structural engineer's design",
   p:[
    "Mass concrete trench fill foundation not less than 750mm thick, of a width not less than the thickness of the wall plus 300mm and in no case less than 450mm, taken to not less than 1000mm below finished ground level and cast against undisturbed ground in a trench excavated to the full width. Concrete to BS EN 206:2013 (+A2:2021) and BS 8500-2.",
    "Foundations supporting internal loadbearing walls to be taken to not less than 600mm below finished ground level. Foundations are to be taken below the invert of any adjacent drain and are not to undermine it. Sulfate-resisting cement, or the BS 8500 design chemical class given by the ground investigation, to be used where the ground or the groundwater requires it.",
    "Where the ground falls the foundation is to be stepped. No step is to exceed the thickness of the foundation, and the higher foundation is to overlap the lower by not less than twice the height of the step, the thickness of the foundation, or 300mm, whichever is the greater — not less than 1m where the foundation is trench fill. Masonry below ground to be stepped to suit.",
    "All work in accordance with Requirement A1/2 of the Building Regulations 2010 (as amended) and BS 8004:2015 (+A1:2020) Code of Practice for Foundations. The depth and the size of the foundation are to be approved on site by the Building Control Officer to suit the conditions found in the excavation.",
    "NOTE — The width is to be confirmed against Table 10 of Approved Document A for the wall load and the subsoil, or against the structural engineer's design. Where the wall is thin, the wall thickness plus 300mm can fall below the 450mm minimum for trench fill; the 450mm governs.",
    "NOTE — Where adverse soil conditions, a change of soil type, made ground, soft spots or major tree roots are found in the excavation, work is to stop, building control is to be notified and the structural engineer's advice obtained before any concrete is placed."
   ]},
  {g:"FD", c:"Foundations", t:"Strip Foundation", u:"Wall + 300 × 225, 1000 deep",
   tgt:"To Approved Document A Table 10 and Section 2E — thickness not less than the projection each side and not less than 225mm",
   p:[
    "Concrete strip foundation with the width taken from Table 10 of Approved Document A for the wall load and the subsoil, not less than the thickness of the wall plus 300mm, and the thickness of the concrete not less than the projection of the foundation beyond each face of the wall and in no case less than 225mm. Concrete to BS EN 206:2013 (+A2:2021) and BS 8500-2, cast on undisturbed ground and taken to not less than 1000mm below finished ground level.",
    "Foundations supporting internal loadbearing walls to be taken to not less than 600mm below finished ground level. Foundations are to be taken below the invert of any adjacent drain and are not to undermine it. Sulfate-resisting cement, or the BS 8500 design chemical class given by the ground investigation, to be used where the ground or the groundwater requires it.",
    "Where the ground falls the foundation is to be stepped. No step is to exceed the thickness of the foundation, and the higher foundation is to overlap the lower by not less than twice the height of the step, the thickness of the foundation, or 300mm, whichever is the greater — not less than 1m where the foundation is trench fill. Masonry below ground to be stepped to suit.",
    "All work in accordance with Requirement A1/2 of the Building Regulations 2010 (as amended) and BS 8004:2015 (+A1:2020) Code of Practice for Foundations. The depth and the size of the foundation are to be approved on site by the Building Control Officer to suit the conditions found in the excavation.",
    "NOTE — The width is to be confirmed against Table 10 for the wall load and the subsoil, or against the structural engineer's design.",
    "NOTE — Where adverse soil conditions, a change of soil type, made ground, soft spots or major tree roots are found in the excavation, work is to stop, building control is to be notified and the structural engineer's advice obtained before any concrete is placed."
   ]},
  {g:"FD", c:"Foundations", t:"Raft Foundation — Single Storey Extension", u:"Engineer's design · 0.17 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To the structural engineer's design and calculations. Thermally, to achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K (calculated 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 at a perimeter/area ratio of 0.5 on clay)",
   p:[
    "Reinforced concrete raft foundation and floor slab to the structural engineer's design and calculations. Steel reinforcement to be lapped not less than 450mm for both mesh and bar, with not less than 40mm cover to the concrete.",
    "Excavate to firm undisturbed load-bearing strata, free from contamination, and lay 150mm of well consolidated hardcore capable of supporting not less than 50 kN/m². Blind the hardcore and cast the reinforced raft to the engineer's detail, with edge beams beneath all loadbearing walls and a thickening beneath any pier.",
    "Over the raft, a polyethylene damp proof membrane of not less than 1200 gauge, lapped 150mm and sealed at all joints and linked to the wall damp proof course; 90mm SOPRATHERM GA4000 — formerly Celotex — or equivalent, with all joints tightly butted; 25mm perimeter upstand insulation carried around the full edge of the floor; and a 75mm sand and cement screed, or 65mm where reinforced, ready to receive the floor finish.",
    "Wall fixings at the junction with the existing building are to suit the differential settlement expected between the extension and the existing structure, and internal stiffening beams are to be provided where the engineer requires them. All new foundations are to be taken below the invert of any adjacent drain.",
    "NOTE — This detail is suitable for single storey extensions to domestic buildings only. Anything else is to be designed by the structural engineer without reference to this entry.",
    "NOTE — 90mm calculates at 0.17 W/m²K at a perimeter/area ratio of 0.5 on clay and meets the 0.18 W/m²K standard for a new thermal element. On a small extension with three exposed sides the ratio rises and the U-value with it: at a ratio of 1.0 the same build-up calculates at 0.19 and does not comply. Confirm the ratio for this floor and increase the insulation where it is needed."
   ]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls", t:"Full Fill Cavity Wall", u:"0.17 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K (actual U-value achieved 0.17 W/m²K)", mf:{"k":"wall","lim":0.18,"p":{"outer":"brick","cavity":100,"fill":"full","insulation":"k106","thickness":90,"inner":"a011","mortar":"gp","finish":"pbdabs","ties":"ss45x90","gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"cavfull","f":"90mm Kingspan Kooltherm K106"}]}, p:[
    "External cavity wall to be built up as a 103mm facing brick outer leaf matched to the existing dwelling, a 100mm cavity fully filled with 90mm Kingspan Kooltherm K106, and a 100mm aircrete blockwork inner leaf of 0.11 W/mK thermal conductivity — Celcon Solar, Thermalite Turbo or equivalent, 2.9N with the strength grade confirmed by the structural engineer — finished internally with 12.5mm plasterboard on dabs. A nominal 10mm residual cavity is to remain between the face of the insulation and the back of the outer leaf. All masonry laid in 1:1:6 cement:lime:sand mortar. This build-up calculates at 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 allowing for mortar joints, wall ties and an air-gap correction of 0.01; with a 0.15 W/mK block (Celcon Standard, Thermalite Shield) it calculates at 0.18 W/m²K and meets the limiting value with no margin. Where a block of a different thermal conductivity is proposed, the contractor is to provide the manufacturer's project-specific U-value calculation for the build-up as it will be built, demonstrating a maximum of 0.18 W/m²K, before any materials are ordered.",
    "Insulation boards to be installed strictly to the manufacturer's published instructions and the current BBA certificate, tightly butted on every edge with vertical joints staggered course to course. Corner, reveal and junction details to follow the certificate rather than being improvised on site.",
    "The exposure of the site is to be assessed for the suitability of full fill construction before boards are ordered. Where exposure is severe, a partial fill build-up is to be adopted instead.",
    "Stainless steel wall ties to be provided at 450mm vertical and 900mm horizontal centres, staggered, and at 225mm vertical centres within 225mm of all unbonded jambs, reveals and movement joints. Ties to be set with a slight fall to the outer leaf and kept free of mortar droppings."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls", t:"Partial Fill Cavity Wall", u:"0.16 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K", mf:{"k":"wall","lim":0.18,"p":{"outer":"brick","cavity":150,"fill":"partial","insulation":"k108","thickness":100,"inner":"a011","mortar":"gp","finish":"pbdabs","ties":"ss45x90","gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"cavpart","f":"100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K108 partial fill cavity board"}]}, p:[
    "Where a residual cavity is required, new cavity wall to comprise of 103mm suitable facing brick, a 150mm cavity with 100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K108 partial fill cavity board retained against the inner leaf with proprietary retaining clips and a minimum 50mm clear residual cavity maintained, a 100mm aircrete block inner leaf of 0.11 W/mK thermal conductivity (Celcon Solar, Thermalite Turbo or equivalent, 2.9N with the strength grade confirmed by the structural engineer), and 12.5mm plasterboard on dabs.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.16 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 by the combined method, allowing for mortar joints in the inner leaf, stainless steel wall ties at 450mm vertical and 900mm horizontal centres and an air-gap correction at level 1; with a 0.15 W/mK block (Celcon Standard, Thermalite Shield) it also calculates at 0.16, but the 0.11 W/mK block is what gives a 0.022 W/mK board of the same thickness the margin to meet 0.18. Insulation thickness to be confirmed by U-value calculation for the build-up adopted where the inner leaf or the board differs from the above."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls", t:"Timber Frame External Wall", u:"0.15 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K (actual U-value achieved 0.15 W/m²K)", mf:{"k":"frame","lim":0.18,"p":{"insulation":"k112","thickness":140,"studWidth":38,"studDepth":140,"spacing":600,"sheathing":"osb9","lining":"k118_375","void":0,"gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"frame","f":"140mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"},{"r":"lining","f":"37.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "Timber frame external wall to comprise 103mm suitable facing brick or proprietary render system outer leaf, minimum 50mm clear ventilated and drained cavity, breather membrane, 9mm OSB3 racking board, 140mm x 38mm C16 studs at 600mm centres to the frame designer's specification with the full stud depth filled with 140mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK), a continuous vapour control layer on the warm side, and 37.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard with joints taped as the internal finish. This build-up calculates at 0.15 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 by the combined method, allowing for the studs bridging the insulation at 6.3% of the area at 600mm centres and an air-gap correction of 0.01; with 12.5mm plasterboard alone in place of the insulated board it calculates at 0.18 and meets the standard with no margin, so the insulated board is not to be omitted.",
    "Stainless steel wall ties fixed to the studs at 450mm vertical centres and at every stud horizontally. Cavity barriers to be provided in accordance with Approved Document B. All timber to be treated and isolated from masonry by a damp proof course."]},
  {g:"IW", c:"Internal Works", t:"Insulated Stud Partition", u:"",
   tgt:"",
   p:[
    "Internal stud partition to comprise 89mm x 38mm C16 studs at 400mm centres with head and sole plates, solid noggins at board joints and at all fixings for sanitaryware, radiators and wall-hung items. Infill the full stud depth with 89mm acoustic mineral wool insulation. Line both faces with 12.5mm plasterboard with joints staggered and all perimeters sealed."]},
  {g:"IW", c:"Internal Works", t:"Internal Masonry Wall", u:"",
   tgt:"",
   p:[
    "Internal loadbearing masonry wall to comprise 100mm dense concrete blockwork to the structural engineer's specification, laid in 1:1:6 mortar, plastered both faces or finished with 12.5mm plasterboard on dabs."]},
  {g:"GF", c:"Ground Floors", t:"Solid Floor — Insulation Over Slab (Screed Finish)", u:"0.18 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K   |   P/A ratio 0.5", mf:{"k":"floorSolid","lim":0.18,"p":{"ground":"clay","insulation":"k103","thickness":80,"slab":"c100","finish":"screed65","edge":"e25x150","pa":0.7,"wall":300},"s":[{"r":"floor","f":"80mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Excavate to formation and lay 150mm minimum well-compacted hardcore, in layers not exceeding 150mm, blinded with 50mm of sand or other suitable fine material to protect the membrane above.",
    "Cast a ground-bearing slab of not less than 100mm in GEN1 or RC20 concrete, conforming to BS EN 206 and BS 8500.",
    "Lay a proprietary polyethylene damp proof membrane not less than 1200 gauge (300 micron), or 1600 gauge where required by the local authority, site conditions or where acting as a combined gas/radon membrane, installed in accordance with the manufacturer's recommendations. Lap and seal joints in accordance with the manufacturer's details and link the membrane with the damp proof course within the walls.",
    "Provide 80mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) over the damp proof membrane with all joints tightly butted; this build-up calculates at 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a floor with a perimeter/area ratio of 0.5 on clay and at 0.18 at a ratio of 0.7, so 80mm complies up to a ratio of 0.7 and not beyond it. Above that ratio 100mm is to be used, which calculates at 0.15 at a ratio of 0.7. The perimeter/area ratio of the whole enlarged dwelling may be used in the calculation (Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.2 note). The ratio for this floor is to be calculated by the designer and one thickness stated on this specification before it is issued; the choice is not to be left to be made on site.",
    "NOTE — Calculate the perimeter/area ratio for this floor, or for the whole enlarged dwelling, and fix the insulation thickness before issue. The floor calculator does it and writes the figure into the build-up. A specification that offers two thicknesses leaves the U-value to whoever is ordering the boards.",
    "Continue 25mm perimeter insulation around floor edges to reduce thermal bridging.",
    "Provide a vapour control layer over the insulation (and beneath the insulation where required by the insulation manufacturer) with 150mm lapped and sealed joints, turned up at room perimeters behind the skirting to maintain continuity.",
    "Finish with 65mm minimum sand/cement screed, reinforced where required in accordance with the screed manufacturer's recommendations and ready to receive the final floor finish."]},
  {g:"GF", c:"Ground Floors", t:"Solid Floor — Insulation Below Slab", u:"0.17 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K", mf:{"k":"floorSolid","lim":0.18,"p":{"ground":"clay","insulation":"tf70","thickness":100,"slab":"c100","finish":"none","edge":"e25x150","pa":0.7,"wall":300},"s":[{"r":"floor","f":"100mm Kingspan Thermafloor TF70 (thermal conductivity 0.022 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Where the slab is to be power floated and left exposed, or where underfloor heating is laid within the slab, provide 150mm minimum well-compacted hardcore blinded with 50mm sand, polyethylene damp proof membrane not less than 1200 gauge, 100mm Kingspan Thermafloor TF70 (thermal conductivity 0.022 W/mK) below the slab, and 100mm minimum GEN1 ground-bearing concrete slab over. This build-up calculates at 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a floor with a perimeter/area ratio of 0.7 on clay.",
    "Continue 25mm perimeter insulation upstand to the full depth of the slab at all abutments with external and internal walls."]},
  {g:"GF", c:"Ground Floors", t:"Suspended Timber Ground Floor", u:"0.17 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K", mf:{"k":"floorSusp","lim":0.18,"p":{"ground":"clay","insulation":"k103","thickness":150,"deck":"timber","joistDepth":150,"spacing":400,"pa":0.7,"wall":300,"vent":1500},"s":[{"r":"floor","f":"150mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Suspended timber ground floor over a minimum 150mm clear ventilated void, to comprise floor joists to the structural engineer's design and calculations, shown here as 47mm x 150mm C24 joists at 400mm centres, indicative only and to be confirmed before construction, with 150mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) between the joists on breathable insulation support netting. This build-up calculates at 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a floor with a perimeter/area ratio of 0.7 on clay.",
    "Ventilate the void beneath the joists through to opposing external walls, equivalent to 1500mm² per metre run, by means of proprietary air bricks and telescopic ducts carried through the cavity.",
    "The oversite is to prevent plant growth; 100mm concrete on well-compacted hardcore may be used, or 50mm concrete on a 1200 gauge damp proof membrane. All timber to be isolated from masonry by a damp proof course. Finish with 18mm moisture resistant tongued and grooved flooring grade board."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"Roofs", t:"Warm Deck Flat Roof", u:"0.15 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.15 W/m²K", mf:{"k":"flat","lim":0.15,"p":{"insulation":"tr27","thickness":150,"joistDepth":200,"spacing":400,"fixing":"adhered"},"s":[{"r":"flat","f":"150mm Kingspan Thermaroof TR27 insulation (thermal conductivity 0.024 W/mK at 120mm and over)"}]}, p:[
    "Warm deck flat roof to comprise timber joists to the structural engineer's design and calculations, shown here as 47mm x 200mm C24 joists at 400mm centres, indicative only and to be confirmed before construction, 18mm external quality plywood decking or OSB3 laid to falls, vapour control layer fully bonded to the deck with all laps sealed, 150mm Kingspan Thermaroof TR27 insulation (thermal conductivity 0.024 W/mK at 120mm and over) laid break-bonded in two layers and fully adhered, and a single ply or high performance built-up felt waterproofing system to the manufacturer's specification and BBA certificate. This build-up calculates at 0.15 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 fully adhered; where the insulation is mechanically fixed the fastener correction takes it to 0.16 and 160mm is to be used instead.",
    "Falls to be designed to a fall of not less than 1:40 so that a finished fall of not less than 1:80 is achieved after construction deviations and deflection, in accordance with BS 6229, set out by firrings or by tapered insulation as detailed. Cold deck flat roof construction is not to be used.",
    "Provide proprietary edge trims, upstands of not less than 150mm at all abutments, and cavity trays with stop ends and weep holes where the roof abuts a cavity wall."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"Roofs", t:"Pitched Roof — Insulation at Rafter Level", u:"0.14 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.15 W/m²K", mf:{"k":"rafter","lim":0.15,"p":{"insulation":"k107","thickness":150,"rafterDepth":150,"spacing":400,"under":"k118_625","gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"rafter","f":"150mm Kingspan Kooltherm K107 pitched roof board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"},{"r":"lining","f":"62.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "Roof covering to match existing on battens and counter-battens over a breathable roofing underlay to BS 5534, on 47mm x 150mm minimum rafters to the structural engineer's design. Provide 150mm Kingspan Kooltherm K107 pitched roof board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) fully filling the rafter depth and 62.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard beneath the rafters, with all joints tightly butted and taped. This build-up calculates at 0.14 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 at 400mm rafter centres, allowing for rafter bridging and an air-gap correction of 0.01; 52.5mm under-rafter board in place of 62.5mm calculates at 0.15 and meets the target with no margin.",
    "Where a non-breathable underlay is used, maintain a clear 50mm ventilated air gap above the insulation with ventilation equivalent to a continuous 25mm gap at the eaves and 5mm at the ridge.",
    "Provide a continuous vapour control layer on the warm side of the insulation, with all laps, junctions and service penetrations sealed, to prevent the migration of warm moist air into the roof construction."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"Roofs", t:"Pitched Roof — Insulation at Ceiling Level", u:"0.11 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.15 W/m²K",
   p:[
    "Provide 100mm mineral wool quilt between ceiling joists and 300mm mineral wool quilt laid cross-wise over the joists, or equivalent to achieve the target U-value. Ceiling finish 12.5mm plasterboard with skim. This build-up calculates at 0.11 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 allowing for the joist fraction at 400mm centres; 300mm total calculates at 0.15.",
    "Ventilate the roof void with ventilation equivalent to a continuous 10mm gap at the eaves on two opposite sides, plus 5mm at the ridge where the pitch exceeds 35° or the span exceeds 10m. Provide proprietary eaves ventilators and insulation stops to ensure the ventilation path is not blocked by the insulation.",
    "Fully fill the gap between the wall plate and the eaves ventilator with flexible insulation of R-value not less than 1.2 m²K/W, tucked down the head of the cavity for continuity with the wall insulation."]},
  {g:"GF", c:"Ground Floors", t:"Block and Beam Floor", u:"0.17 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K", mf:{"k":"floorSusp","lim":0.18,"p":{"ground":"clay","insulation":"k103","thickness":90,"deck":"bb","pa":0.7,"wall":300,"vent":1500},"s":[{"r":"floor","f":"90mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Precast prestressed concrete beams to the manufacturer's design, spanning to loadbearing walls over a minimum 150mm clear ventilated void, with 100mm infill blocks laid between and the joints grouted or a dry sand and cement mix brushed in and wetted.",
    "Ventilate the void through to opposing external walls by proprietary air bricks and telescopic ducts carried through the cavity. Oversite to be treated to prevent plant growth.",
    "Lay a polyethylene damp proof membrane of not less than 1200 gauge over the beam and block deck, lapped and taped and linked to the damp proof course, then 90mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) with a 25mm perimeter upstand, a vapour control layer where required by the insulation manufacturer, and a 65mm minimum sand/cement screed or a floating floor deck. This build-up calculates at 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a floor with a perimeter/area ratio of 0.7 on clay.",
    "Beam layout, bearing and any trimming to openings to the manufacturer's design, coordinated with the structural engineer."]},
  {g:"GF", c:"Ground Floors", t:"In-Situ Suspended Concrete Floor", u:"0.17 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K", mf:{"k":"floorSusp","lim":0.18,"p":{"ground":"clay","insulation":"k103","thickness":90,"deck":"bb","pa":0.7,"wall":300,"vent":1500},"s":[{"r":"floor","f":"90mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Reinforced in-situ concrete slab to the structural engineer's design, spanning to loadbearing walls or beams over a minimum 150mm clear ventilated void, with reinforcement, cover and concrete grade as the engineer's specification. The detail is drawn on a 150mm slab; the engineer's design governs the thickness actually built.",
    "Provide a minimum 150mm clear ventilated void beneath with through ventilation to opposing external walls. Lay a polyethylene damp proof membrane of not less than 1200 gauge over the slab, then 90mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) with a 25mm perimeter upstand and a 65mm minimum sand/cement screed. This build-up calculates at 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a floor with a perimeter/area ratio of 0.7 on clay."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls", t:"Solid Wall — Internally Insulated", u:"0.26 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve the standard for a retained or renovated thermal element in Approved Document L, Volume 1", mf:{"k":"lined","lim":0.3,"p":{"wall":"brick215","gap":10,"lining":"k118_725","gapLevel":0},"s":[{"r":"lining","f":"72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Where an existing solid masonry wall is retained within the thermal envelope, provide 72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) to the internal face of the existing wall, shown here on a 215mm solid brick wall, or 62.5mm to an uninsulated cavity wall, on dabs or on treated battens, with all joints tightly butted and taped and a continuous vapour control layer where the board does not incorporate one. The 72.5mm board on a 215mm solid brick wall calculates at 0.26 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 and the 62.5mm board on an uninsulated cavity wall at 0.29, both better than the 0.30 W/m²K retained-element standard. The value achieved depends on the construction and thickness of the existing wall and is to be confirmed for the wall on this job.",
    "The existing wall is to be inspected for rising and penetrating damp, defective pointing and cracked render before lining, and every defect made good. External rainwater goods are to be checked and repaired.",
    "NOTE — Internal insulation of a solid wall moves the dew point into the existing masonry and materially increases the risk of interstitial condensation. A condensation risk analysis to BS 5250 is to be carried out where the wall is exposed or of solid construction, and the result recorded."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls", t:"External Wall Insulation", u:"0.30 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve the standard for a renovated thermal element in Approved Document L, Volume 1",
   p:[
    "Proprietary external wall insulation system applied to the existing masonry, drawn here as a 215mm solid brick wall and to be confirmed on site, comprising 100mm insulation boards of not more than 0.032 W/mK mechanically fixed and adhered, reinforced base coat with mesh, and a finish coat, all to the system manufacturer's specification and current BBA certificate. Components from a single manufacturer's system throughout.",
    "Provide beads, stop beads, movement joints and a bellcast at the base, stopped a minimum 150mm above finished ground level so the damp proof course is not bridged. Detail reveals, cills, eaves and verges to the system manufacturer's standard details, and extend cills and any projecting features as required.",
    "NOTE — The combustibility of the insulation and of the system as a whole is to be checked against Approved Document B, particularly where the wall is within 1m of the relevant boundary. Confirm the required reaction to fire classification before specifying."]},
  {g:"IW", c:"Internal Works", t:"Metal Stud Partition", u:"",
   tgt:"",
   p:[
    "Non-loadbearing internal partition of 12.5mm plasterboard to BS EN 520 lining both faces of 50mm minimum galvanised steel C-section studs, at 600mm centres, the stud depth infilled with 50mm mineral wool acoustic insulation of 10–45 kg/m³ density (25mm minimum where the stud depth is shallower). Studs in matching galvanised steel U-tracks at head and floor, all to BS EN 14195. Studs and tracks to be not less than 0.5mm gauge.",
    "Reduce the studs to 400mm centres where higher impact resistance is required or where the partition carries a heavy finish such as tiling. Head and floor tracks to be fixed securely to the substrate with fixings appropriate to it.",
    "Boards to BS EN 520, moisture-resistant in bathrooms and kitchens, fixed with drywall screws at 300mm centres, joints staggered and finished with joint tape and filler. Finish with a plaster skim.",
    "Where the partition forms part of a fire-resisting enclosure the lining is to be fireline plasterboard: one layer each side is the minimum for 30 minutes, and two layers each side where the manufacturer's tested detail requires it.",
    "Partitions separating bedrooms, bathrooms and habitable rooms are to achieve not less than 40 dB Rw airborne sound insulation in accordance with Approved Document E: fill the studs with mineral wool acoustic insulation of 10–45 kg/m³ and line each side with two layers of plasterboard, or use a proprietary system tested and certified to achieve the standard.",
    "Where the partition forms part of an escape route it is to give not less than 30 minutes fire resistance in accordance with Approved Document B. Use a proprietary system installed to the manufacturer's tested details — British Gypsum, Knauf, Siniat or equivalent.",
    "Services within the stud cavity to have cables protected by grommets, and any notching or opening of studs to follow the manufacturer's guidance.",
    "Provide proprietary fixing plates or timber pattresses at all fixings for sanitaryware, radiators and wall-hung items. All perimeters sealed."]},
  {g:"IF", c:"Internal Works", t:"Intermediate Floor — Solid Timber Joists", u:"",
   tgt:"",
   p:[
    "Floor joists of solid structural timber to the structural engineer's design and span, at centres as scheduled, with herringbone strutting or solid blocking at not more than 1.8m centres and at mid-span. The detail is drawn on 47mm x 220mm C24 joists at 400mm centres; the engineer's design governs the size and the centres for the span actually built.",
    "Notching and drilling to be within the limits given in the engineer's specification: notches only in the top of the joist between 0.07 and 0.25 of the span and not deeper than 0.125 of the joist depth; holes only on the neutral axis, not more than 0.25 of the depth in diameter, spaced not less than three diameters apart, and between 0.25 and 0.4 of the span. No notch or hole in an engineered member.",
    "Deck with 18mm or 22mm moisture resistant tongued and grooved flooring grade board, glued and screwed. Infill the joist void with 100mm mineral wool of not less than 10 kg/m³ laid between the joists, and line the underside with 12.5mm plasterboard to satisfy Requirement E2 where the floor separates a bedroom or a room containing a WC from other rooms.",
    "Provide galvanised lateral restraint straps to walls at not more than 2m centres, carried over a minimum of three joists with solid noggins."]},
  {g:"IF", c:"Internal Works", t:"Intermediate Floor — Engineered Open Web Joists", u:"",
   tgt:"",
   p:[
    "Proprietary engineered open web or I-section joists to the manufacturer's design and the structural engineer's approval, at centres as scheduled, with web stiffeners, hangers and bearing details strictly to the manufacturer's instructions. The detail is drawn on 250mm open web joists at 400mm centres; the manufacturer's design governs the depth and the centres for the span actually built.",
    "No engineered joist is to be notched, drilled or cut other than at the positions and sizes expressly permitted by the manufacturer. Services to be run through the open webs or the manufacturer's designated service holes.",
    "Deck as the solid joist floor with 18mm moisture resistant tongued and grooved flooring grade board, 100mm mineral wool of not less than 10 kg/m³ laid between the joists, and 12.5mm plasterboard to the underside. Where the underside is exposed or forms part of a fire resisting element, the protection to the joists is to be to the manufacturer's tested specification — an engineered joist has less inherent fire resistance than a solid timber joist of the same depth."]}
 ],
 notes:[
  {c:"Planning & Permitted Development", t:"Planning Status and Permitted Development", p:[
    "The planning status of the works is to be confirmed before construction. Where the extension is carried out under Permitted Development, it is to be built strictly within the limits and conditions of Class A, Part 1, Schedule 2 of the Town and Country Planning (General Permitted Development) (England) Order 2015 as amended. Where planning permission has been granted, all conditions attached to the permission are to be discharged in accordance with their terms.",
    "Building Regulations approval does not confer planning permission, and planning permission does not confer Building Regulations approval. Both are required where applicable.",
    "Where the property is listed, in a conservation area, or subject to an Article 4 Direction, separate consent is required and no work is to commence until that consent is in place."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Compliance and Scope", p:[
    "All works to be carried out in accordance with the Building Regulations 2010 (as amended) and the Approved Documents current at the date of issue. All materials and workmanship to comply with Approved Document 7 (Regulation 7).",
    "This specification is to be read in conjunction with the drawing pack issued with this specification, the structural engineer's design and calculations, and any specialist sub-contractor design. Where this specification and the drawings differ, refer to the designer before proceeding.",
    "All dimensions to be checked on site before fabrication or ordering. Do not scale from the drawings. All works to be carried out in a safe manner in accordance with the CDM Regulations 2015.",
    "Products to be installed strictly in accordance with the manufacturer's current published instructions and relevant BBA certificate. Where a proprietary system is specified, components from a single manufacturer's system are to be used throughout."]},
  {c:"Site Preparation", t:"Site Preparation and Resistance to Contaminants", p:[
    "The ground to be covered by the extension to be cleared of turf, vegetable matter and any material liable to decompose, in accordance with Approved Document C.",
    "Where excavation reveals contaminants, made ground or evidence of landfill, work is to stop and the designer and Building Control notified before proceeding.",
    "Provide subsoil drainage where the water table can rise to within 250mm of the lowest floor, or where surface water would otherwise enter the building.",
    "Where the property lies in a radon affected area, provide radon protective measures in accordance with BRE Report BR 211. Confirm the affected area status for the postcode before issue."]},
  {c:"Foundations", t:"Foundations", p:[
    "Foundations to the structural engineer's design and calculations, to be submitted to Building Control prior to the affected work commencing. All excavations to be inspected and approved by the Building Control Officer before concrete is placed.",
    "Excavate trial holes adjacent to the existing building to establish the depth and form of the existing footings before the foundation design is finalised. New foundations to be taken to at least the depth of the existing where the extension abuts.",
    "Foundations to be taken down clear of the influence of tree roots and seasonal ground movement. Where trees are present or have been removed, refer to NHBC Standards Chapter 4.2 for depth and heave precautions.",
    "Concrete to be of a mix and strength class to the engineer's specification, complying with BS EN 206 and BS 8500, and where sulfates are present, to the appropriate design chemical class.",
    "No service is to be built into or cast through a foundation without the engineer's written approval."]},
  {c:"Foundations", t:"Substructure Walls Below Damp Proof Course", p:[
    "Walls below ground level to be constructed in dense concrete blockwork of minimum 7.0 N/mm² compressive strength, in a mortar designation suitable for work below DPC, with the cavity filled with lean mix concrete to a level not less than 225mm below the damp proof course, finished with a fall to the outside, with weep holes above the fill.",
    "Where blockwork below ground exceeds 900mm in height, 215mm thick blockwork to be adopted (blocks laid flat and coursed to 225mm vertical centres) and backfilling carried out equally to both faces using well-graded granular material.",
    "Contractor to obtain confirmation from the manufacturer as to the suitability of blocks and bricks for use below DPC."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Structural Design — General", p:[
    "All structural elements including foundations, steelwork, padstones, lintels, joists and trimming to the structural engineer's design and calculations, which are to be submitted to Building Control prior to the affected work commencing.",
    "Existing structure to be opened up as required by the Building Control Officer. Any condition differing from that assumed in the design to be reported to the structural engineer immediately and work in that area stopped.",
    "The contractor is responsible for the design, installation, maintenance and removal of all temporary works, and for the stability of the existing building at all times."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Structural Steelwork", p:[
    "Structural steelwork to be designed, fabricated and erected in accordance with the current Eurocode for steel structures and the National Structural Steelwork Specification, to the grades stated in the engineer's specification. Masonry to be designed and constructed in accordance with the current Eurocode for masonry structures.",
    "All steel beams and columns to be fire protected to achieve the required period of fire resistance, generally a minimum of 30 minutes for a two storey dwelling, by encasement in two layers of 12.5mm fire-rated plasterboard or an approved proprietary system.",
    "Padstones to be as detailed by the engineer. Cut bricks or blocks beneath padstones will not be accepted. All lintels and beams to have a minimum 150mm end bearing.",
    "Steelwork that carries only the roof of a single-storey extension does not itself require fire protection; steelwork carrying a floor, a wall above or the enclosure of a stair is to be protected as the Lintels, Beams and Fire Protection note."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Lateral Restraint", p:[
    "Provide galvanised lateral restraint straps at not more than 2m centres at floor and roof level, carried over a minimum of three joists with solid noggins between. Joists to be doubled up beneath all stud partitions running parallel to the joists."]},
  {c:"Additional Notes for Walls", t:"Cavity Closers and Reveals", p:[
    "Provide proprietary insulated cavity closers to all jambs, heads and cills, installed to close the cavity with the insulation held against its full length with no gaps. Window and door frames to overlap the cavity closer by between 30mm and 50mm for windows, and 50mm for doors, to maintain continuity of insulation at the reveal.",
    "Where required to achieve the design psi-value, add 37.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard to the reveal, butting the frame and sealed with flexible sealant."]},
  {c:"Additional Notes for Walls", t:"Lintels", p:[
    "Lintels to the structural engineer's design and calculations, with a minimum 150mm end bearing onto new or existing masonry. Insulated cavity wall lintels to be used, or an independent steel lintel externally with an independent concrete lintel internally and an insulated reveal, to limit thermal bridging at the head.",
    "Manufacturer's full design and specification details for all lintels to be submitted to Building Control prior to installation."]},
  {c:"Additional Notes for Walls", t:"Junction With the Existing Dwelling", p:[
    "Where the extension abuts the existing dwelling, form the junction to maintain continuity of insulation and of the air barrier, and to accommodate differential movement. Provide vertical movement joints with proprietary wall tie connectors, or toothing and bonding into the existing as directed by the structural engineer.",
    "Existing external walls that become internal are to have render and any impermeable finish removed where required, and the cavity is to be closed and sealed at the junction."]},
  {c:"Additional Notes for Walls", t:"Render, Cladding and Chases", p:[
    "Where a rendered finish is specified, render to be a proprietary system applied in accordance with the manufacturer's instructions and BBA certificate, on a suitable background, with beads, stop beads and movement joints as the system requires. Render is not to bridge the damp proof course and is to be stopped a minimum 150mm above finished ground level with a bellcast bead.",
    "Where cladding is specified, it is to be fixed on treated battens over a breather membrane, with a ventilated and drained cavity behind, and cavity barriers provided in accordance with Approved Document B.",
    "Chases in masonry are not to exceed one third of the wall thickness in horizontal runs or one sixth in vertical runs, and are not to be cut back to back."]},
  {c:"Roofs", t:"Rainwater Goods", p:[
    "Gutters and downpipes to be sized for the roof area served in accordance with Approved Document H3, generally 112mm half round gutters and 68mm downpipes for roof areas up to 40m². Gutters laid to a fall of not less than 1:350 with outlets sized to the flow, downpipes discharging over a trapped gully or direct to the surface water drain, and rainwater pipes kept separate from the foul system unless the sewer is combined."]},
  {c:"Internal Works", t:"Staircases and Guarding", p:[
    "Private stairs to Approved Document K: pitch not more than 42 degrees, rise between 150mm and 220mm, going not less than 220mm, twice the rise plus the going between 550mm and 700mm, all rises and goings in a flight equal, headroom of 2m over the pitch line and landings, and a handrail on at least one side at 900mm to 1000mm above the pitch line, on both sides where the flight is 1m or wider, with not less than 600mm clear between handrails where two are fitted. Tapered treads to have a going of not less than 50mm at the narrow end and to satisfy the going rule at the centre line (or 270mm from the narrow end where the stair is wider than 1m); landings at the top and bottom of every flight at least as long as the flight is wide and level, with no door swinging over a landing other than a cupboard door which leaves 400mm clear; open risers not to pass a 100mm sphere; timber stairs to BS 585 with the strings, treads and risers glued and wedged and the newels bolted to the floor structure.",
    "Guarding to every flight, landing, open side, balcony or roof terrace where the drop exceeds 600mm: 900mm high to stairs and internal landings and 1100mm to external balconies and roof terraces, not climbable by children, not passing a 100mm sphere, and designed for the horizontal load in BS EN 1991-1-1 (0.36 kN/m for a dwelling)."]},
  {c:"Internal Works", t:"Internal Finishes and Joinery", p:[
    "Plasterboard to be fixed and finished in accordance with the manufacturer's instructions, with all perimeters and service penetrations sealed to maintain airtightness. Provide solid noggins at all board joints and at fixings for sanitaryware, radiators and wall-hung items.",
    "Internal wall and ceiling linings to achieve the surface spread of flame classification required by Approved Document B for the room or circulation space concerned."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Smoke and Heat Alarms", p:[
    "Provide a fire detection and alarm system to BS 5839-6 of not less than Grade D1 Category LD2 — mains powered, interlinked smoke alarms to BS EN 14604 with an integral tamper-proof standby supply — in the circulation space on every storey, in the principal habitable room and in any room in which a fire could start that opens onto the escape route, with a heat alarm to BS 5446-2 in the kitchen. This exceeds the Grade D2 Category LD3 minimum given in Approved Document B Volume 1 for extended dwellings and is adopted as this practice's standard so that the whole house is protected, not only the new work. Where the existing dwelling has no alarms, or battery-only alarms, alarms to the same grade are to be provided throughout and interlinked with the new ones.",
    "NOTE — Grade D1 Category LD2 is this practice's standard and exceeds the Grade D2 Category LD3 that Approved Document B gives as the minimum for an extension. It means interlinked alarms with a standby supply through the existing house, not only the new work, and it has a cost. Confirm with the client that the whole house is to be upgraded, or reduce the note to the new work before issue.",
    "Alarms to be ceiling mounted not less than 300mm from any wall or light fitting, within 7.5m of the door to every habitable room and within 3m of every bedroom door, with no point in a hallway more than 7.5m from an alarm; where the kitchen is not separated from the stair or circulation space by a door an interlinked heat alarm in the kitchen is required in every case. A carbon monoxide alarm to BS EN 50291 is to be fitted in every room containing a fixed combustion appliance. Alarms to be supplied from an independent circuit at the consumer unit or from a regularly used local lighting circuit, as BS 5839-6 permits for Grade D1.",
    "A design, installation and commissioning certificate for the fire detection and alarm system to be issued to Building Control on completion of the works."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Means of Escape", p:[
    "Where the works create a habitable room at first floor level or above, provide an openable escape window to that room with an unobstructed openable area of at least 0.33m², a minimum clear height and width of 450mm, and the bottom of the openable area not more than 1100mm above finished floor level (and not less than 800mm unless guarding is provided to Approved Document K), the window openable without a key from inside and, where the room is more than 4.5m above ground, the room instead served by a protected stair or the fire strategy agreed with building control before submission.",
    "Where the works alter the existing escape route, the route to the final exit is to be reviewed and brought into compliance. Where the extension makes an existing habitable room an inner room (a room whose only escape is through another room), the inner room is to have an escape window to the standard above or a door to a protected route, or the arrangement is to be resolved before construction. Where the extension is to a three-storey house the existing protected stair, its fire doors and the final exit are to be maintained and any new opening onto it fitted with an FD30S self-closing door.",
    "Access for a fire service pumping appliance to be provided within 45m of all points within the dwelling. Where this cannot be achieved the arrangement is to be agreed with Building Control prior to submission."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Cavity Barriers and Fire Stopping", p:[
    "Provide cavity barriers at the top of the external cavity wall, at the junction with the existing dwelling, around all openings, and at the edges of cavities, in accordance with Approved Document B Volume 1. Cavity barriers to be flexible mineral wool or a proprietary closer to the manufacturer's specification.",
    "Fire stop all service penetrations through fire resisting construction using a proprietary system tested for the substrate and service concerned."]},
  {c:"Windows & Doors", t:"Windows and External Doors", p:[
    "Windows and external doors to be double glazed units with a low-emissivity coating and argon fill, in powder coated aluminium or uPVC frames as the elevations, achieving a whole-window U-value not exceeding 1.4 W/m²K or Window Energy Rating Band B minimum. External doors to achieve 1.4 W/m²K or Doorset Energy Rating Band C minimum where more than 60% glazed, and Band B otherwise.",
    "The total area of windows, doors and rooflights in the extension is not to exceed 25% of the floor area of the extension, plus the area of any openings closed off by the works, unless compliance is demonstrated by calculation."]},
  {c:"Windows & Doors", t:"Safety Glazing and Guarding", p:[
    "Glazing in critical locations — within 800mm of floor level, and within 1500mm of floor level in doors and in side panels within 300mm of a door — to be safety glazing satisfying the impact performance requirements of Approved Document K, permanently marked.",
    "Where a window cill is less than 800mm above finished floor level and the drop externally exceeds 600mm, provide guarding or an opening restrictor in accordance with Approved Document K.",
    "NOTE — Approved Document Q applies to new dwellings and is not generally applicable to an extension to an existing dwelling. Include PAS 24 requirements only where the work creates a new dwelling or the local authority has asked for them."]},
  {c:"Condensation (C2)", t:"Surface and Interstitial Condensation", p:[
    "Reasonable precautions are to be taken to prevent surface and interstitial condensation in roofs, walls and floors, in accordance with Approved Document C2 and BS 5250.",
    "Provide a continuous vapour control layer on the warm side of the insulation in all roof constructions and in timber frame walls, with all laps a minimum 150mm, taped and sealed, and sealed at all service penetrations, junctions and perimeters. The vapour control layer is not to be punctured by downlighters, cabling or fixings without being made good.",
    "Maintain continuity of insulation at all junctions to limit the risk of surface condensation and mould growth at thermal bridges. The temperature factor at each junction is to be not less than 0.75 for dwellings.",
    "Provide adequate ventilation to all rooms to control moisture generated within the dwelling. Extract ducts passing through unheated voids are to be insulated to prevent condensation forming within the duct."]},
  {c:"Drainage", t:"Foul Drainage", p:[
    "Foul drainage to comply with Approved Document H1. Below ground foul drains to be a minimum 100mm diameter, laid to a self-cleansing gradient of not flatter than 1:80 for 100mm pipes, on a 100mm granular bed with 100mm surround, with rodding access at every change of direction and gradient and at all junctions.",
    "All appliances to be trapped with a minimum 75mm trap seal. Branch pipe diameters, lengths and gradients to comply with Approved Document H1.",
    "Soil and vent pipes to terminate not less than 900mm above any opening into the building within 3m, and to be fitted with a cage or perforated cover. Internal SVPs to be boxed in and wrapped with 25mm unfaced mineral wool insulation.",
    "Locate all existing drain runs and manholes affected by the works on site before excavation. Any private drain serving another property that crosses the site is a shared sewer vested in the sewerage undertaker and is to be treated as a public sewer."]},
  {c:"Drainage", t:"Rainwater Drainage and Soakaways", p:[
    "Rainwater drainage to comply with Approved Document H3. Rainwater to discharge to a soakaway, watercourse or surface water sewer in that order of priority.",
    "Soakaways to be sited not less than 5m from the building and any boundary, and sized on the results of a percolation test carried out in accordance with BRE Digest 365."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Electrical Installation", p:[
    "All electrical work to comply with Approved Document P and BS 7671, designed, installed, inspected and tested by a competent person registered with an approved Part P scheme. Electrical Installation Certificate and Building Regulations Compliance Certificate to be issued to Building Control on completion.",
    "Socket outlets and switches in new habitable rooms to be positioned between 450mm and 1200mm above finished floor level."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Hot Water Safety and Sanitary Provision (Part G)", p:[
    "Hot water storage vessels to Approved Document G3 with a thermostat and a non-self-resetting energy cut-out so that the stored water cannot exceed 100°C, the cylinder capable of being stored at not less than 60°C to control legionella, and the vessel marked with its installer, capacity, pressure and the location of the discharge. Vented cylinders to have an open vent pipe of not less than 19mm bore over the cold water cistern; unvented systems to BS EN 12897 installed by a person registered with a competent person scheme for unvented hot water, with the tundish, D1 and D2 discharge pipework and termination arranged as Approved Document G3 and the notification made to building control.",
    "The hot water supplied to every bath is to be limited to not more than 48°C by a thermostatic mixing valve to BS EN 1111 or BS EN 1287 fitted as close to the bath as practicable, set and locked so that it cannot readily be altered by the building user, and compatible with the water supply pressures and the appliances served (Approved Document G3). Sanitary appliances, WCs, wash hand basins and their connections to comply with Approved Document G4 and G5, with a wash hand basin in or adjacent to every room containing a WC, and all new fittings and pipework to the Water Supply (Water Fittings) Regulations 1999."]},
  {c:"Conservation of Fuel & Power", t:"Thermal Bridging and Air Permeability", p:[
    "Maintain continuity of insulation at all junctions, reveals, cills, heads, and at the wall/floor and wall/roof interfaces. Insulation boards to be firmly held against the inner leaf, lightly butted, with all joints taped with a minimum 50mm overlap. No gaps are permitted at board joints, wall ties or service penetrations.",
    "Where psi-values are relied upon in the SAP calculation, the manufacturer's published values for the specific build-up are to be used and the corresponding details constructed exactly as published. The thermal junction schedule is to be issued to the SAP assessor.",
    "Reasonable provision to be made to limit air leakage. Seal all penetrations of the air barrier, including at service entries, joist ends, and the perimeter of window and door frames."]},
  {c:"Conservation of Fuel & Power", t:"Existing Thermal Elements and Fixed Building Services", p:[
    "Where an existing thermal element is renovated as part of the works, and the renovated area exceeds 50% of the surface area of that element or 25% of the total building envelope, the element is to be upgraded to the standard for a renovated thermal element given in Approved Document L Volume 1.",
    "Where new fixed building services are installed, they are to meet the minimum efficiency standards in Approved Document L and the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide, with appropriate controls, and commissioning certificates issued to Building Control.",
    "NOTE — Approved Documents L1 and F1, 2026 editions, come into force on 24 March 2027. Work with a full plans application submitted before that date remains under the current standards provided work commences before 24 March 2028. Confirm the applicable thresholds and target U-values for retained and renovated thermal elements against the edition in force at the date of submission."]},
  {c:"Conservation of Fuel & Power", t:"Fixed Lighting — Internal and External", p:[
    "All new fixed internal and external lighting to have a luminous efficacy of not less than 75 lumens per circuit-watt (Approved Document L Volume 1, 2021 edition, Section 6 and the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide), with LED luminaires or lamps throughout; fittings rated at less than 5 circuit-watts are excluded from the count. Light fittings in fire-resisting or insulated ceilings to be fire-rated or fitted with fire hoods and sealed to the vapour control layer, and recessed fittings kept clear of insulation as the manufacturer requires.",
    "Fixed external lighting to be controlled so that it is automatically switched off when there is enough daylight and when not required at night, by a photocell with a PIR or time switch, and to meet the same 75 lumens per circuit-watt efficacy figure. Circuits to Approved Document P, with outdoor fittings of an IP rating suitable for their exposure."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Party Wall Matters", p:[
    "Compliance with the Party Wall etc. Act 1996 is the responsibility of the client. Notifiable work includes excavating within 3m of an adjoining owner's structure to a lower depth than its foundations, excavating within 6m where the excavation cuts a line drawn at 45 degrees from the base of the adjoining foundation, building on the line of junction, and cutting into or raising a party wall.",
    "Awards are to be in place before the affected work commences. Building Regulations approval does not remove the obligation."]},
  {c:"Foundations", t:"Pipes Through Walls and Foundations", p:[
    "Where a drain or service passes through a wall or foundation, provide either an opening formed with a lintel over, giving a minimum 50mm clear space around the pipe with the opening masked internally and externally with rigid sheet material to prevent the entry of fill or vermin, or a short length of pipe bedded in the wall with flexible joints within 150mm of each face and a rocker pipe of not more than 600mm each side.",
    "No pipe is to be built solidly into a foundation or wall without the engineer's approval. Where a drain passes beneath a foundation the arrangement is to be to Approved Document H1 and confirmed by the engineer.",
    "Where the opening is masked with granular backfill rather than rigid sheet, pea shingle is to be used around the pipe, a damp proof course provided over the opening, and any lintel carrying the wall above borne on semi-engineering brickwork each side.",
    "Where a pipe passes through a trench fill foundation the load-bearing capability of the foundation must not be affected. The pipe is to be sleeved, with flexible material around it and flexible joints where it leaves the foundation; alternatively it may pass through a shuttered opening with a lintel over, giving sufficient space for movement so that the drain maintains its line and gradient, with pea shingle around the pipe and a damp proof course provided. The structural engineer’s advice is to be obtained on the suitability of running a pipe through a foundation before construction."]},
  {c:"Internal Works", t:"Construction Between House and Integral Garage", opt:1, p:[
    "Where the works form or alter a junction between the dwelling and an integral or attached garage, the garage is to be separated from the dwelling by construction achieving a minimum REI 30 standard, including the wall, any floor above and the ceiling where habitable accommodation is over.",
    "Any door between the garage and the dwelling is to be an FD30S fire doorset (30 minutes integrity, E 30, with intumescent strips and cold smoke seals, Sa) fitted with a self-closing device, with a step of not less than 100mm up from the garage floor into the dwelling, or the garage floor laid to fall away from the door to a drain or the garage door opening so that fuel spillage cannot flow into the dwelling.",
    "Service penetrations through the separating construction to be fire stopped with a proprietary system tested for the substrate and service concerned."]},
  {c:"Site Preparation", t:"Tanking and Below-Ground Waterproofing", opt:1, p:[
    "Where any part of the works is below external ground level, waterproofing is to be designed in accordance with BS 8102, with the grade of protection selected for the intended use of the space and the type of waterproofing protection stated on the drawings.",
    "Type A barrier protection, Type B structurally integral protection and Type C drained protection may be used singly or in combination. The design is to be carried out by a suitably qualified waterproofing specialist, and a maintainable drainage and pumping arrangement provided where Type C is adopted.",
    "The groundwater level and any perched water table are to be established before the design is finalised."]},
  {c:"Drainage", t:"Drains Under or Near the Building", p:[
    "Drains laid under the building are to be surrounded in concrete integral with the floor slab where the cover is less than the minimum given in Approved Document H1, and access is to be provided so that every length can be rodded.",
    "Drains laid close to a foundation are to be arranged so that the foundation does not impose load on the drain, in accordance with Approved Document H1. Where a drain runs parallel to and below the level of a foundation, the trench is to be filled with concrete to the level of the underside of the foundation.",
    "Where a pumped drainage arrangement is required, the pumping station, storage capacity, alarm and standby arrangements are to be to Approved Document H1 and the manufacturer's design, with the discharge to a gravity system agreed with the sewerage undertaker."]},
  {c:"Renewable Energy", t:"Water Reuse — Greywater and Rainwater Harvesting", opt:1, p:[
    "Where a greywater reuse or rainwater harvesting system is installed, it is to be designed and installed so that there is no possibility of cross connection or backflow to the wholesome water supply, with appropriate backflow prevention and physical separation, in accordance with the Water Supply (Water Fittings) Regulations 1999 and Approved Document G.",
    "All non-wholesome water pipework, outlets and storage are to be clearly and durably marked to distinguish them from the wholesome supply. Storage is to be accessible for cleaning and maintenance, with an overflow discharging to the surface water system.",
    "Where the system is relied upon in the water efficiency calculation, the assumed yield is to be substantiated and the calculation submitted to Building Control."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Cantilevered Floor with Exposed Soffit", opt:1, p:[
    "Where a floor cantilevers beyond the wall below or otherwise has a soffit exposed to outside air, the cantilever is to be designed by the structural engineer, and the exposed floor treated as a thermal element with insulation continuous with the wall insulation at the junction.",
    "The soffit is to be fully insulated and finished with a weather resistant board or render system on a ventilated and drained batten zone, with a continuous vapour control layer on the warm side and cavity barriers at the perimeter in accordance with Approved Document B.",
    "The junction between the exposed soffit and the wall below is a significant thermal bridge; the detail is to maintain continuity of insulation and the temperature factor is to be checked where psi-values are relied upon."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Regulation 16 Notices — Commencement and Completion", p:[
    "Regulation 16 of the Building Regulations 2010 (as amended in October 2023) requires three notices to the building control body: notice of intention to start work, given so that at least two clear days elapse before work begins; a commencement notice within five days of the work being regarded as commenced under Regulation 46A, which for a new building or a horizontal extension is when the sub-structure including the ground floor structure is complete and for other work (loft, garage, basement and flat conversions) is when 15 per cent of the work is complete, after which the authority has four weeks to reject the notice; and a completion notice within five days of completion. The completion notice must give the name, address, telephone number and email of the client, the principal contractor and the principal designer, state that the work is complete, include a signed statement by the client that the work complies with the Building Regulations, and include signed statements from the principal designer and principal contractor confirming that they have fulfilled their duties under Part 2A with the dates of their appointments; where a statement cannot be obtained the reason is to be given.",
    "Inspection stages to be notified as they arise: excavations before concreting, foundations before covering, damp proof courses and membranes, oversite, structural steel and fire-resisting construction before it is closed up, insulation before it is boarded, drains before backfilling and on test, and completion. Certificates from competent person schemes (electrical, gas, replacement windows, heating appliances) and commissioning notices for fixed building services, ventilation and pressure tests are to be given to building control within the periods the Regulations set (normally five days) and copied to the client with the completion certificate, which purchasers' solicitors will ask for.",
    "NOTE — The two-day and five-day limbs are separate duties. Missing the commencement notice means the work is not treated as commenced for transitional purposes, which matters where an edition of the Approved Documents is about to change."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Houses in Multiple Occupation", opt:1, p:[
    "Where the dwelling is, or on completion will be, occupied by three or more people forming more than one household sharing a kitchen, bathroom or WC, it is a house in multiple occupation: the local housing authority's HMO amenity and room-size standards and the LACORS housing fire safety guidance apply alongside the Building Regulations, licensing is mandatory for five or more occupants, and the fire strategy changes — typically a Grade D1 Category LD2 alarm system (Grade A with a control panel for licensable three-storey houses), FD30S self-closing fire doors to all letting rooms and the kitchen onto a 30 minute protected route, emergency lighting to BS 5266-1 where the route is long or unlit, a fire blanket in the kitchen, and 30 minute separation between letting rooms. Confirm the intended occupation with the client in writing before the fire strategy is fixed; a dwelling designed for a single family and let as an HMO fails its licence inspection on the doors and alarm grade."]},
  {c:"Planning & Permitted Development", t:"Permitted Development Limits (Class A)", p:[
    "Where the extension is to be carried out under Class A of Part 1 of Schedule 2 to the General Permitted Development Order the limits are: single-storey rear extensions not projecting more than 3m from the rear wall of the original house for a terraced or semi-detached house and 4m for a detached house (6m and 8m under the larger home extension route, which needs prior approval through the neighbour consultation scheme) and not more than 4m high; two-storey rear extensions not more than 3m deep, not within 7m of the rear boundary, with the roof pitch matching the house and any side-facing upper windows obscure glazed and non-opening below 1.7m above floor; side extensions single storey, not more than 4m high and not wider than half the width of the original house; eaves within 2m of a boundary not more than 3m high; the total footprint of extensions and outbuildings not more than half the curtilage; materials of similar appearance to the house; no verandas, balconies or raised platforms; and no extension forward of a principal or side elevation fronting a highway. On designated land (conservation areas, national parks, AONBs, World Heritage Sites) side extensions, cladding and the larger home extension route are excluded. Where any limit is exceeded, or a condition removes the right, planning permission is required, and a lawful development certificate is advisable in every case."]},
  {c:"Site Preparation", t:"Radon and Ground Gas Protection — Detail", opt:1, p:[
    "Where the site is in a radon affected area on the UK radon map, protection to BR 211 is to be built in. Basic protection is a radon-resistant membrane, of a grade certified for radon resistance (typically 1600 gauge / 400 micron polyethylene or a reinforced or aluminium-cored membrane), laid across the whole footprint in place of the standard damp proof membrane with laps of not less than 300mm sealed with gas-resistant tape, every penetration sealed with a top hat, and the membrane carried across the cavity to link with a cavity tray or the damp proof course in both leaves so the barrier is continuous; the slab is to be laid over sound compacted fill so the membrane cannot tear. Full protection, where the map shows the higher probability band, adds a radon sump beneath the slab connected to a capped pipe taken to the outside so a fan can be fitted later, or, for a suspended floor, a cross-ventilated void of not less than 150mm with the vents on opposite sides; a radon test after occupation and the fan fitted if the result is high. Where the site investigation identifies landfill gas, hydrocarbons or volatile organic compounds, gas protection is to be designed to BS 8485 and CIRIA C735 with a specialist membrane, its installation independently inspected and a verification report given to building control."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Lintels, Beams and Fire Protection", p:[
    "Lintels over openings in masonry to be proprietary steel lintels to BS EN 845-2 with a thermal break where they cross a cavity, or precast prestressed concrete lintels, selected from the manufacturer's tables for the span and the load with not less than 150mm end bearing on whole blocks or bricks (never on cut pieces) and padstones where the manufacturer requires them, the masonry not overhanging the lintel toe by more than 25mm, the toe projecting beyond the frame head, insulation between the lintel and the frame and a separate damp proof course tray with stop ends and weep holes over any external lintel that has no built-in one. Lintels and beams that carry a floor or the fire-resisting enclosure of a stair, and not only the roof, are to be protected to give 30 minutes fire resistance (60 where the element requires it) by encasement in fire-resisting board with staggered joints or an intumescent coating applied to the certified thickness for the section factor, with the certificate given to building control. Steel beams to BS EN 10025 on padstones to the engineer's design, primed, and with timber joists on hangers or a bolted timber plate rather than resting on the flange; internal door openings in non-loadbearing masonry may use a 65mm concrete plank lintel up to 750mm and a 150mm lintel at 900mm with 100mm bearings."]},
  {c:"Foundations", t:"Foundations — Fallback Dimensions and Rules", p:[
    "Where the structural engineer is not designing the foundations and the ground is a firm clay, sand or gravel without trees, fill or soft spots, Approved Document A Section 2E gives the fallback: strip foundations of GEN1 concrete not less than 225mm thick with the width from Table 10 for the wall load and soil type (not less than the wall thickness plus 300mm in most domestic cases, and never less than the projection each side equalling the thickness), taken to not less than 1000mm below finished ground in clay and 750mm elsewhere, with internal loadbearing walls on foundations not less than 600mm deep; trench-fill foundations not less than 450mm wide; steps in the foundation not exceeding its thickness with the higher foundation overlapping the lower by not less than twice the step, the thickness or 300mm whichever is greater (1m for trench fill). Foundations to be taken below the invert of any adjacent drain and not to be undermined by it, with the drain sleeved or bridged with 50mm clearance and flexible joints each side where it passes through; sulfate-resisting cement or the BS 8500 designated class where the ground or groundwater requires it. Where trees are within influencing distance, foundation depths follow NHBC Chapter 4.2 and heave precautions apply; where the ground is filled, soft or variable, a raft or piled foundation to the engineer's design is used instead, with raft reinforcement lapped 450mm and 40mm cover."]},
  {c:"Ground Floors", t:"Existing Sub-Floor Ventilation Under the Extension", p:[
    "Where the extension is built against a wall containing air bricks serving a suspended timber floor in the existing house, the ventilation to that floor is to be maintained: provide 100mm ducts beneath the new floor slab from the position of each existing air brick to new air bricks in the external wall of the extension, sleeved through the cavity with a cavity tray over, laid to fall away from the house and sealed through any radon or gas membrane, or provide equivalent cross ventilation to the existing void by another route agreed with building control. Blocking the existing air bricks without providing an alternative will cause the existing floor to rot. Where the new floor is itself suspended, the void is to be ventilated at not less than 1500mm² per metre run of external wall or 500mm² per square metre of floor area, whichever is greater, on opposite sides, with the oversite not lower than the external ground and the wall plate not less than 75mm above it."]},
  {c:"Additional Notes for Walls", t:"Movement Joints, Wall Ties, Cavity Trays and Damp Proof Courses", p:[
    "Vertical movement joints in the outer leaf at not more than 12m centres in clay brickwork, 7.5m to 9m in calcium silicate brickwork and dense concrete blockwork and 6m in aircrete blockwork, at not more than 1.5m from corners in parapets and at half the spacing in parapets and free-standing walls, and wherever the length of a panel exceeds three times its height or the wall changes thickness or height; joint width not less than 1.3mm per metre between joints in clay brickwork (16mm at 12m) and 10mm in other masonry, formed with a compressible filler and a low-modulus sealant and tied across with debonded ties. Wall ties to BS EN 845-1 of the type and length for the cavity width (PD 6697: 200mm long for a 50–75mm cavity, 225mm for 76–100mm, 250mm for 101–125mm, 275mm for 126–150mm), at 900mm horizontal and 450mm vertical centres staggered (2.5 per m²), at not more than 300mm vertical centres within 225mm of jambs, unbonded edges and movement joints, set level or with a slight fall to the outer leaf and kept free of mortar droppings; ties for cavities over 150mm to the manufacturer's data.",
    "Damp proof courses to BS 6398 in both leaves at not less than 150mm above finished ground, continuous with the floor membrane and with the existing damp proof course where new work meets old, vertical damp proof courses at every reveal and abutment where the cavity is closed, and cavity trays with 150mm upstands, stop ends and not fewer than two weep holes per tray at not more than 900mm centres over every opening, at abutments and at the base of the cavity where it does not extend 225mm below the damp proof course. Vapour control layers to insulated framed walls of not less than 500 gauge polyethylene or a taped foil facing, lapped 100mm onto the floor membrane and sealed."]},
  {c:"Roofs", t:"Leadwork, Flashings, Valleys, Hips and Ridges", p:[
    "Leadwork to BS EN 12588 and the Lead Sheet Training Academy's Rolled Lead Sheet Manual: Code 4 for soakers, cover flashings and small aprons, Code 5 for valley gutters, back gutters and the flat areas of dormer roofs, and Code 6 for wide gutters, with piece lengths limited to 1.5m and the laps, upstands and expansion allowances in the manual; stepped and cover flashings tucked 25mm into the masonry joint and wedged, a slip layer between mortar and lead, lead flashings beneath window sills where the sill has no drip, and patination oil applied to new lead on completion. Valleys as lead-lined or GRP valleys on 19mm x 225mm valley boards with an exposed channel of not less than 100mm (125mm below 30 degrees pitch) and a 100mm lap between lengths, or a proprietary dry valley where the pitch and tile allow; tiles bedded on fillets clear of the lead. Where lead is in contact with oak or a copper-containing alloy it is to be isolated with a building paper. Lead substitutes may be used where the planning authority accepts them and the product carries a BBA certificate for the application.",
    "Hips and ridges to be mechanically fixed with a proprietary dry-fix system, or bedded and clipped, in accordance with BS 5534; mortar bedding alone without mechanical fixing is not acceptable. Secret gutters are to be avoided where an open valley can be used and, where unavoidable, are to be fitted with removable access for cleaning."]},
  {c:"Internal Works", t:"Intermediate Floors and Internal Sound Insulation", p:[
    "New intermediate floors within the extension of C24 joists to the engineer's design with 22mm tongued and grooved P5 moisture-resistant chipboard to BS EN 312 (marked face up in kitchens and bathrooms) glued and screwed, strutting at mid-span for spans over 2.5m, joists on hangers to BS EN 845-1 at masonry bearings, and a 12.5mm plasterboard ceiling of not less than 10 kg/m² with 100mm mineral wool of not less than 10 kg/m³ between the joists to meet Requirement E2 (40 dB Rw) where the floor separates a bedroom or WC from other rooms; in a three-storey house the floor is to give 30 minutes fire resistance (REI 30) with a 15mm fire-resisting board or two layers of 12.5mm plasterboard and the joists at 400mm centres. Internal partitions between a bedroom or a room containing a WC and other rooms to achieve 40 dB Rw: 89mm x 38mm timber studs or 70mm metal C studs with 25mm minimum mineral wool of 10 kg/m³ in the cavity and a single 12.5mm plasterboard of 10 kg/m² each side with all joints and perimeters sealed satisfies Approved Document E Section 5 without testing; metal stud to BS EN 14195 at 400mm centres where tiled, with moisture-resistant board in wet rooms."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"External Fire Spread — Boundaries, Unprotected Areas and Roof Coverings", p:[
    "External walls within 1m of the relevant boundary are to have 30 minutes fire resistance from both sides (60 minutes where they separate buildings), an external surface of Class B-s3, d2 or better, and unprotected areas (windows, doors and combustible cladding) limited to small areas not exceeding 1m² and not less than 4m apart. Walls more than 1m from the boundary may have unprotected areas up to the limits in Approved Document B Volume 1 Section 11 for a residential building: 5.6m² at 1m, 12m² at 2m, 18m² at 3m, 24m² at 4m, 30m² at 5m and unlimited at 6m or more, measured for each side of the building facing that boundary; where cladding is worse than Class B-s3, d2 and more than 1mm thick, half its area counts as unprotected. The relevant boundary is the site boundary or the centre line of an adjoining road, canal or river.",
    "Roof coverings within 6m of the boundary are to be classified BROOF(t4) to BS EN 13501-5, which tiles, slates, felt on a substrate, single-ply membranes, GRP systems and glass with the appropriate classification achieve; thermoplastic rooflights are limited in area and distance as Section 12 and are not to be within 6m of the boundary unless classified. The classification of the roof covering, the rooflights and any cladding is to be stated on the drawings, and the position of the relevant boundary shown on the site plan so building control can check the unprotected area calculation."]},
  {c:"Windows & Doors", t:"Rooflights", p:[
    "Rooflights to have a whole-unit U-value not exceeding 2.2 W/m²K (Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.2) or a Rooflight Energy Rating of Band C, set on an insulated proprietary kerb of not less than 150mm to a flat roof or fitted with the manufacturer's flashing kit to a pitched roof, with the trimming rafters or joists doubled and the insulation and vapour control layer carried up the kerb; where the rooflight is the only opening in a room it is to give the purge ventilation area and, where relied on for escape from a room in the roof, the egress dimensions. Rooflights within 6m of the boundary are to be of a material or classification acceptable under Approved Document B Volume 1 Section 12 for their distance from the boundary. Glazing in rooflights overhead to be laminated or toughened-laminated safety glass to BS EN 12600 or BS EN 14179."]},
  {c:"Ventilation", t:"Ventilation — Approved Document F", p:[
    "Ventilation to comply with Approved Document F Volume 1. Extending the dwelling must not make the existing ventilation provision worse than it was before the work: where an existing habitable room loses its window, or its window now opens into the extension or a conservatory, that room is to be given alternative background, purge and (where it is a wet room) extract ventilation by another route, or the two rooms treated as one space ventilated together in accordance with Approved Document F Section 3. Check every room whose external wall is being built against before issue — this is the clause most often missed on rear and wraparound extensions.",
    "Intermittent mechanical extract ventilation to BS EN 13141-4 at not less than 30 litres per second from a cooker hood over the hob (fitted 650mm to 750mm above it, to BS EN 13141-3) or 60 litres per second elsewhere in the kitchen, 30 litres per second from a utility room, 15 litres per second from a bathroom and 6 litres per second from a WC. Every extract fan is to discharge directly to the outside air, not into a roof void or a cavity. Fans may be wired to the light switch and are to have a 15 minute overrun where the room has no openable window. Ductwork rigid wherever possible with flexible duct limited to 1.5m at final connections and run straight, installed to BSRIA BG 43, insulated where it passes through unheated voids and fire stopped or fitted with fire dampers where it passes through fire-resisting construction. All ducts to discharge directly to outside air through a terminal with a back-draught shutter.",
    "Background ventilators for natural ventilation (System 1) to give an equivalent area of not less than 8000mm² in every habitable room and kitchen of a dwelling with more than one storey, 10,000mm² in a single-storey dwelling or flat, and 4000mm² in every bathroom, with not fewer than four ventilators in a one-bedroom dwelling and five otherwise, set not less than 1700mm above floor level (trickle ventilators in the window heads ordered as part of the window units), with the equivalent area marked or recorded and noise-attenuating ventilators used on facades exposed to traffic or other noise. Purge ventilation to every habitable room by an openable area of not less than one twentieth of the floor area where the window opens more than 30 degrees, or one tenth where it opens between 15 and 30 degrees, or a mechanical system giving four air changes per hour; the openable areas are to be checked against the window schedule before issue. Internal doors undercut 10mm above the floor finish (7600mm² for a 760mm door).",
    "Where the design air permeability is below 5 m³/(h·m²), or the dwelling is single-aspect, or a room has no external wall, continuous mechanical extract (System 3) is to be used with 4000mm² background ventilators to each habitable room and not fewer than the number of bedrooms plus two, or mechanical ventilation with heat recovery (System 4) with no background ventilators, supply terminals placed to avoid draughts and wet-room air not recirculated; continuous rates not less than 13 litres per second from the kitchen and 8 from each bathroom and utility room and 6 from a WC, and the whole-dwelling rate the greater of 0.3 litres per second per square metre and the bedroom-count rate. Every fixed mechanical ventilation system to be commissioned with the flow rates measured to BS EN 13141-6 or 13141-7 and the results given to building control within five days of the final test and to the occupier in the Home User Guide."]},
  {c:"Drainage", t:"Drainage — Depths, Chambers, Branch Pipes, Building Over Sewers and Pumped Drainage", p:[
    "Below-ground drains to be laid with not less than 600mm cover under gardens and fields and 900mm under drives and roads (1200mm for rigid pipes under roads), or protected by a reinforced concrete slab on a compressible layer with 75mm of granular material between the pipe and the slab where the cover is less; bedded on and surrounded by 100mm of pea gravel, with flexible joints and rocker pipes not more than 600mm long each side of a wall or foundation and 50mm clearance in the opening, or the foundation carried down below the drain and the drain sleeved. Inspection chambers at every change of direction or gradient, at junctions and at not more than 45m centres, 450mm diameter to 1.2m deep, with double-sealed bolt-down covers inside the building and vehicle-rated covers in drives; rodding eyes at the head of each run. Drains passing under a floor slab to have a 1m wide strip of A142 mesh in the slab over the run and a 150mm concrete surround where the crown is within 300mm of the underside of the slab.",
    "Above-ground pipework to BS EN 12056-2 with 75mm seal traps and branch lengths not exceeding 1.7m for a 32mm basin waste (3m at 40mm), 3m for a 40mm bath or shower waste (4m at 50mm) and 6m for a 100mm WC branch, beyond which anti-vacuum traps or a ventilated branch are needed; no branch is to connect within 200mm below a WC branch connection, the stack is to have a long-radius bend at its foot, and an air admittance valve to BS EN 12380 may replace an open vent only where at least one stack on the drain remains open to the air and the valve is above the highest spill-over level in an accessible ventilated position. Building over or within 3m of a public sewer, over a manhole, or where the sewer is more than 3m deep or larger than 225mm requires a build-over agreement with the sewerage undertaker with a CCTV survey before and after. A pumped WC or macerator is permitted only where a gravity WC is also available in the dwelling, with pumping stations to BS EN 12056-4 and BS EN 12050 with a non-return valve, alarm and 24 hours' storage. All new drains to be tested by air or water in the presence of the Building Control Officer before backfilling and again at completion, with any manhole or inspection chamber that falls within the footprint relocated or fitted with a double-sealed, screw-down cover as agreed with building control."]},
  {c:"Drainage", t:"Off-Mains Foul Drainage — Treatment Plants, Septic Tanks and Drainage Fields", opt:1, p:[
    "Where no public sewer is within reasonable distance, foul drainage is to discharge to a packaged treatment plant to BS EN 12566-3 sized to British Water's Flows and Loads for the number of bedrooms plus two persons, capable of operating for six hours without power, sited not less than 7m from the dwelling and within 30m of a hardstanding for the desludging tanker, with the effluent discharged to a drainage field designed to BS 6297 on three percolation tests, laid in trenches 300mm to 900mm wide at not more than 700mm deep on a 300mm bed of clean shingle with the pipes at a gradient not steeper than 1:200 and not less than 2m between trenches, sited at least 15m from any building, 10m from a watercourse or permeable drain and 50m from a well, spring or borehole, with the water table not less than 1m below the invert and no paving or traffic over it. Discharge to a watercourse is only permitted under the Environment Agency's general binding rules or an environmental permit, and new septic tanks discharging to a watercourse are not permitted.",
    "Where a septic tank is still acceptable with a drainage field it is to have a capacity of not less than 2700 litres plus 180 litres for each user over four, sited 7m from the dwelling with access for emptying, and a cesspool is a last resort requiring the local authority's agreement with a capacity of 18,000 litres for two users plus 6,800 litres per additional user. The installation is to be registered with the Environment Agency where required and the maintenance schedule handed to the occupier."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Heating Systems — Boilers, Heat Pumps, Underfloor Heating and Hot Water", p:[
    "Heat producing appliances, flues and hearths to comply with Approved Document J; all gas work by a Gas Safe registered engineer, oil by an OFTEC registered technician and solid fuel by a HETAS registered installer. New and replacement wet heating systems are to be designed for a maximum flow temperature of 55°C with emitters sized accordingly. Gas boilers to have an ErP seasonal efficiency of not less than 92 per cent, a room-sealed flue with the terminal positioned to Approved Document J and the manufacturer's clearances and not less than 50mm from combustible material, inspection hatches of 300mm x 300mm at every joint and change of direction where a flue runs in a void, a carbon monoxide alarm to BS EN 50291 between 1m and 3m from the appliance, and, for a combination boiler, one of the additional efficiency measures in the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide (flue gas heat recovery, weather or load compensation, or a smart thermostat with automation and optimisation). Controls to Approved Document L Volume 1 Section 5: time and temperature control of every heating zone, two zones where the dwelling exceeds 150m², thermostatic radiator valves, a cylinder thermostat and time control for hot water independent of the heating, and a boiler interlock; primary and accessible pipework, and all pipework outside the heated envelope, insulated to Table 4.4 of Approved Document L Volume 1 and the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide.",
    "Heat pumps to MCS MIS 3005 with the heat loss calculation, emitter and cylinder sizing and the outdoor unit sited to the MCS 020 noise limit at the nearest neighbour's window; any back-up heater interlocked so it never leads. Oil-fired appliances installed by an OFTEC registered technician with the oil tank not less than 1.8m from the building, 760mm from the boundary, on a fire-resisting base extending 300mm beyond the tank, bunded to 110 per cent where there is a pollution risk, and fitted with a fire valve. Electric storage heaters with automatic charge control and panel heaters with time and temperature control in each room; electric underfloor heating on a dedicated circuit with 30mA RCD protection and a floor sensor thermostat to each zone, with continuity and insulation tests recorded before the floor is covered.",
    "Wet underfloor heating designed to BS EN 1264 with the floor surface temperature limited to 29°C (27 with resilient finishes), insulation beneath the pipes of not less than ten times the resistance of the floor finish (R 0.75 m²K/W minimum on intermediate floors), 25mm edge insulation, a 65mm minimum screed over the pipes with movement joints at doorways, zone boundaries and bays over 40m² or 8m long, the system pressure tested before the screed is placed and commissioned to BS EN 1264-4 with the manifold zones labelled. Unvented hot water storage to BS EN 12897 installed by a competent person with the temperature and pressure relief valve, expansion vessel and non-self-resetting thermal cut-out, a tundish within 500mm of the valve, the discharge pipe one size larger than the valve outlet routed as D1/D2 to a safe visible termination, and a warning label. The commissioning certificate and the Benchmark record for every appliance to be given to building control and the occupier."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Chimneys, Flues, Hearths and Stoves (Part J)", opt:1, p:[
    "Any new chimney, flue, hearth or solid fuel appliance is to comply with Approved Document J. A masonry chimney to have a lined flue to BS EN 1457 or a flexible or rigid metal liner to BS EN 1856 of the diameter the appliance requires (not less than 150mm for a closed appliance up to 30kW, 200mm for an open fire), backfilled with insulating concrete, with not more than two offsets at not more than 45 degrees, combustible material kept 200mm from the inner face of the flue and 40mm from the outer face of the chimney, and the chimney height not more than 4.5 times its least width unless designed. Flue outlets to terminate not less than 600mm above the ridge where within 600mm of it, 2.3m horizontally from and 1m above any adjacent roof surface or opening, and clear of the boundary as Diagram 3.4. Single-wall flue pipes for stoves to be kept three times their diameter from combustibles, or 1.5 times with a shield, and to have 300mm x 300mm access panels where they pass through voids.",
    "Hearths of 125mm concrete extending not less than 500mm in front of and 150mm each side of a fireplace opening, or a superimposed hearth of not less than 12mm for a stove whose base does not exceed 100°C, with the hearth edge clearly apparent; fireplace recess walls 200mm thick at the sides where separating dwellings and 100mm at the back. A permanently open air supply of 550mm² for each kilowatt over 5kW for a stove, more where an extract fan is in the same room (a fan is not to be fitted in the room of an open-flued appliance without a spillage test), a register plate where a liner enters an existing chimney, a carbon monoxide alarm to BS EN 50291 in the room, and a notice plate fixed beside the meter or consumer unit recording the flue and hearth and the appliance they suit. Installation by a HETAS or equivalent registered installer who notifies the work, or by building notice with a smoke and spillage test on completion, and the Approved Document J Appendix A checklist completed."]},
  {c:"Renewable Energy", t:"Renewable and Low Carbon Systems — PV, Solar Thermal, Heat Pumps, Wind and Heat Recovery", opt:1, p:[
    "Where photovoltaic panels are added, the array is to be installed by an MCS-certificated installer with the roof structure checked by the structural engineer for the additional dead load and wind uplift before installation, the panels fixed with the manufacturer's roof hooks or in-roof system with the underlay and flashing maintained, the roof covering and any plastic rooflights within 6m of the boundary still classified for external fire spread with the array in place, and all cabling routed and protected so as not to compromise the roof covering, the vapour control layer or the fire resistance of the construction. Provide a clearly labelled DC isolator and generation meter in an accessible position, an AC isolator adjacent to the consumer unit and a fire service isolation switch, the inverter in a ventilated position with a smoke alarm in any loft that houses it, and the distribution network operator notified under G98 (or G99 above 16A per phase). Solar thermal collectors to BS EN 12975 with a twin-coil cylinder, expansion vessel, high-limit thermostat and high-temperature pipe insulation.",
    "Air source heat pumps to MCS MIS 3005, sited to the MCS 020 noise limit at the nearest neighbour's window and, where installed under permitted development, not within 1m of the boundary, with the space heating designed for a maximum flow temperature of 55°C and emitters sized accordingly. Ground source heat pumps to have the ground loop or borehole array designed by a specialist to the site's ground conditions and thermal properties, laid at not less than 1.2m depth clear of foundations, drainage and services, pressure tested before backfilling, with borehole arrangements notified to the Environment Agency where required. Where a wind turbine is installed, planning permission is to be confirmed, the structural adequacy of any supporting structure verified by the structural engineer, and the electrical connection made to the distribution network operator's requirements. Waste water heat recovery units on shower drains to the SAP Appendix Q listing, mounted vertically on the floor below with the drain run under 3m and access maintained for cleaning.",
    "Every system to be entered in the energy assessment as installed, with the commissioning certificates and the MCS certificate given to building control and to the client and occupier on completion.",
    "Waste water heat recovery: where the SAP assessment relies on it, or the client wants it, a waste water heat recovery system is to be fitted to the shower waste — a vertical or horizontal heat exchanger that passes the warm drain water past the incoming cold feed to the shower mixer, the combination boiler or the cylinder — as a product listed in the SAP Product Characteristics Database so that its saving can be claimed, installed with the connections, falls and access for cleaning the manufacturer requires, shown on the drainage drawings, and recorded in the as-built SAP and in the information handed to the occupier."]},
 ]},

loft: { name:"Loft Conversion", region:"England",
 cats:["Planning & Permitted Development","General Notes","Structure","Hip to Gable","Dormer Construction (Walls)","Dormer Construction (Roof)","Dwarf / Ashlar Walls","Upgrading Existing External Wall","Upgrading Existing Party Wall","Upgrading Existing Roof","Roofs","Loft Floor","Internal Works","Sound Insulation","Means of Escape & Fire Regs","Windows & Rooflights","Ventilation","Condensation (C2)","Drainage","Services","Conservation of Fuel & Power"],
 buildups:[
  {g:"RF", c:"Roofs", t:"Pitched Roof — Insulation Between and Under Rafters (Room in Roof)", u:"0.14 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.15 W/m²K", mf:{"k":"rafter","lim":0.15,"p":{"insulation":"k107","thickness":125,"rafterDepth":150,"spacing":400,"under":"k118_725","gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"rafter","f":"125mm Kingspan Kooltherm K107 pitched roof board"},{"r":"lining","f":"72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "Existing roof covering to be retained or renewed as the drawings, on battens and counter-battens over a breathable roofing underlay to BS 5534. Rafters to be strengthened or replaced to the structural engineer's design.",
    "Rafters to be a minimum 47mm x 150mm deep, deepened where the existing are shallower. Provide 125mm Kingspan Kooltherm K107 pitched roof board between rafters, held tightly between and lightly butted, with a 50mm ventilated gap above the board where a non-breathable underlay is present (BS 5250) and the rafters deepened where necessary to provide it. Provide 72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard beneath the rafters, joints staggered and taped, to give a continuous internal lining. This build-up calculates at 0.14 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 at 400mm rafter centres with K107 at a thermal conductivity of 0.019 W/mK, allowing for rafter bridging and an air-gap correction of 0.01; 62.5mm under-rafter board in place of 72.5mm calculates at 0.15 and meets the target with no margin.",
    "The repeating thermal bridge of the rafters roughly halves the effective resistance of the between-rafter insulation, so thinner arrangements fail the target by a wide margin: 100mm between with 37.5mm under calculates at 0.20 W/m²K. Where the rafter depth is insufficient, rafters are to be deepened by the structural engineer rather than the insulation reduced, and the U-value recalculated for the actual rafter depth and centres.",
    "Provide a continuous vapour control layer on the warm side of the insulation, sealed at all laps, junctions and service penetrations. Downlighters are not to be installed through the vapour control layer unless a proprietary sealed hood is used."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"Dormer Construction (Roof)", t:"Dormer Flat Roof — Warm Deck", u:"0.15 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.15 W/m²K", mf:{"k":"flat","lim":0.15,"p":{"insulation":"tr27","thickness":150,"joistDepth":200,"spacing":400,"fixing":"adhered"},"s":[{"r":"flat","f":"150mm Kingspan Thermaroof TR27 insulation (thermal conductivity 0.024 W/mK at 120mm and over)"}]}, p:[
    "Dormer roof to comprise timber joists to the structural engineer's design and calculations, shown here as 47mm x 200mm C24 joists at 400mm centres, indicative only and to be confirmed before construction, 18mm external quality plywood or OSB3 decking laid to falls, vapour control layer fully bonded to the deck with all laps sealed, 150mm Kingspan Thermaroof TR27 insulation (thermal conductivity 0.024 W/mK at 120mm and over) laid break-bonded in two layers and fully adhered, and a single ply or high performance built-up felt waterproofing system to the manufacturer's specification and BBA certificate. This build-up calculates at 0.15 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 fully adhered; where the insulation is mechanically fixed the fastener correction takes it to 0.16 and 160mm is to be used instead.",
    "Falls to be designed to a fall of not less than 1:40 so that a finished fall of not less than 1:80 is achieved after construction deviations and deflection, in accordance with BS 6229. Cold deck construction is not to be used. Provide proprietary edge trims and upstands of not less than 150mm at all abutments, with cavity trays and stepped flashings where the dormer abuts masonry."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"Roofs", t:"Pitched Roof — Insulation at Ceiling Level (Residual Void)", u:"0.11 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.15 W/m²K",
   p:[
    "To flat ceiling areas and to any residual roof void outside the insulated envelope, provide 100mm mineral wool quilt between ceiling joists and 300mm mineral wool quilt laid cross-wise over the joists. This build-up calculates at 0.11 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 allowing for the joist fraction at 400mm centres; 300mm total calculates at 0.15.",
    "Ventilate the residual void with ventilation equivalent to a continuous 10mm gap at the eaves on two opposite sides, plus 5mm at the ridge where the pitch exceeds 35°. Where the conversion isolates part of the void, cross ventilation is to be maintained by proprietary vents or the void is to be brought inside the insulated envelope. Provide insulation stops at the eaves so the ventilation path is not blocked."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"Dormer Construction (Roof)", t:"Dormer Pitched Roof — Insulation at Rafter Level", u:"0.14 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.15 W/m²K", mf:{"k":"rafter","lim":0.15,"p":{"insulation":"k107","thickness":150,"rafterDepth":150,"spacing":400,"under":"k118_625","gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"rafter","f":"150mm Kingspan Kooltherm K107 Pitched Roof Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"},{"r":"lining","f":"62.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "Pitched or gabled dormer roof of 47mm x 150mm C24 rafters at 400mm centres to the structural engineer's design, tiles or slates to match the main roof on treated battens and counter-battens over a breathable roofing underlay to BS 5534, with the ridge, hips and valleys formed to match the main roof and the covering fixed to the manufacturer's schedule for the exposure.",
    "Insulation of 150mm Kingspan Kooltherm K107 Pitched Roof Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) fully filling the rafter depth beneath the breathable underlay and 62.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard beneath the rafters, joints staggered and taped, with a continuous vapour control layer on the warm side. This build-up calculates at 0.14 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 at 400mm rafter centres allowing for rafter bridging and an air-gap correction of 0.01.",
    "Dormer roof to be flashed to the main roof with lead or a code-approved substitute, with soakers or a secret gutter at the cheek junctions and a back gutter or valley to the main slope as the Leadwork note. Ventilate the small roof void formed above the insulation at the dormer ridge where a non-breathable underlay is used."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"Dormer Construction (Walls)", t:"Dormer Cheek", u:"0.16 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K (actual U-value achieved 0.16 W/m²K)", mf:{"k":"frame","lim":0.18,"p":{"insulation":"k112","thickness":140,"studWidth":38,"studDepth":140,"spacing":400,"sheathing":"ply18","lining":"k118_375","void":0,"gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"frame","f":"140mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"},{"r":"lining","f":"37.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "Dormer cheek to comprise external cladding — tile hanging, slate, or proprietary panel as the elevations — on treated battens over a breather membrane, with a 25mm minimum ventilated and drained cavity behind, on 18mm external quality plywood sheathing, 140mm x 38mm C16 studs at 400mm centres with the full stud depth filled with 140mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK), a continuous vapour control layer on the warm side, and 37.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard with joints taped. This build-up calculates at 0.16 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 by the combined method, allowing for the studs bridging the insulation at 9.5% of the area at 400mm centres and an air-gap correction of 0.01. Where internal width matters more than the thickness of the cheek, 184mm studs fully filled with 12.5mm plasterboard achieve the same figure.",
    "Where the dormer cheek abuts or is close to a boundary, the fire resistance and the extent of unprotected area are to be checked against Approved Document B before construction. Cavity barriers to be provided at the perimeter of the cheek and at any junction with the main roof."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"Hip to Gable", t:"Hip to Gable — New Gable Wall", u:"0.17 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K (actual U-value achieved 0.17 W/m²K)", mf:{"k":"wall","lim":0.18,"p":{"outer":"brick","cavity":100,"fill":"full","insulation":"k106","thickness":90,"inner":"a011","mortar":"gp","finish":"pbdabs","ties":"ss45x90","gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"cavfull","f":"90mm Kingspan Kooltherm K106 Cavity Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Where the roof is converted from hip to gable, the hip rafters and jack rafters are to be removed and a new gable wall constructed, all to the structural engineer's design. The new gable is to be built off the existing flank wall, whose adequacy — together with that of the foundation beneath it — is to be confirmed by the engineer before the hip is removed.",
    "New gable in masonry to comprise a 103mm brick outer leaf to match existing, 100mm cavity with 90mm Kingspan Kooltherm K106 Cavity Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) retained against the inner leaf, and a 100mm aircrete block inner leaf of 0.11 W/mK thermal conductivity (Celcon Solar, Thermalite Turbo or equivalent, 2.9N with the strength grade confirmed by the structural engineer) with 12.5mm plasterboard on dabs. This build-up calculates at 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 allowing for mortar joints, wall ties and an air-gap correction of 0.01; with a 0.15 W/mK block (Celcon Standard, Thermalite Shield) it calculates at 0.18 and meets the standard with no margin, and the 0.11 block is what gives a mineral wool full fill of the same cavity the margin to meet it. Where a block of a different thermal conductivity is proposed, the contractor is to provide the manufacturer's project-specific U-value calculation for the build-up as it will be built, demonstrating a maximum of 0.18 W/m²K, before any materials are ordered. Alternatively in timber frame, with an outer leaf of tile hanging or render to match the elevations.",
    "The new gable is to be tied into the existing flank wall and to the new roof structure, with a new wall plate bedded and strapped. Provide lateral restraint straps at not more than 2m centres at roof and floor level. Cavity trays, damp proof courses and cavity barriers to be continued through the new work.",
    "Temporary support to the roof is to be designed and installed before the hip is cut, and is not to be struck until the new gable and roof structure are complete and have gained adequate strength.",
    "NOTE — A 90mm board in a 100mm cavity leaves only a 10mm residual cavity, less than the board's certificate normally allows for partial fill. Confirm the actual cavity width on site and the residual cavity the BBA certificate permits before ordering; where it cannot be achieved, widen the cavity to the engineer's approval or use the timber frame gable build-up."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"Dormer Construction (Walls)", t:"Dormer Cheek — Rendered on Carrier Board", u:"0.16 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K", mf:{"k":"frame","lim":0.18,"p":{"insulation":"k112","thickness":140,"studWidth":38,"studDepth":140,"spacing":400,"sheathing":"ply18","lining":"k118_375","void":0,"gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"frame","f":"140mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"},{"r":"lining","f":"37.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "Dormer cheek as the tile-hung cheek but finished in a through-coloured silicone or polymer render system applied to a proprietary render carrier board on treated battens over the breather membrane, with a 25mm minimum drained and ventilated cavity behind the board, stainless steel beads, a bell cast drip at the base and movement beads at not more than 5m centres and at changes of substrate. Render system to be installed by the system manufacturer's approved contractor to the BBA certificate.",
    "Framing of 18mm plywood sheathing, 140mm x 38mm C16 studs at 400mm centres with the full stud depth filled with 140mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK), a continuous vapour control layer and 37.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard with joints taped. This build-up calculates at 0.16 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 by the combined method, allowing for the stud fraction at 400mm centres and an air-gap correction of 0.01.",
    "Where the cheek is within 1m of the boundary the render system and carrier board are to achieve the fire performance for an external wall within 1m of the relevant boundary and the unprotected area limited to 1m²; cavity barriers at the perimeter of the cheek and at the junction with the main roof."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"Dormer Construction (Walls)", t:"Dormer Cheek — Timber or Fibre Cement Cladding", u:"0.16 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K", mf:{"k":"frame","lim":0.18,"p":{"insulation":"k112","thickness":140,"studWidth":38,"studDepth":140,"spacing":400,"sheathing":"ply18","lining":"k118_375","void":0,"gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"frame","f":"140mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"},{"r":"lining","f":"37.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "Dormer cheek as the tile-hung cheek but finished in vertical or horizontal timber boarding of a durable species (larch, western red cedar or thermally modified softwood) or fibre cement weatherboard, fixed with stainless steel fixings to treated battens and counter-battens over the breather membrane with a 25mm minimum drained and ventilated cavity, insect mesh at the head and base, and cover trims at corners and abutments. The cheek behind the cladding is as the tile-hung cheek: 18mm external quality plywood sheathing, 140mm x 38mm C16 studs at 400mm centres with the full stud depth filled with 140mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK), a continuous vapour control layer on the warm side, and 37.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard with joints taped.",
    "Framing, insulation, vapour control layer and lining as the rendered cheek, calculating at 0.16 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946. Timber cladding is a combustible surface: where the cheek is within 1m of the boundary the cladding is to be fire retardant treated to Class B-s3, d2 or the cheek clad in a non-combustible material, and cavity barriers are to be provided at the perimeter of the cheek and at the junction with the main roof.",
    "NOTE — Timber cladding weathers to grey within two years unless coated and maintained; agree the finish with the client and the planning officer before ordering."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"Hip to Gable", t:"Hip to Gable — Timber Frame Gable on Existing Wall", u:"0.16 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K", mf:{"k":"frame","lim":0.18,"p":{"insulation":"k112","thickness":140,"studWidth":38,"studDepth":140,"spacing":400,"sheathing":"osb9","lining":"k118_375","void":0,"gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"frame","f":"140mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"},{"r":"lining","f":"37.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "Where the existing flank wall or its foundation will not carry a new masonry gable, or where the gable is to be clad to match tile hanging or render on the elevations, the new gable is to be a timber frame panel of 9mm OSB3 sheathing externally over 140mm x 38mm C16 studs, built off a treated softwood sole plate bedded on a damp proof course on the head of the existing wall and fixed with resin anchors or straps to the engineer's design. Panel of 140mm x 38mm C16 studs at 400mm centres with head and sole plates and noggins, 9mm OSB3 sheathing externally, breather membrane, treated battens and counter-battens, and tile hanging, slate hanging or render on a carrier board to match the elevations.",
    "Fill the full stud depth with 140mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK), a continuous vapour control layer on the warm side taped at all laps, and 37.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard with joints taped and skimmed. The panel calculates at 0.16 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 by the combined method, allowing for the studs bridging the insulation at 9.5% of the area at 400mm centres and an air-gap correction of 0.01. With 12.5mm plasterboard alone in place of the insulated board the same panel calculates at 0.21 and does not meet the standard for a new thermal element.",
    "The existing wall below is to be assessed by the engineer for the new loads and for its lateral stability without the hip, and the new gable is to be strapped to the new roof structure and to the loft floor at not more than 2m centres. Cavity barriers at the junction with the existing wall and at the eaves, and a cavity tray or flashing at the junction between the timber frame and the masonry below with weep holes to the outer cladding.",
    "NOTE — Where the existing wall is a single-leaf solid wall, the new gable above it is exposed to more weather than the wall below and the cladding, membrane and flashing at the base of the panel are the details that keep the loft dry. Draw them."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"Dwarf / Ashlar Walls", t:"Dwarf / Ashlar Wall at Eaves", u:"0.17 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K", mf:{"k":"frame","lim":0.18,"p":{"insulation":"k112","thickness":89,"studWidth":38,"studDepth":89,"spacing":400,"sheathing":"none","lining":"k118_625","void":0,"gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"frame","f":"89mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"},{"r":"lining","f":"62.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "Dwarf wall to the eaves generally 1050mm to 1200mm high as the sections, comprising 89mm x 38mm C16 studs at 400mm centres between sole plate and head plate, fixed to the new floor structure and to the rafters. Infill the full stud depth with 89mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) and add 62.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard to the room face, with a continuous vapour control layer on the warm side. This build-up calculates at 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 by the combined method, allowing for the studs bridging the insulation at 400mm centres and an air-gap correction of 0.01; with 37.5mm board on the room face it calculates at 0.21 and does not meet the standard for a new thermal element, and 52.5mm calculates at 0.18 with no margin.",
    "Insulation is to run continuously from the sloping ceiling, down the face of the dwarf wall, and across the floor of the eaves void behind it, so that the thermal envelope is unbroken at both the head and the foot of the wall. The insulation line at the foot is to be lapped and sealed to the floor insulation.",
    "The eaves void behind the dwarf wall remains outside the thermal envelope and is to be cross-ventilated. Provide an insulated and draught-sealed access hatch to each isolated section of void. Where services run in the void they are to be insulated."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"Upgrading Existing Party Wall", t:"Existing Party or Separating Wall — Upgrade", u:"0.26 W/m²K",
   tgt:"", mf:{"k":"lined","lim":0.3,"p":{"wall":"brick215","internalBoth":true,"gap":10,"lining":"k118_725","gapLevel":0},"s":[{"r":"lining","f":"72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "The existing separating wall is to be continued to the underside of the roof covering and fire stopped at the junction with the roof, in accordance with Approved Document B. Any gap, void, missing masonry or open perpend revealed during the works is to be made good in full, in masonry, before the new construction is closed up.",
    "Where the separating wall is cut into for beam bearings, the fire resistance of the wall is to be fully reinstated around the bearing, and the beam end sealed with fire resisting material.",
    "Where the separating wall is of cavity construction, the cavity is to be sealed at the eaves and at the top of the wall with a flexible cavity barrier to prevent the thermal bypass that would otherwise draw air through the wall. Full or partial filling of the party cavity is to be confirmed with the engineer and the adjoining owner before proceeding.",
    "Where the loft on the other side of the separating wall is not converted and remains an unheated roof void, the wall at loft level is a thermal element to an unheated space and is to be insulated to 0.30 W/m²K: 72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard on dabs or battens to the existing wall, drawn here as a 215mm solid brick wall. That calculates at 0.26 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 with both faces treated as internal surfaces, and to a 100mm single-leaf block wall at 0.27; 62.5mm calculates at 0.30 and 0.31 respectively and is not to be used. Where the adjoining loft is already a heated room the wall is not a thermal element and needs no insulation, but the fire and sound treatment below still applies.",
    "Improvement of the acoustic performance of the separating wall — by an independent lining on resilient bars with mineral wool infill — is recommended good practice at loft level, though not a Building Regulations requirement for a conversion within a single dwelling.",
    "NOTE — Requirement E1 applies to dwellings formed by new build or material change of use. A loft conversion within an existing single dwelling does not trigger it, so the acoustic upgrade above is offered as good practice rather than specified as compliance. Fire stopping of the separating wall is a requirement and is not optional."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"Upgrading Existing External Wall", t:"Existing Gable or Flank Wall — Internally Insulated", u:"0.26 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.30 W/m²K for a retained wall becoming part of the thermal envelope (Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.3), improved to 0.18 where floor area allows", mf:{"k":"lined","lim":0.3,"p":{"wall":"brick215","gap":10,"lining":"k118_725","gapLevel":0},"s":[{"r":"lining","f":"72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "Where an existing solid or cavity masonry gable wall becomes part of the thermal envelope, provide 72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard on dabs or on treated battens, with all joints tightly butted and taped and a continuous vapour control layer where the board does not incorporate one. On a 215mm solid brick wall 72.5mm calculates at 0.26 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 (62.5mm calculates at 0.30 with no margin); on an uninsulated cavity wall 62.5mm calculates at 0.29. Where the new-element value of 0.18 is wanted, 112.5mm board or a 90mm insulated stud lining is needed and the loss of floor width is to be weighed against it.",
    "The existing wall is to be inspected for damp, defective pointing and cracked render before lining, and any defect made good. Internal insulation increases the risk of interstitial condensation within the existing masonry.",
    "Where achieving 0.18 W/m²K internally would create a technical risk or an unreasonable loss of floor area, Approved Document L allows a lesser standard on the grounds of technical or functional feasibility, with the reasoning recorded. State the achieved value and the justification rather than leaving it unaddressed."]},
  {g:"IW", c:"Internal Works", t:"Insulated Stud Partition", u:"",
   tgt:"",
   p:[
    "Internal stud partition to comprise 89mm x 38mm C16 studs at 400mm centres with head and sole plates and solid noggins at board joints and at all fixings. Infill the full stud depth with 89mm acoustic mineral wool. Line both faces with 12.5mm plasterboard with joints staggered and all perimeters sealed."]},
  {g:"IW", c:"Internal Works", t:"Fire Resisting Partition to Protected Stairway", u:"",
   tgt:"",
   p:[
    "Partitions enclosing the protected stairway to achieve 30 minutes fire resistance (EI 30, or REI 30 where the partition is loadbearing). Construction to comprise 89mm x 38mm C16 studs at 400mm centres, 89mm mineral wool infill, and one layer of 12.5mm fire-rated plasterboard each face with all joints taped and filled and all perimeters sealed with an intumescent or fire-rated sealant.",
    "Where the existing enclosure to the stair is retained, it is to be inspected and upgraded as necessary to achieve the same standard. Lath and plaster in sound condition may be acceptable subject to the Building Control Officer's agreement."]},
  {g:"IF", c:"Loft Floor", t:"New Loft Floor", u:"",
   tgt:"",
   p:[
    "New floor to comprise floor joists to the structural engineer's design, generally spanning independently of the existing ceiling joists and supported on new steel beams at wall plate level. The detail is drawn on 47mm x 220mm C24 joists at 400mm centres; the engineer's design governs the size and the centres for the span actually built. Deck with 22mm moisture resistant tongued and grooved flooring grade board, glued and screwed.",
    "Provide 100mm mineral wool quilt of minimum density 10 kg/m³ between the joists for airborne sound resistance, and line the underside with two layers of 12.5mm plasterboard with joints staggered, taped and filled, to achieve a minimum REI 30 fire resistance and to satisfy Requirement E2.",
    "All perimeters and service penetrations to be sealed. Where the existing ceiling is retained below the new floor, its condition and fire resistance are to be confirmed on site."]}
 ],
 notes:[
  {c:"Planning & Permitted Development", t:"Planning Status and Permitted Development (Classes B and C)", p:[
    "The planning status of the works is to be confirmed before construction. Building Regulations approval does not confer planning permission, and planning permission does not confer Building Regulations approval.",
    "Where the conversion is carried out under permitted development it is to be built strictly within Class B (roof enlargement) and Class C (rooflights) of Part 1 of Schedule 2 to the Town and Country Planning (General Permitted Development) (England) Order 2015 as amended: the additional roof volume not exceeding 40 cubic metres for a terraced house or 50 cubic metres for a semi-detached or detached house (counting any previous roof additions), no part of the enlargement higher than the existing ridge, no part extending beyond the plane of the existing roof slope on the principal elevation fronting a highway, the enlargement set back not less than 200mm from the original eaves (other than a hip-to-gable extension or where the enlargement joins the roof of a rear extension), materials of similar appearance to the existing roof, no verandas, balconies or raised platforms, and side-facing windows obscure glazed and non-opening below 1.7m above the floor. Rooflights under Class C are not to project more than 150mm beyond the roof plane. Class B is not permitted development on designated land (conservation areas and other Article 2(3) land), permitted development rights may have been removed by an Article 4 Direction or by a condition on the original permission, and listed buildings require listed building consent in every case. Where the limits are exceeded, planning permission is required.",
    "NOTE — Permitted development limits are set by legislation that is amended from time to time. Confirm the current volume allowances and conditions, and check for an Article 4 Direction, before relying on this note for a specific scheme. A lawful development certificate is recommended where the scheme relies on permitted development."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Compliance and Scope", p:[
    "All works to be carried out in accordance with the Building Regulations 2010 (as amended) and the Approved Documents current at the date of issue. All materials and workmanship to comply with Approved Document 7 (Regulation 7).",
    "This specification is to be read in conjunction with the drawing pack issued with this specification, the structural engineer's design and calculations, and any specialist sub-contractor design.",
    "All dimensions to be checked on site before fabrication or ordering. Do not scale from the drawings. All works to be carried out in a safe manner in accordance with the CDM Regulations 2015."]},
  {c:"Upgrading Existing Party Wall", t:"Party Wall Matters", p:[
    "Compliance with the Party Wall etc. Act 1996 is the responsibility of the client. Loft conversions in terraced and semi-detached properties will generally involve notifiable work where beams bear onto or are cut into the party wall, or where the party wall is raised or cut into. Awards are to be in place before the affected work commences."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Structural Design — General", p:[
    "All structural elements including new floor joists, steel beams, padstones, trimming to openings and any alteration to the existing roof structure to the structural engineer's design and calculations, to be submitted to Building Control prior to the affected work commencing.",
    "New floor joists are to span independently of the existing ceiling joists, which are not to be relied upon to carry floor loading. New steel beams generally at wall plate level, with load paths traced down to foundations of adequate capacity. Where the load path passes through the existing structure, the adequacy of the supporting walls and foundations is to be confirmed by the engineer.",
    "Padstones to be as detailed by the engineer, with a minimum 150mm end bearing to all beams. Cut bricks or blocks beneath padstones will not be accepted.",
    "Existing structure to be opened up as required by the Building Control Officer. Any condition differing from that assumed in the design to be reported to the structural engineer immediately and work in that area stopped."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Existing Roof Structure", p:[
    "Where the existing roof is of trussed rafter construction, no member is to be cut, notched or removed without the structural engineer's design. Trussed rafter roofs generally require the trusses to be replaced or a new independent structure installed.",
    "Where purlins are to be removed or repositioned, temporary support is to be provided and the permanent replacement installed before the temporary works are struck. Removal of a purlin affects the whole roof and is not to proceed without the engineer's design.",
    "Existing rafters are to be inspected for rot, insect attack and deflection before the design is finalised, and defective members replaced."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Temporary Works", p:[
    "The contractor is responsible for the design, installation, maintenance and removal of all temporary works, and for the stability of the existing building at all times. The roof is not to be left open to the weather without adequate protection and restraint."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Houses in Multiple Occupation", opt:1, p:[
    "Where the house is, or on completion will be, occupied by three or more people forming more than one household sharing facilities, it is a house in multiple occupation. A loft conversion in an HMO changes the fire strategy: the local housing authority's HMO standards and the LACORS housing fire safety guidance apply alongside the Building Regulations, and typically require a Grade D1 Category LD2 or LD1 alarm system, fire doors to all bedrooms and the kitchen onto the protected route, 30 minute separation throughout, and emergency lighting where the route is long or unlit. Where the HMO has five or more occupants it is licensable and the licence conditions govern.",
    "NOTE — Confirm the intended occupation with the client in writing before the fire strategy is fixed. A conversion designed for a single family and let as an HMO will fail its licence inspection on the fire doors and alarm grade."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Lateral Restraint and Strapping", p:[
    "The new loft floor and the roof are to provide lateral restraint to the walls they bear on. Provide 30mm x 5mm galvanised mild steel restraint straps not less than 1m long at not more than 2m centres: at the new floor level to walls parallel to the joists, fixed across not less than three joists with solid noggins beneath and packing between the wall and the first joist; at the wall plate and at rafter level to the gable and flank walls, with the straps turned down not less than 100mm into the cavity or over the inner face and screwed to the masonry; and to any new flat roof, strapping the joists to the walls in the same way and holding the wall plate down against wind uplift.",
    "Straps to be fixed with not less than four fixings into the timber and to be carried over noggins where they cross joists, so the restraint is to the whole floor and not to one member. Where the existing walls have no restraint at the existing ceiling level the engineer is to confirm whether new straps are needed there as part of the works."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Steelwork and Fire Protection", p:[
    "Structural steel beams and posts to BS EN 10025, fabricated and erected to the engineer's design with bolted connections and bearing plates, on padstones as the Structural Design note. Steel to be primed and, where it forms part of the floor or the protected stairway enclosure, protected to give 30 minutes fire resistance by encasement in two layers of 12.5mm plasterboard or one layer of 15mm fire-resisting board with the joints staggered, or by an intumescent coating applied to the certified thickness for the section factor, with the certificate given to building control. Timber joists bearing on a steel beam to be on hangers or bolted to a timber plate bolted to the web, not simply resting on the flange."]},
  {c:"Dormer Construction (Walls)", t:"Dormer Construction", p:[
    "Dormer structure to the structural engineer's design, framed off the new floor structure or off trimmed rafters as detailed, with the cheek studs, corner posts and head and sole plates fixed to the trimmers and the dormer roof joists or rafters tied back to the main roof structure. Proprietary cavity trays and stepped flashings at all abutments with masonry, and soakers, secret gutters and back gutters at the junction with the main roof slope, all as the Leadwork, Flashings and Valleys note.",
    "Where a dormer or roof extension is within 1m of the relevant boundary, the cheek is to have 30 minutes fire resistance from both sides with its unprotected areas and external surface limited as the External Fire Spread note, and cavity barriers are to be provided at the perimeter of every cheek and at the junction with the main roof as the Fire Stopping and Cavity Barriers note."]},
  {c:"Upgrading Existing Roof", t:"Upgrading the Existing Roof Covering", p:[
    "Where the roof covering is stripped and re-laid, provide a new breathable roofing underlay to BS 5534 and new treated battens of the size and grade required by BS 5534 for the rafter spacing and exposure. Tiles or slates may be re-used where sound; broken, delaminated or spalled units are to be replaced with matching new.",
    "All existing roof timbers exposed by the strip are to be inspected for rot, insect attack and deflection, and defective members replaced to the structural engineer's design. Existing flashings, soakers and valley linings are to be renewed rather than re-used.",
    "Where more than 50% of the surface area of the roof covering is replaced, the roof becomes a renovated thermal element and any part of it with a U-value worse than 0.35 W/m²K is to be upgraded to 0.16 W/m²K or better (Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.3) unless this is not technically or functionally feasible; the room-in-roof build-ups in this specification achieve 0.15 W/m²K or better and satisfy both the renovated-element and the new-element standard."]},
  {c:"Dormer Construction (Roof)", t:"Leadwork, Flashings and Valleys", p:[
    "Leadwork to the Lead Sheet Training Academy's Rolled Lead Sheet Manual: Code 4 lead for soakers, cover flashings and small aprons, Code 5 for valley gutters, back gutters and the flat areas of dormer roofs, and Code 6 for wide gutters, all with the piece lengths limited to 1.5m and the laps, upstands and expansion allowances in the manual. Stepped and cover flashings at every abutment with masonry to be tucked 25mm into the joint and wedged; flashings against a rendered dormer cheek to be carried behind the render carrier board. Patination oil to be applied to all new lead on completion.",
    "Valleys between the dormer roof and the main slope to be formed as a lead-lined or GRP valley on valley boards with a 100mm minimum lap between lengths, or as a proprietary dry valley system where the pitch and tile allow. Back gutters behind a dormer to be laid to fall to the sides with a minimum 150mm upstand and a sole wide enough to clear snow and debris. Where lead is used with a copper-containing alloy or on oak, isolate it with a building paper. Lead substitutes may be used where the planning authority accepts them and the product carries a BBA certificate for the application."]},
  {c:"Windows & Rooflights", t:"Rooflights", p:[
    "Rooflights to achieve a U-value not exceeding 2.2 W/m²K (1.6 W/m²K specified), installed to the manufacturer's instructions with proprietary flashing kits appropriate to the roof covering and pitch. Provide a fully sealed vapour control layer and insulated liner to the rooflight reveal, continuous with the roof insulation.",
    "Where a rooflight is to serve as the means of escape, refer to the Means of Escape section for the required opening size and cill height."]},
  {c:"Loft Floor", t:"Fire Resistance of the New Floor over a Retained Ceiling", p:[
    "The new loft floor becomes a floor in a three-storey house and is to achieve REI 30 as the New Loft Floor build-up, including any part of the existing ceiling below that is retained. Where the existing lath and plaster or plasterboard ceiling is retained beneath the new joists and cannot be boarded from below, the 30 minute fire resistance is to be demonstrated by the combination actually built: either the existing ceiling assessed to BRE Digest 208 and upgraded with a further 12.5mm fire-resisting board fixed through to the existing joists, or 100mm mineral wool of not less than 33 kg/m³ laid on netting between the new joists beneath the new tongued and grooved deck (not less than 18mm; 22mm specified), with the arrangement stated on the drawings and agreed with building control before the floor is closed.",
    "Recessed downlighters, loft hatches, extract ducts and pipe and cable penetrations through the new floor or the retained ceiling are to be fire-rated or fire stopped so that the 30 minute standard is maintained across the whole floor area, including inside the eaves voids."]},
  {c:"Internal Works", t:"Staircase to the Loft", p:[
    "New stair to Approved Document K: pitch not more than 42 degrees, rise between 150mm and 220mm, going not less than 220mm, twice the rise plus the going between 550mm and 700mm, all rises and goings in a flight equal, and a handrail on at least one side at 900mm to 1000mm above the pitch line, on both sides where the flight is 1m or wider, with not less than 600mm clear between handrails where two are fitted. Tapered treads to have a going of not less than 50mm at the narrow end and to satisfy the going rule at the centre line (or 270mm from the narrow end where the stair is wider than 1m); landings at the top and bottom of every flight at least as long as the flight is wide and level, with no door swinging over a landing other than a cupboard door which leaves 400mm clear; open risers not to pass a 100mm sphere; timber stairs to BS 585 with the strings, treads and risers glued and wedged and the newels bolted to the floor structure.",
    "Headroom to be not less than 2m measured vertically from the pitch line over every flight and landing. In a loft conversion where the full 2m cannot be achieved, the reduced headroom shown in Diagram 1.4 of Approved Document K may be used, measured at the centre of the stair width and at the side; the new stair is to be set out so that the roof slope, the trimmer to the new floor opening and any dormer head do not encroach on it.",
    "An alternating tread stair may be used only in a loft conversion, only where there is not enough space for a stair complying with the normal requirements, and only where it gives access to one habitable room and, if required, a bathroom and/or WC, provided that WC is not the only one in the dwelling; it is to be in one or more straight flights with uniform steps, slip-resistant treads, handrails on both sides and 2m headroom.",
    "Guarding to every flight, landing and open side where the drop exceeds 600mm: 900mm high to stairs and to internal landings and floor edges, and 1100mm to external balconies and roof terraces (Approved Document K Diagram 3.1), not climbable by children, not passing a 100mm sphere, and designed for the horizontal load in BS EN 1991-1-1 (0.36 kN/m for a dwelling). The guarding around the new stairwell opening in the loft floor is to be built before the stair is used for access.",
    "NOTE — The reduced headroom dimensions are given in Diagram 1.4 rather than in the text of Approved Document K. The values generally applied are 1.9m at the centre of the stair width and 1.8m at the side. Confirm against the diagram in the current edition before relying on them, and agree the arrangement with the Building Control Officer at design stage where headroom is tight."]},
  {c:"Internal Works", t:"Internal Finishes and Joinery", p:[
    "Plasterboard to be fixed and finished in accordance with the manufacturer's instructions, with all perimeters and service penetrations sealed. Provide solid noggins at all board joints and at fixings for sanitaryware, radiators and wall-hung items.",
    "Internal wall and ceiling linings to achieve the surface spread of flame classification required by Approved Document B for the room or circulation space concerned."]},
  {c:"Internal Works", t:"Door Between House and Integral Garage", opt:1, p:[
    "Where the dwelling has an integral garage and the works affect the connection between the two, the garage is to be separated from the dwelling by construction achieving a minimum REI 30 standard.",
    "Any door between the garage and the dwelling is to be an FD30S doorset (E 30 Sa, intumescent strips and cold smoke seals) fitted with a self-closing device. The threshold is to be formed with a step of not less than 100mm up from the garage floor into the dwelling, or alternatively the garage floor is to be laid to fall away from the door to a drain or to the garage door opening, so that fuel spillage cannot flow into the dwelling.",
    "Any service penetration through the separating construction is to be fire stopped with a proprietary system tested for the substrate and service concerned."]},
  {c:"Internal Works", t:"Balconies and Juliet Balustrading", opt:1, p:[
    "Guarding to balconies and to Juliet balustrades across door openings to be a minimum 1100mm high measured from finished floor level, constructed so that a 100mm sphere cannot pass through any opening and so that children cannot readily climb it.",
    "Guarding to be designed to resist the horizontal line load, uniformly distributed load and point load appropriate to a domestic balcony in accordance with BS 6180 and BS EN 1991-1-1 with its UK National Annex. Loadings and the fixing design to be confirmed by the structural engineer.",
    "Where glass is used it is to be laminated safety glass, capable of sustaining the design loading and of remaining in place if fractured. Frameless and structural glass balustrades to be designed by the balustrade specialist, with calculations submitted to Building Control.",
    "Fixings to be into the structural frame or into masonry with resin anchors as the engineer's design, not into the floor deck or plasterboard. Where a balustrade is fixed through the waterproofing or the vapour control layer, the penetration is to be fully sealed and made good."]},
  {c:"Sound Insulation", t:"Internal Walls and Floors — Requirement E2", p:[
    "New internal walls between a bedroom or a room containing a WC and other rooms, and new internal floors within the dwelling, are to achieve a minimum laboratory sound reduction of 40 dB Rw in accordance with Requirement E2 and Approved Document E.",
    "The new loft floor and the stud partitions are specified to meet this standard. All perimeters, junctions and service penetrations to be sealed, and gaps around pipes and cables filled, as an unsealed penetration will defeat the construction."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Escape Strategy — Protected Stairway and the Alternatives", p:[
    "A loft conversion to a two-storey dwellinghouse creates a third storey whose floor is more than 4.5m above ground, so escape windows are not an acceptable means of escape from the new storey and escape is to be by way of the stair. Approved Document B Volume 1 Section 2 (paragraphs 2.5 and 2.6) gives three ways of protecting it and one is to be chosen, agreed with building control before the layouts are fixed, and shown on the fire plan. Option 1, the protected stairway (the arrangement this specification is written around): the stair enclosed at every storey by construction giving 30 minutes fire resistance (EI 30, or REI 30 where loadbearing) as the Fire Resisting Partition build-up, with fire doorsets as the Fire Doors note to every habitable room and the kitchen opening onto it, discharging to a final exit or giving access to two escape routes at ground level each leading to a final exit and separated from one another by fire-resisting construction and a fire door. Option 2, where the ground floor is or will be open plan: either the new top storey separated from the lower storeys by 30 minute fire-resisting construction with its own alternative escape route, or the stair separated from the open-plan ground floor by a 30 minute fire-resisting enclosure with a fire door, the open-plan area fitted with a sprinkler system to BS 9251 and the kitchen separated from the open-plan area and the stair by a fire-resisting partition and door. Option 3: a sprinkler system to BS 9251 throughout the dwelling, with an escape window from the new storey where its floor is not more than 4.5m above ground and otherwise with the stair enclosure retained.",
    "Where a bungalow is converted to give a new first floor whose floor is not more than 4.5m above ground, escape from the new rooms may be by emergency egress windows or rooflights with an unobstructed openable area of at least 0.33m², not less than 450mm high and 450mm wide, the bottom of the openable area not more than 1100mm above finished floor level and not less than 800mm unless guarding is provided to Approved Document K, openable without a key from inside, or by a protected stair; where any floor exceeds 4.5m the protected stairway options above apply.",
    "Any inner room created by the conversion (a habitable room reached only through another room) is to be identified and resolved: the inner room is to have an escape window to the standard above where its floor is not more than 4.5m above ground, or a door onto the protected stairway, and no habitable room is to be accessed only through another habitable room unless the arrangement satisfies Approved Document B. The existing protected route at ground and first floor, including its doors, glazing and the final exit, is to be inspected and upgraded as part of the works so that the strategy is complete from the new storey to the outside."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Fire Doors to the Protected Stairway", p:[
    "New doors to all habitable rooms and the kitchen opening onto the protected stairway, at every storey, to be FD30S doorsets (E 30 Sa, with intumescent strips and cold smoke seals) fitted with self-closers and hung on hinges of the type and number the doorset certificate requires. This exceeds the Approved Document B Volume 1 Appendix C minimum for a dwellinghouse of an E 20 (FD20) doorset without a self-closer and is adopted as this practice's standard.",
    "Existing doors at ground and first floor level on the protected route are to be upgraded or replaced as necessary in accordance with paragraph 2.21. Solid panelled doors of not less than 35mm in sound condition may be upgraded in place with intumescent strips and cold smoke seals, a proprietary tested upgrade to the panels where needed and the stops and frame made good, to give the door a fair chance of meeting the E 20 minimum, subject to the Building Control Officer's agreement; hollow-core doors are to be replaced with FD30S doorsets.",
    "Glazing within a fire door or fire resisting partition to be fire resisting glazing in a tested and approved frame and beading system, of the same period as the door or partition."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Fire Detection and Alarm", p:[
    "Provide a fire detection and alarm system to BS 5839-6 of not less than Grade D1 Category LD2 — mains powered, interlinked smoke alarms to BS EN 14604 with an integral tamper-proof standby supply — with at least one smoke alarm in the circulation space at every storey including the new loft storey, in the principal habitable room and in any room in which a fire could start that opens onto the escape route, and a heat alarm to BS 5446-2 in the kitchen, all interlinked with each other and with the existing system. This exceeds the Grade D2 Category LD3 minimum given in Approved Document B Volume 1 for a materially altered dwelling and is adopted as this practice's standard; BS 5839-6 itself recommends Category LD2 for a three-storey house.",
    "Alarms to be ceiling mounted not less than 300mm from any wall or light fitting, within 7.5m of the door to every habitable room and within 3m of every bedroom door, with no point in a hallway more than 7.5m from an alarm; where the kitchen is not separated from the stair or circulation space by a door an interlinked heat alarm in the kitchen is required in every case. A carbon monoxide alarm to BS EN 50291 is to be fitted in every room containing a fixed combustion appliance. Alarms to be supplied from an independent circuit at the consumer unit or from a regularly used local lighting circuit, as BS 5839-6 permits for Grade D1.",
    "Where the dwelling is or will be let, the Smoke and Carbon Monoxide Alarm (England) Regulations 2015 as amended in 2022 additionally require at least one smoke alarm on every storey with living accommodation and a carbon monoxide alarm in every room with a fixed combustion appliance other than a gas cooker, tested at the start of each tenancy and repaired or replaced when reported faulty; the system above satisfies this, and the landlord is to be given the commissioning certificate. Where the house is a house in multiple occupation the Houses in Multiple Occupation note applies.",
    "A design, installation and commissioning certificate for the fire detection and alarm system to be issued to building control on completion."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Minimum Periods of Fire Resistance", p:[
    "For a dwellinghouse with a top storey not more than 5m above ground (the usual two-storey house) the floors and loadbearing elements require 30 minutes fire resistance; where the loft conversion takes the top storey above 5m but not more than 18m, which is every three-storey house, the elements of structure remain 30 minutes provided the house has no more than three storeys, with the separating wall to a neighbour at 60 minutes. Where the house has four or more storeys including a basement, 60 minutes applies to the elements of structure and the fire strategy is to be reviewed as a whole. Fire resistance is stated in the European notation: REI 30 is loadbearing capacity, integrity and insulation for 30 minutes (floors and loadbearing walls), EI 30 is integrity and insulation (the non-loadbearing stair enclosure), and E 30 Sa is integrity for 30 minutes with restricted smoke leakage at ambient temperature (the FD30S doorset); E 20 is the modern equivalent of the old FD20 designation. The new floor over the existing first floor becomes a floor in a three-storey house and is to achieve REI 30 including any part of the existing ceiling below that is retained, as the Loft Floor note."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Fire Stopping and Cavity Barriers", p:[
    "Fire stop all service penetrations through fire resisting construction using a proprietary system tested for the substrate and service concerned, including pipes, ducts and cables passing through the new floor, the stair enclosure and the party wall. Provide cavity barriers at the perimeter of dormer cheeks, at the junction of the dormer with the main roof, at the eaves and at the junction of the roof with the separating wall, and at the edges of all other cavities in accordance with Approved Document B Volume 1 Section 9; the separating wall is to be fire stopped to the underside of the roof covering as the Existing Party or Separating Wall build-up."]},
  {c:"Windows & Rooflights", t:"Windows and Glazing", p:[
    "New windows to achieve a whole-window U-value not exceeding 1.4 W/m²K or Window Energy Rating Band B (Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.2), with 1.2 W/m²K specified; rooflights as the Rooflights note. Replacement windows to be installed by a FENSA or CERTASS registered installer or notified to building control.",
    "Glazing in critical locations — within 800mm of floor level, and within 1500mm of floor level in doors and in side panels within 300mm of a door — to be safety glazing satisfying the impact performance requirements of Approved Document K, permanently marked.",
    "Where a window cill is less than 800mm above finished floor level and the drop externally exceeds 600mm, provide guarding or an opening restrictor in accordance with Approved Document K. Any restrictor fitted to a window serving as an emergency escape window is to be releasable without a key from inside."]},
  {c:"Condensation (C2)", t:"Surface and Interstitial Condensation", p:[
    "Reasonable precautions are to be taken to prevent surface and interstitial condensation in roofs, walls and floors, in accordance with Approved Document C2 and BS 5250.",
    "Provide a continuous vapour control layer on the warm side of the insulation in all roof and dormer constructions, with all laps a minimum 150mm, taped and sealed, and sealed at all service penetrations, junctions and perimeters. The vapour control layer is not to be punctured by downlighters, cabling or fixings without being made good.",
    "Where an existing masonry wall is insulated internally, the risk of interstitial condensation within the existing masonry is increased. The external face is to be maintained weathertight, rainwater goods are to be checked and repaired, and a condensation risk analysis is to be carried out where the wall is exposed or of solid construction.",
    "Ventilate all residual roof voids. Extract ducts passing through unheated voids are to be insulated to prevent condensation forming within the duct."]},
  {c:"Drainage", t:"Rainwater Drainage", p:[
    "New dormer and roof extension roofs to drain to gutters and downpipes sized to BS EN 12056-3 for the roof area, in a material to match the existing, with the dormer gutters discharging to the main roof slope only where the tile course below can take the concentrated flow and otherwise to a downpipe connected to the existing rainwater system. Existing gutters, downpipes and gullies to be cleared, checked for falls and repaired where the works disturb them, and the existing surface water connection confirmed as running to a surface water or combined drain and not to the foul drain. Where a new downpipe cannot reach an existing drain, a soakaway to BRE Digest 365 not less than 5m from the building is to be provided. Rainwater is not to discharge onto a neighbour's land or against the base of the wall."]},
  {c:"Drainage", t:"Foul Drainage", p:[
    "Foul drainage to new sanitary accommodation to comply with Approved Document H1 and BS EN 12056-2. All appliances to be trapped with a minimum 75mm trap seal, with branch lengths not exceeding 1.7m for a 32mm basin waste (3m at 40mm), 3m for a 40mm bath or shower waste (4m at 50mm) and 6m for a 100mm WC branch, beyond which anti-vacuum traps or a ventilated branch are needed; no branch is to connect within 200mm below a WC branch connection and the stack is to have a long-radius bend at its foot. A pumped WC or macerator to an en-suite is permitted only where a gravity WC is also available in the dwelling, installed to the manufacturer's certificate with an accessible isolating valve.",
    "Where the existing soil and vent pipe is extended or a new stack installed, it is to terminate not less than 900mm above any opening into the building within 3m and be fitted with a cage or perforated cover. An air admittance valve to BS EN 12380 may replace an open vent only where at least one stack on the drainage system remains open to atmosphere and the valve is above the highest spill-over level in a ventilated and accessible position. Internal stacks to be boxed in and wrapped with 25mm unfaced mineral wool insulation, and where a stack passes through the new floor or other fire resisting construction it is to be fire stopped and, where required, fitted with an intumescent collar.",
    "Where a new stack needs a new below-ground connection, the drain is to be laid with flexible joints and 100mm pea gravel bedding and surround, not less than 600mm cover under gardens and 900mm under drives (or protected by a concrete slab on a compressible layer where the cover is less), with rocker pipes each side of any wall or foundation it passes through, an inspection chamber or rodding eye at the connection, and the run tested by air or water in the presence of the Building Control Officer before backfilling and again at completion. Building over or within 3m of a public sewer requires a build-over agreement with the sewerage undertaker."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Electrical Installation", p:[
    "All electrical work to comply with Approved Document P and BS 7671, designed, installed, inspected and tested by a competent person registered with an approved Part P scheme. Electrical Installation Certificate and Building Regulations Compliance Certificate to be issued to Building Control on completion.",
    "Cables passing through the new floor structure or through insulation are to be derated or routed to avoid it, in accordance with BS 7671. No cable is to be buried within insulation without the appropriate derating.",
    "All new fixed light fittings to be low energy with an efficacy of not less than 75 light-source lumens per circuit-watt with independent switching to each room, and downlighters in an insulated ceiling or the roof slope to be fire-rated fittings with a sealed hood so they do not breach the vapour control layer or the insulation. Sockets, switches and controls between 450mm and 1200mm above floor level."]},
  {c:"Conservation of Fuel & Power", t:"Thermal Elements and Thermal Bridging", p:[
    "New thermal elements to achieve U-values no worse than Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.2: roof 0.15 W/m²K, wall 0.18 W/m²K, windows 1.4 W/m²K (or Window Energy Rating Band B), rooflights 2.2 W/m²K, doors 1.4 W/m²K; the build-ups in this specification are written to these values and the U-value actually achieved is stated with each.",
    "Maintain continuity of insulation at all junctions — at the eaves, at the dormer cheek to roof junction, at the wall plate, at the junction with the party wall, at the dwarf wall head and foot, and around all rooflight and window reveals. Insulation is to be tightly butted with no gaps.",
    "Reasonable provision to be made to limit air leakage. Seal all penetrations of the air barrier including at joist ends, service entries, loft hatches and the perimeter of window and rooflight frames. Any new loft hatch or eaves access hatch to the residual voids to be insulated and draught sealed."]},
  {c:"Conservation of Fuel & Power", t:"Existing Thermal Elements", p:[
    "Where an existing thermal element is renovated as part of the works and the renovated area exceeds 50% of the surface area of that element or 25% of the total building envelope, the element is to be upgraded to the standard for a renovated thermal element in Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.3 where its existing U-value is worse than the threshold: roof (all types) threshold 0.35 W/m²K improved to 0.16 W/m²K, cavity wall 0.70 improved to 0.55, solid or other wall 0.70 improved to 0.30, floor 0.70 improved to 0.25. An existing gable, flank or party wall that becomes part of the thermal envelope of the new rooms is a retained thermal element and is to be upgraded to 0.30 W/m²K as the Upgrading Existing External Wall and Party Wall build-ups, improved towards 0.18 where floor area allows.",
    "Where achieving the figure would not be technically or functionally feasible, or would not pay back within 15 years, Approved Document L allows a lesser standard with the reasoning recorded; state the achieved value and the justification on the drawings rather than leaving it unaddressed."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Regulation 16 Notices — Commencement, Inspections and Completion", p:[
    "Regulation 16 of the Building Regulations 2010 (as amended in October 2023) requires three notices to the building control body: notice of intention to start work, given so that at least two clear days elapse before work begins; a commencement notice within five days of the work being regarded as commenced under Regulation 46A, which for a loft conversion (as for other work to an existing building) is when 15 per cent of the work is complete, after which the authority has four weeks to reject the notice; and a completion notice within five days of completion. The completion notice must give the name, address, telephone number and email of the client, the principal contractor and the principal designer, state that the work is complete, include a signed statement by the client that the work complies with the Building Regulations, and include signed statements from the principal designer and principal contractor confirming that they have fulfilled their duties under Part 2A with the dates of their appointments; where a statement cannot be obtained the reason is to be given. Where a registered building control approver is used, the same stages apply through the initial notice.",
    "Inspection stages to be notified as they arise: structural steelwork, beams and padstones before they are cased in; the new floor, the protected stairway enclosure and all other fire-resisting construction before it is closed up; roof and dormer insulation and the vapour control layer before they are boarded over; new drainage before it is covered and on test; and completion. Certificates from competent person schemes (electrical, gas, replacement windows and rooflights, heating appliances) and commissioning notices for fixed building services and ventilation are to be given to building control within the periods the Regulations set (normally five days) and copied to the client with the completion certificate, which is the document a purchaser's solicitor will ask for.",
    "NOTE — The two-day and five-day limbs are separate duties. Missing the commencement notice means the work is not treated as commenced for transitional purposes, which matters where an edition of the Approved Documents is about to change: the 2026 editions of Approved Documents L Volume 1 and F Volume 1 were published on 24 March 2026 and come into force on 24 March 2027 — check which edition applies at the date of the application or initial notice."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"External Fire Spread — Boundaries, Unprotected Areas and Roof Coverings", p:[
    "External walls within 1m of the relevant boundary are to have 30 minutes fire resistance from both sides (60 minutes where they separate buildings), an external surface of Class B-s3, d2 or better, and unprotected areas (windows, doors and combustible cladding) limited to small areas not exceeding 1m² and not less than 4m apart. Walls more than 1m from the boundary may have unprotected areas up to the limits in Approved Document B Volume 1 Section 11 for a residential building: 5.6m² at 1m, 12m² at 2m, 18m² at 3m, 24m² at 4m, 30m² at 5m and unlimited at 6m or more, measured for each side of the building facing that boundary; where cladding is worse than Class B-s3, d2 and more than 1mm thick, half its area counts as unprotected. The relevant boundary is the site boundary or the centre line of an adjoining road, canal or river.",
    "Roof coverings within 6m of the boundary are to be classified BROOF(t4) to BS EN 13501-5, which tiles, slates, felt on a substrate, single-ply membranes, GRP systems and glass with the appropriate classification achieve; thermoplastic rooflights are limited in area and distance as Section 12 and are not to be within 6m of the boundary unless classified. The classification of the roof covering, the rooflights and any cladding is to be stated on the drawings, and the position of the relevant boundary shown on the site plan so building control can check the unprotected area calculation."]},
  {c:"Ventilation", t:"Ventilation — Approved Document F", p:[
    "Ventilation to comply with Approved Document F Volume 1. The conversion must not make the existing ventilation provision in the dwelling worse than it was before the work: where the new stair or floor removes a window or a loft hatch that ventilated a room or a void, the provision is to be replaced. Background ventilators for natural ventilation (System 1) to give an equivalent area of not less than 8000mm² in every new habitable room (10,000mm² in a single-storey dwelling or flat) and 4000mm² in every bathroom, set not less than 1700mm above floor level, by trickle ventilators in the window and rooflight heads ordered as part of the window and rooflight units, with the equivalent area marked or recorded and noise-attenuating ventilators used on facades exposed to traffic or other noise. Purge ventilation to every habitable room by an openable area of not less than one twentieth of the floor area where the window or rooflight opens more than 30 degrees, or one tenth where it opens between 15 and 30 degrees, or a mechanical system giving four air changes per hour; the openable areas are to be checked against the window and rooflight schedule before issue. Internal doors undercut 10mm above the floor finish (7600mm² for a 760mm door).",
    "Intermittent mechanical extract to BS EN 13141-4 at not less than 15 litres per second from a bathroom or shower room, 6 litres per second from a WC, 30 litres per second from a utility room and, where a kitchen is formed, 30 litres per second from a cooker hood over the hob (fitted 650mm to 750mm above it, to BS EN 13141-3) or 60 litres per second elsewhere in the kitchen; fans may be wired to the light switch and are to have a 15 minute overrun where the room has no openable window. Ductwork rigid wherever possible with flexible duct limited to 1.5m at final connections and run straight, installed to BSRIA BG 43, insulated where it passes through the eaves voids or other unheated spaces to prevent condensation, fire stopped or fitted with fire dampers where it passes through fire-resisting construction, and discharged directly to outside air through a terminal with a back-draught shutter. Ducts are not to discharge into the roof void or the eaves voids.",
    "Where the design air permeability is below 5 m³/(h·m²), or the dwelling is single-aspect, or a room has no external wall, continuous mechanical extract (System 3) is to be used with 4000mm² background ventilators to each habitable room and not fewer than the number of bedrooms plus two, or mechanical ventilation with heat recovery (System 4) with no background ventilators, supply terminals placed to avoid draughts and wet-room air not recirculated; continuous rates not less than 13 litres per second from the kitchen and 8 from each bathroom and utility room and 6 from a WC, and the whole-dwelling rate the greater of 0.3 litres per second per square metre and the bedroom-count rate. Every fixed mechanical ventilation system to be commissioned with the flow rates measured to BS EN 13141-6 or 13141-7 and the results given to building control within five days of the final test and to the occupier in the Home User Guide."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Heating Systems — Boilers, Heat Pumps, Underfloor Heating and Hot Water", p:[
    "Where the works add to the existing heating system, the boiler and system capacity are to be confirmed as adequate for the additional load and new radiators fitted with thermostatic radiator valves; where a cold water storage cistern or feed and expansion tank in the loft is relocated or removed, the effect on the existing hot and cold water and heating systems is to be established and resolved before the work proceeds, and any retained cistern is to be supported on a platform to the engineer's design, insulated, and with access to the lid maintained. New and replacement wet heating systems are to be designed for a maximum flow temperature of 55°C with emitters sized accordingly. Gas boilers to have an ErP seasonal efficiency of not less than 92 per cent, a room-sealed flue with the terminal positioned to Approved Document J and the manufacturer's clearances and not less than 50mm from combustible material, inspection hatches of 300mm x 300mm at every joint and change of direction where a flue runs in a void, a carbon monoxide alarm to BS EN 50291 between 1m and 3m from the appliance, and, for a combination boiler, one of the additional efficiency measures in the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide (flue gas heat recovery, weather or load compensation, or a smart thermostat with automation and optimisation). Controls to Approved Document L Volume 1 Section 5: time and temperature control of every heating zone, two zones where the dwelling exceeds 150m², thermostatic radiator valves, a cylinder thermostat and time control for hot water independent of the heating, and a boiler interlock; primary and accessible pipework, and all pipework in the eaves voids and other unheated spaces, insulated to Table 4.4 of Approved Document L Volume 1 and the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide.",
    "Heat pumps to MCS MIS 3005 with the heat loss calculation, emitter and cylinder sizing and the outdoor unit sited to the MCS 020 noise limit at the nearest neighbour's window; any back-up heater interlocked so it never leads. Oil-fired appliances installed by an OFTEC registered technician with the oil tank not less than 1.8m from the building, 760mm from the boundary, on a fire-resisting base extending 300mm beyond the tank, bunded to 110 per cent where there is a pollution risk, and fitted with a fire valve. Electric storage heaters with automatic charge control and panel heaters with time and temperature control in each room; electric underfloor heating on a dedicated circuit with 30mA RCD protection and a floor sensor thermostat to each zone, with continuity and insulation tests recorded before the floor is covered.",
    "Wet underfloor heating designed to BS EN 1264 with the floor surface temperature limited to 29°C (27 with resilient finishes), insulation beneath the pipes of not less than ten times the resistance of the floor finish (R 0.75 m²K/W minimum on intermediate floors), 25mm edge insulation, a 65mm minimum screed over the pipes with movement joints at doorways, zone boundaries and bays over 40m² or 8m long, the system pressure tested before the screed is placed and commissioned to BS EN 1264-4 with the manifold zones labelled. Hot water storage to Approved Document G3 with a thermostat and a non-self-resetting energy cut-out, the cylinder capable of being stored at not less than 60°C to control legionella; unvented hot water storage to BS EN 12897 installed by a person registered with a competent person scheme for unvented hot water, notified to building control, with the temperature and pressure relief valve, expansion vessel and non-self-resetting thermal cut-out, a tundish within 500mm of the valve, the discharge pipe one size larger than the valve outlet routed as D1/D2 to a safe visible termination, and a warning label; hot water to every new bath limited to not more than 48°C by a thermostatic mixing valve to BS EN 1111 or BS EN 1287 fitted as close to the bath as practicable and set so that it cannot readily be altered by the building user (Approved Document G3). Every new fixed building service to meet the minimum efficiency standards in Approved Document L Volume 1 and the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide, and the commissioning certificate and the Benchmark record for every appliance to be given to building control and the occupier."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Chimneys, Flues, Hearths and Stoves (Part J)", opt:1, p:[
    "Any new chimney, flue, hearth or solid fuel appliance is to comply with Approved Document J. Existing chimneys passing through the new storey are to be kept in use or capped and ventilated as the drawings, with new floor joists, trimmers and studs kept not less than 40mm from the outer face of the chimney and 200mm from the inner face of any flue, and no flue that remains in use is to be built over or enclosed without the access and clearances Approved Document J requires; where a chimney breast is removed the remaining stack is to be supported to the structural engineer's design. A masonry chimney to have a lined flue to BS EN 1457 or a flexible or rigid metal liner to BS EN 1856 of the diameter the appliance requires (not less than 150mm for a closed appliance up to 30kW, 200mm for an open fire), backfilled with insulating concrete, with not more than two offsets at not more than 45 degrees, combustible material kept 200mm from the inner face of the flue and 40mm from the outer face of the chimney, and the chimney height not more than 4.5 times its least width unless designed. Flue outlets to terminate not less than 600mm above the ridge where within 600mm of it, 2.3m horizontally from and 1m above any adjacent roof surface or opening, and clear of the boundary as Diagram 3.4. Single-wall flue pipes for stoves to be kept three times their diameter from combustibles, or 1.5 times with a shield, and to have 300mm x 300mm access panels where they pass through voids.",
    "Hearths of 125mm concrete extending not less than 500mm in front of and 150mm each side of a fireplace opening, or a superimposed hearth of not less than 12mm for a stove whose base does not exceed 100°C, with the hearth edge clearly apparent; fireplace recess walls 200mm thick at the sides where separating dwellings and 100mm at the back. A permanently open air supply of 550mm² for each kilowatt over 5kW for a stove, more where an extract fan is in the same room (a fan is not to be fitted in the room of an open-flued appliance without a spillage test), a register plate where a liner enters an existing chimney, a carbon monoxide alarm to BS EN 50291 in the room, and a notice plate fixed beside the meter or consumer unit recording the flue and hearth and the appliance they suit. Installation by a HETAS or equivalent registered installer who notifies the work, or by building notice with a smoke and spillage test on completion, and the Approved Document J Appendix A checklist completed."]},
 ]},

flat: { name:"Flat Conversion", region:"England",
 cats:["Applicability","Planning","Consultations & Party Wall","General Notes","Site Preparation & Moisture","Structure","Separating Walls","Separating Floors","Ground Floors","External Walls, Internal Walls & Roofs","Internal Works","Sound Insulation (Part E)","Means of Escape & Fire Regs","Windows & External Doors","Ventilation","Condensation (C2)","Drainage & Waste","Water & Sanitary","Services","Security (Part Q)","EV Charging (Part S)","Conservation of Fuel & Power","Extra Notes & External Works"],
 buildups:[
  {g:"SW", c:"Separating Walls", t:"Separating Wall — Independent Lining to Existing Masonry", u:"43 dB",
   tgt:"To achieve airborne sound insulation of not less than 43 dB DnT,w + Ctr",
   p:[
    "Where an existing masonry wall is to become a separating wall between flats, retain the existing masonry and add an independent lining to one or both faces as required to achieve the performance standard. The existing masonry, drawn here as a 215mm solid brick wall and to be confirmed on site, is lined with a free-standing frame set clear of it with a minimum 25mm gap: 70mm metal C studs or 89mm x 38mm timber studs, not fixed to the masonry other than at head and base.",
    "Fill the frame depth with 60mm mineral wool of minimum density 10 kg/m³ in the frame and line with two layers of 12.5mm plasterboard of minimum mass 10 kg/m², joints staggered, taped and filled, with all perimeters sealed with an acoustic sealant.",
    "Where an existing timber stud or lath and plaster partition is to become a separating wall to the common hall or the maisonette stair, it is to be upgraded on the flat side with two layers of 15mm fire-resisting board on resilient bars over 50mm mineral wool, or replaced by the new twin frame wall, so that the wall gives the compartment period of fire resistance and the 43 dB standard; a single layer of plasterboard on an existing stud wall meets neither. The existing masonry is to be inspected and any open perpends, missing mortar or air paths made good before lining. Where the existing wall is of unknown construction, open up and confirm before the design is finalised.",
    "Piped services are not to pass through the separating wall except where unavoidable, and then each pipe is enclosed for its full height in a duct or boxing lined on both faces as Approved Document E describes, the space around the pipe fire stopped and sealed on both sides with a flexible sealant, and no socket, switch or recess cut back to back with one on the other side; soil stacks and rising mains are to be kept off the separating wall wherever the layout allows."]},
  {g:"SW", c:"Separating Walls", t:"Separating Wall — New Twin Independent Frame", u:"43 dB",
   tgt:"To achieve airborne sound insulation of not less than 43 dB DnT,w + Ctr",
   p:[
    "Where a new separating wall is formed, construct as two independent frames of 70mm metal C studs or 89mm x 38mm timber studs, each with its own head and base track and no rigid connection between the two leaves, to a minimum 200mm overall thickness, 60mm mineral wool of minimum density 10 kg/m³ in each frame and a 50mm minimum gap between the frames, each leaf lined with two layers of 12.5mm plasterboard.",
    "Where floor joists run into the line of the wall, the wall is to be built off a continuous support and the joists are not to be continuous through the wall. All perimeters and service penetrations to be sealed."]},
  {g:"SF", c:"Separating Floors", t:"Separating Floor — Platform Floor with Independent Ceiling", u:"43 / 64 dB",
   tgt:"Airborne not less than 43 dB DnT,w + Ctr; impact not more than 64 dB L'nT,w",
   p:[
    "Existing timber floor to be upgraded to comprise, from the top: a platform floating floor of 18mm tongued and grooved board on 19mm plank flooring, laid on a resilient layer of 25mm mineral wool board, isolated from all abutting walls by a resilient flanking strip carried up behind the skirting; the existing joists, drawn as 47mm x 220mm joists at 400mm centres and to be confirmed on site, with the void between them filled with 100mm mineral wool of minimum density 10 kg/m³; and an independent ceiling below.",
    "Independent ceiling to be supported on resilient bars fixed to the underside of the joists, or on an independent joist system carried on the walls and not in contact with the existing floor joists, lined with two layers of 12.5mm plasterboard of minimum total mass 20 kg/m², joints staggered, taped, filled and sealed at all perimeters.",
    "Where the floor is a compartment floor requiring 60 minutes fire resistance (top storey more than 5m above ground), the independent ceiling is to be a tested 60 minute system of two layers of 15mm fire-resisting board (which also exceeds the 20 kg/m² acoustic mass) with the joists protected as the test requires, so that one build-up satisfies both Part B and Part E; the 2 x 12.5mm ceiling is sufficient only where 30 minutes applies. No rigid connection is to be made between the floating floor and the structural floor, or between the ceiling and the joists other than through the resilient bars. Downlighters are not to be installed in the separating ceiling; where lighting is required it is to be surface mounted or installed within a lowered secondary ceiling below the separating construction."]},
  {g:"SF", c:"Separating Floors", t:"Separating Floor — Independent Ceiling Only", u:"43 / 64 dB",
   tgt:"Airborne not less than 43 dB DnT,w + Ctr; impact not more than 64 dB L'nT,w",
   p:[
    "Where floor level cannot be raised, upgrade from below only: fill the joist void with 100mm mineral wool of minimum density 10 kg/m³ between the existing joists, drawn here as 47mm x 220mm joists at 400mm centres and to be confirmed on site, and provide an independent ceiling of two layers of 12.5mm plasterboard as the platform floor build-up, with the maximum practicable void between the ceiling and the underside of the joists.",
    "A ceiling-only treatment is less effective against impact sound than a platform floor and is more likely to fail the impact test. Where this build-up is adopted, a soft floor covering to the flat above is to be specified and recorded, and the risk is to be raised with the client before construction.",
    "NOTE — Impact sound is the usual reason a conversion fails its test. Where headroom allows the platform floor, specify it. Where it does not, price the consequence of a failed test into the programme rather than assuming a ceiling-only treatment will pass."]},
  {g:"SF", c:"Separating Floors", t:"Separating Floor — Existing Concrete Floor with Floating Floor", u:"43 / 64 dB",
   tgt:"Airborne not less than 43 dB DnT,w + Ctr; impact not more than 64 dB L'nT,w",
   p:[
    "Where the existing separating floor is a concrete slab, hollow pot or beam and block floor, its mass will normally satisfy the airborne standard and the treatment is for impact sound. The detail is drawn on a 150mm concrete slab, whose thickness and construction are to be confirmed on site. Lay a resilient layer of 10mm or more closed-cell foam or 25mm mineral wool board with the edges turned up at every wall, and over it a 65mm sand and cement screed reinforced with mesh, isolated from every wall and from the skirting by a flanking strip. As an alternative to the screed a floating timber deck of 18mm tongued and grooved chipboard glued at the joints on 19mm plasterboard plank may be used, isolated in the same way. No screed, batten, pipe or threshold is to bridge the resilient layer.",
    "Where the soffit is to be lined, a plasterboard ceiling on resilient bars or a metal furring system with mineral wool in the void improves airborne performance; a plasterboard ceiling fixed direct to the soffit adds little. The existing floor is to be inspected for cracks, service holes and missing grout, and every hole through the slab made good in full with concrete or a fire-stopping mortar before the resilient layer is laid.",
    "NOTE — A concrete floor that fails the impact test has almost always been bridged at the edges. The flanking strip is to stand proud of the finished screed and be trimmed after the skirting is fixed, not before the screed is poured."]},
  {g:"GF", c:"Ground Floors", t:"Existing Solid Ground Floor — Insulation Over Slab", u:"0.17 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.25 W/m²K for a renovated floor (0.18 W/m²K where the floor is renewed)", mf:{"k":"floorSolid","lim":0.25,"p":{"ground":"clay","insulation":"k103","thickness":80,"slab":"c100","finish":"chip22","edge":"e25x150","pa":0.6,"wall":300},"s":[{"r":"floor","f":"80mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Where the ground floor flat has an existing solid floor and the floor is renovated as part of the works, or where the existing floor has no damp proof membrane, provide a polyethylene damp proof membrane of not less than 1200 gauge over the cleaned existing slab, drawn here as 100mm concrete and to be confirmed on site, lapped and taped and linked to the wall damp proof course, 80mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) tightly butted with 25mm perimeter upstands, a vapour control layer, and 22mm tongued and grooved P5 chipboard as a floating floor with a 10mm perimeter gap, or a 65mm screed where headroom allows and the slab will carry it.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a floor with a perimeter/area ratio of 0.6 on clay, typical of a terraced or semi-detached footprint. Where headroom is critical, 60mm calculates at 0.21 and still meets the renovated-floor standard. Confirm the finished floor level against door thresholds, the stair and the front entrance before the thickness is fixed.",
    "NOTE — Renovating more than 50 per cent of the floor's surface, or more than 25 per cent of the whole building envelope, brings Regulation 23 into play and the floor must then be upgraded to the standard above where technically and economically feasible. Relaying a floor without insulation because the levels are awkward is not a feasibility argument building control will accept for a flat conversion."]},
  {g:"GF", c:"Ground Floors", t:"Existing Suspended Timber Ground Floor — Insulated Between Joists", u:"0.17 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.25 W/m²K for a renovated floor", mf:{"k":"floorSusp","lim":0.25,"p":{"ground":"clay","insulation":"k103","thickness":150,"deck":"timber","joistDepth":150,"spacing":400,"pa":0.6,"wall":300,"vent":1500},"s":[{"r":"floor","f":"150mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Where the ground floor flat has a suspended timber floor, lift the boards, inspect the joists, wall plates and sleeper walls for rot and beetle and treat or replace as the engineer directs, and clear the oversite of debris. Over the ventilated void beneath, drawn here as a 150mm clear ventilated void and to be confirmed on site, fit 150mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) tightly between the joists, drawn here as 47mm x 150mm joists at 400mm centres and to be confirmed on site, on treated battens or breathable netting stapled to the joist sides so the board is flush with the top of the joists, seal the joints with foil tape, lay a breathable membrane or vapour control layer over the joists lapped up the walls behind the skirting, and relay the boards or a new 22mm P5 chipboard deck.",
    "The void beneath is to remain ventilated by air bricks on opposite walls giving not less than 1500mm² per metre run, cleared and added to where blocked, and the insulation is not to be pushed down to obstruct the air path. This build-up calculates at 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a perimeter/area ratio of 0.6 on clay at 400mm joist centres, allowing for the joists and the ventilated void; 100mm calculates at 0.23 and also meets the renovated-floor standard."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls, Internal Walls & Roofs", t:"Existing External Wall — Internally Insulated", u:"0.26 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve the standard for a retained thermal element in Approved Document L, Volume 1", mf:{"k":"lined","lim":0.3,"p":{"wall":"brick215","gap":10,"lining":"k118_725","gapLevel":0},"s":[{"r":"lining","f":"72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Where an existing external wall is retained and upgraded, provide 72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) to the internal face of the existing wall, shown here on a 215mm solid brick wall, or 62.5mm to an uninsulated cavity wall, on dabs or on treated battens, with all joints tightly butted and taped and a continuous vapour control layer where the board does not incorporate one.",
    "The existing wall is to be inspected for damp, defective pointing and cracked render before lining, and any defect made good. Internal insulation increases the risk of interstitial condensation within the existing masonry.",
    "The 72.5mm board on a 215mm solid brick wall calculates at 0.26 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 and the 62.5mm board on an uninsulated cavity wall at 0.29, both meeting the 0.30 standard for a retained wall.",
    "NOTE — A retained wall with a U-value worse than the threshold of 0.70 W/m²K must be improved to 0.30 (0.55 where a cavity wall is upgraded by cavity fill alone) under Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.3 where technically, functionally and economically feasible."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls, Internal Walls & Roofs", t:"Existing External Wall — External Wall Insulation", u:"0.27 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve the standard for a retained thermal element in Approved Document L, Volume 1 (0.30 W/m²K)",
   p:[
    "Where the elevation is not of architectural or planning value and internal space is at a premium, insulate the existing solid wall externally with 100mm expanded polystyrene of 0.032 W/mK or 100mm mineral wool slab of 0.036 W/mK (0.30) mechanically fixed and adhered to the prepared masonry, drawn here as a 215mm solid brick wall and to be confirmed on site, a base coat with alkali-resistant mesh, and a through-coloured silicone render finish, all as a complete system with a BBA certificate installed by the system holder's approved contractor. Starter track and drip at the base above the damp proof course, stainless steel beads at corners and openings, window sills extended to give a projecting drip over the new face, and the eaves, verges, rainwater goods and soil pipes extended or re-fixed to clear the new thickness.",
    "The 100mm expanded polystyrene build-up on a 215mm solid brick wall calculates at 0.27 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946; the mineral wool alternative calculates at 0.30 and meets the standard with no margin. Where the building has a storey more than 11m above ground, or where the wall is within 1m of the boundary, the insulant is to be mineral wool or another material of limited combustibility. External wall insulation is development requiring planning permission on most elevations and is not to be specified without the permission or a written confirmation that it is permitted.",
    "NOTE — External insulation keeps the existing wall warm and dry and avoids the interstitial condensation risk of an internal lining, but it changes the appearance of the building and the junctions at the eaves, windows and party wall have to be drawn. Where only one flat's wall is insulated the party wall line is a cold bridge and a return of insulation onto the neighbour's wall needs their agreement."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls, Internal Walls & Roofs", t:"Existing Party Wall to the Adjoining Property", u:"0.26 W/m²K / REI 60",
   tgt:"To maintain the separating wall to the adjoining building as a compartment wall with 60 minutes fire resistance, and to insulate it where the adjoining space is unheated", mf:{"k":"lined","lim":0.3,"p":{"wall":"brick215","internalBoth":true,"gap":10,"lining":"k118_725","gapLevel":0},"s":[{"r":"lining","f":"72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "The existing party wall to the neighbouring house is a compartment wall separating two buildings and is to be carried up to the underside of the roof covering and fire stopped, with any gap, missing brickwork, open perpend, redundant flue or timber built into the wall made good in masonry so the wall gives 60 minutes fire resistance and no air path exists to the neighbour. Sockets and recesses are not to be cut into it back to back and no beam is to bear on it without the fire resistance being reinstated around the bearing.",
    "Where the space on the other side is unheated, for example a neighbour's unconverted loft or a garage, the wall is a thermal element to an unheated space and is to be lined with 72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard on dabs or battens to the existing wall, drawn here as a 215mm solid brick wall. That calculates at 0.26 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 with both faces treated as internal surfaces; where the adjoining rooms are heated no insulation is required. An independent lining on a metal frame with mineral wool infill, as the separating wall build-up, is recommended where the neighbour is a different household and the wall is a single-leaf 100mm wall, and is a requirement where the conversion makes the wall a separating wall between dwellings that did not previously exist.",
    "Work to the party wall is notifiable under the Party Wall etc. Act 1996 as the Consultations note."]},
  {g:"IW", c:"External Walls, Internal Walls & Roofs", t:"Internal Partition Within a Flat", u:"",
   tgt:"",
   p:[
    "Internal stud partition to comprise 89mm x 38mm C16 studs at 400mm centres with head and sole plates and solid noggins at board joints and at all fixings. Infill the full stud depth with 89mm acoustic mineral wool and line both faces with 12.5mm plasterboard, joints staggered and all perimeters sealed. Walls between a bedroom or a room containing a WC and other rooms within the same flat are to satisfy Requirement E2."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"External Walls, Internal Walls & Roofs", t:"Existing Flat Roof — Warm Deck Overlay", u:"0.15 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.16 W/m²K for a renovated roof (Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.3)", mf:{"k":"flat","lim":0.16,"p":{"insulation":"tr26","thickness":130,"joistDepth":150,"spacing":400,"fixing":"adhered"},"s":[{"r":"flat","f":"130mm Kingspan Thermaroof TR26 LPC/FM (thermal conductivity 0.022 W/mK)"}]}, p:[
    "Where an existing flat roof over a flat is re-covered, strip the existing covering, replace any soft or delaminated decking over the existing joists, drawn here as 47mm x 200mm joists at 400mm centres and to be confirmed on site, with 18mm exterior grade plywood or OSB3 laid to falls, lay a vapour control layer fully bonded to the deck with laps sealed, 130mm Kingspan Thermaroof TR26 LPC/FM (thermal conductivity 0.022 W/mK) fully adhered, and a single ply or high performance built-up felt waterproofing system to the manufacturer's specification and BBA certificate with 150mm upstands and new edge trims and outlets. Existing soffit vents to the joist void are to be sealed once the warm deck is complete.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.15 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 fully adhered on 150mm joists; 120mm calculates at 0.17 and does not meet the 0.16 renovation standard. Where the roof is a separating element between a top-floor flat and the open air only, no sound requirement applies, but where a roof terrace is formed over another flat the floor beneath the terrace is a separating floor and is to be treated as one."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"External Walls, Internal Walls & Roofs", t:"Existing Pitched Roof — Insulation at Rafter Level (Room in Roof)", u:"0.15 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.16 W/m²K for a renovated roof (0.15 where the roof is renewed)", mf:{"k":"rafter","lim":0.16,"p":{"insulation":"k107","thickness":125,"rafterDepth":150,"spacing":400,"under":"k118_625","gapLevel":1},"s":[{"r":"rafter","f":"125mm Kingspan Kooltherm K107 Pitched Roof Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK)"},{"r":"lining","f":"62.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard"}]}, p:[
    "Where a top-floor flat occupies the roof space, or the ceiling follows the roof slope, insulate at rafter level: 125mm Kingspan Kooltherm K107 Pitched Roof Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) fitted tightly between rafters deepened to 150mm where necessary so that a 50mm ventilated gap remains above the board where the underlay is not breathable (BS 5250), and 62.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard beneath the rafters with joints staggered and taped and a continuous vapour control layer. Where the roof covering is renewed at the same time, a breathable underlay to BS 5534 with counter-battens allows the rafter depth to be fully filled.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.15 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 at 400mm rafter centres allowing for rafter bridging and an air-gap correction of 0.01; with 100mm K107 filling existing 100mm rafters (breathable underlay with counter-battens only, as no ventilated gap remains) and 72.5mm K118 beneath it calculates at 0.15 also; 62.5mm beneath gives 0.17 and does not meet the 0.16 renovation standard. Ventilate any residual void above the insulation with eaves and ridge ventilation equivalent to a 25mm and 5mm continuous gap where the underlay is not breathable.",
    "NOTE — The separating wall between top-floor flats must be carried up tight to the roof covering and fire stopped, and the roof space over each flat must not communicate with the space over the next; this build-up does not remove that requirement."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"External Walls, Internal Walls & Roofs", t:"Existing Roof — Insulation at Ceiling Level", u:"0.11 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve the standard for a renovated thermal element in Approved Document L, Volume 1",
   p:[
    "Where the roof void is accessible, top up the insulation to 100mm mineral wool quilt between the ceiling joists and 300mm mineral wool quilt laid cross-wise over them, or equivalent to achieve the target U-value, with proprietary eaves ventilators and insulation stops to maintain the ventilation path. The 400mm calculates at 0.11 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 allowing for the joist fraction at 400mm centres; 300mm total calculates at 0.15, which still meets the 0.16 renovation standard. Loft hatches to be insulated and draught sealed."]}
 ],
 notes:[
  {c:"Applicability", t:"Requirements That Apply on a Material Change of Use", p:[
    "Conversion of a house into two or more self-contained flats is a material change of use under Regulation 5(b) (the building contains a flat where previously it did not) and 5(g) (a greater number of dwellings) of the Building Regulations 2010. Regulation 6 sets out the requirements the building as converted must satisfy. This list is authoritative and it is narrower than many assume.",
    "The following apply: B1 means of warning and escape; B2 internal fire spread (linings); B3 internal fire spread (structure); B4(2) external fire spread (roofs); B5 access and facilities for the fire service; C1(2) resistance to contaminants; C2(c) resistance to interstitial and surface condensation; E1 to E3 resistance to the passage of sound; F1 ventilation; G1 cold water supply; G2 water efficiency; G3(1) to (4) hot water supply, systems and hot water to fixed baths; G4, G5 and G6 sanitary conveniences, bathrooms and kitchens; H1 foul water drainage; H6 solid waste storage; J1 to J4 combustion appliances and fuel storage; L1 conservation of fuel and power; P1 electrical safety; Q1 security, which Regulation 6(1)(j) applies to a change of use under Regulation 5(a), (b) or (g) and so to a house converted into flats; and S2 infrastructure for the charging of electric vehicles, which Regulation 6(1)(a) lists for every material change of use and which is engaged where the building has associated parking. B4(1) external fire spread from walls is added by Regulation 6(1)(c) where the building has a storey more than 11m above ground level.",
    "The whole of C2 (resistance to moisture from the ground and the weather) is added by Regulation 6(1)(e) only where a building that was not a dwelling becomes one (Regulation 5(a)); on a house converted into flats the change of use brings in C2(c) alone, but any floor or wall renovated as part of the works must satisfy C2 in full as building work under Regulation 4.",
    "NOTE — Building work carried out as part of the conversion must comply with the requirements applicable to that work under Regulation 4. New structural openings engage Part A, new stairs and guarding engage Part K, and so on. The distinction is between what the change of use brings in for the whole building, and what the building work itself must satisfy."]},
  {c:"Applicability", t:"Requirements That Do Not Apply", p:[
    "The following are not brought into effect by the change of use, and are recorded here so the position is stated on the face of the document rather than argued on site.",
    "O1 overheating — applies to new residential buildings; extension to material change of use has been consulted on but is not in force. S1 electric vehicle charging for new residential buildings — not listed; the change-of-use requirement is S2, which does apply and is covered in its own section. M4 access to and use of dwellings — not listed for conversion to flats. H3 rainwater drainage — not listed; existing rainwater drainage to be retained and made good. A1 to A3 structure — not listed for a conversion to flats (they are listed for changes to hotels, institutions, public buildings and shops), although any structural work forming part of the conversion must comply with Part A under Regulation 4.",
    "NOTE — Confirm the current position with the local authority before issue; Regulation 6 is amended from time to time. Specifying a requirement that does not apply is as damaging as missing one that does."]},
  {c:"Planning", t:"Planning Status", p:[
    "Conversion of a dwellinghouse into two or more flats is not permitted development and requires planning permission. All conditions attached to the permission are to be discharged in accordance with their terms before the affected work proceeds.",
    "Where the property is listed, in a conservation area, or subject to an Article 4 Direction, separate consent is required.",
    "Building Regulations approval does not confer planning permission, and planning permission does not confer Building Regulations approval."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Regulation 16 Notices — Commencement and Completion", p:[
    "Regulation 16 of the Building Regulations 2010 (as amended in October 2023) requires three notices to the building control body: notice of intention to start work, given so that at least two clear days elapse before work begins; a commencement notice within five days of the work being regarded as commenced under Regulation 46A, which for a new building or a horizontal extension is when the sub-structure including the ground floor structure is complete and for other work (loft, garage, basement and flat conversions) is when 15 per cent of the work is complete, after which the authority has four weeks to reject the notice; and a completion notice within five days of completion. The completion notice must give the name, address, telephone number and email of the client, the principal contractor and the principal designer, state that the work is complete, include a signed statement by the client that the work complies with the Building Regulations, and include signed statements from the principal designer and principal contractor confirming that they have fulfilled their duties under Part 2A with the dates of their appointments; where a statement cannot be obtained the reason is to be given.",
    "Inspection stages to be notified as they arise: any new excavations and foundations before concreting, damp proof membranes and floor insulation before the floor is laid, structural steelwork and beams before they are cased in, separating walls and floors and fire stopping before they are closed up, insulation before it is boarded, drains before backfilling and on test, and completion; a completion certificate is to be obtained for each flat and given to the client. Certificates from competent person schemes (electrical, gas, replacement windows, heating appliances) and commissioning notices for fixed building services, ventilation and pressure tests are to be given to building control within the periods the Regulations set (normally five days) and copied to the client with the completion certificate, which purchasers' solicitors will ask for.",
    "NOTE — The two-day and five-day limbs are separate duties. Missing the commencement notice means the work is not treated as commenced for transitional purposes, which matters where an edition of the Approved Documents is about to change."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"CDM 2015 and Houses in Multiple Occupation", p:[
    "The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015 apply. Where more than one contractor is involved the client is to appoint a principal designer and a principal contractor in writing; where the client is a domestic client and no appointment is made the duties pass to the contractor or to the designer in control of the pre-construction phase. A construction phase plan is required for every project and the health and safety file for the completed building is to be handed to the client.",
    "Where any part of the building will be occupied by three or more people forming more than one household sharing a kitchen, bathroom or WC, that part is a house in multiple occupation and the local housing authority's HMO standards, the LACORS housing fire safety guidance and, for five or more occupants, mandatory licensing apply in addition to the Building Regulations. Self-contained flats are not HMOs, but a building converted to flats that does not meet the 1991 Building Regulations standard and has less than two thirds owner-occupation is a section 257 HMO and may need a licence; confirm with the housing authority."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Compliance and Scope", p:[
    "All works to be carried out in accordance with the Building Regulations 2010 (as amended) and the Approved Documents current at the date of issue. All materials and workmanship to comply with Approved Document 7 (Regulation 7).",
    "This specification is to be read in conjunction with the drawing pack issued with this specification, the structural engineer's design and calculations, the acoustic consultant's design where appointed, and any specialist sub-contractor design.",
    "All dimensions to be checked on site. Do not scale from the drawings."]},
  {c:"Site Preparation & Moisture", t:"Resistance to Contaminants, Moisture and Radon", p:[
    "Requirements C1(2) and C2(c) apply to the building as converted, and every floor or wall renovated as part of the works must satisfy C2 as building work under Regulation 4. Where the ground floor is at or below external ground level, the resistance of the floor and walls to the passage of moisture is to be assessed and remedial measures provided where necessary, including a damp proof membrane, tanking or a cavity drain system as appropriate.",
    "Existing damp proof courses are to be located and their continuity confirmed. Where bridged by raised external levels, render or debris, the bridge is to be removed.",
    "Where the site is in a radon affected area on the UK radon map, protection to BR 211 is to be built in. Basic protection is a radon-resistant membrane, of a grade certified for radon resistance (typically 1600 gauge / 400 micron polyethylene or a reinforced or aluminium-cored membrane), laid across the whole footprint in place of the standard damp proof membrane with laps of not less than 300mm sealed with gas-resistant tape, every penetration sealed with a top hat, and the membrane carried across the cavity to link with a cavity tray or the damp proof course in both leaves so the barrier is continuous; the slab is to be laid over sound compacted fill so the membrane cannot tear. Full protection, where the map shows the higher probability band, adds a radon sump beneath the slab connected to a capped pipe taken to the outside so a fan can be fitted later, or, for a suspended floor, a cross-ventilated void of not less than 150mm with the vents on opposite sides; a radon test after occupation and the fan fitted if the result is high. Where the site investigation identifies landfill gas, hydrocarbons or volatile organic compounds, gas protection is to be designed to BS 8485 and CIRIA C735 with a specialist membrane, its installation independently inspected and a verification report given to building control.",
    "NOTE — Confirm the radon affected area status for the postcode on the UK radon map before issue; the protection level follows from it."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Structural Design", p:[
    "Building work carried out as part of the conversion must comply with the requirements applicable to that work. New openings, removed walls, new beams, altered floor structures and any change to the loading of the existing structure are to be designed by the structural engineer, with calculations submitted to building control before the affected work commences.",
    "Existing floors are to be assessed for their capacity to carry the imposed loading of the proposed use and for the additional dead load of any sound insulation treatment. A platform floor and an independent ceiling together add significant load; this is to be confirmed by the engineer rather than assumed.",
    "Existing structure to be opened up as required by the Building Control Officer. Any condition differing from that assumed in the design to be reported to the engineer immediately and work in that area stopped."]},
  {c:"Internal Works", t:"Staircases, Guarding and Internal Doors", p:[
    "New stairs within a flat to Approved Document K for a private stair: pitch not more than 42 degrees, rise between 150mm and 220mm, going not less than 220mm, twice the rise plus the going between 550mm and 700mm, all rises and goings in a flight equal, headroom of 2m over the pitch line and landings, and a handrail on at least one side at 900mm to 1000mm above the pitch line, on both sides where the flight is 1m or wider, with not less than 600mm clear between handrails where two are fitted. Tapered treads to have a going of not less than 50mm at the narrow end and to satisfy the going rule at the centre line (or 270mm from the narrow end where the stair is wider than 1m); landings at the top and bottom of every flight at least as long as the flight is wide and level, with no door swinging over a landing other than a cupboard door which leaves 400mm clear; open risers not to pass a 100mm sphere; timber stairs to BS 585 with the strings, treads and risers glued and wedged and the newels bolted to the floor structure. Guarding of 900mm to every open side and landing within a flat, not climbable and not passing a 100mm sphere, designed for the horizontal load in BS EN 1991-1-1 (0.36 kN/m for a dwelling).",
    "The common stair, where existing, is to be retained where its pitch and headroom are reasonable for an existing stair and building control agrees, with the handrail and guarding brought up to 900mm on flights and 1100mm at landings; where the stair is rebuilt it is to be a common stair with a pitch not more than 38 degrees, a rise of 150mm to 170mm, a going of not less than 250mm and a clear width of not less than 1000mm.",
    "Where a stair is enclosed to form the separating construction between two flats, for example a former hall stair now serving the upper flat only, the enclosure is a separating wall for Part E and a compartment wall for Part B, and the underside of the stair is to be lined to the same fire and sound standard as the wall. Internal doors within a flat to be standard doorsets other than doors onto a protected entrance hall, which are specified with the flat entrance doorsets in the Means of Escape note. Door frames in a separating wall are to be fitted with the frame sealed to the wall and the head fire stopped."]},
  {c:"Internal Works", t:"Heating, Hot Water and Lighting Within Each Flat", p:[
    "Each flat is to have an independent heating and hot water system unless a communal system with heat metering is designed: a condensing gas combination or system boiler, a heat pump or an electric system as the energy strategy requires, with radiators sized for a flow temperature of not more than 55°C, wet underfloor heating in the screed of an insulated floor, or electric panel or underfloor heating with individual room controls; hot water by the combination boiler, an unvented or vented cylinder, or an electric water heater with the expansion and discharge arrangements the manufacturer requires. Wood burning stoves and oil-fired appliances are not to be installed in a flat without a flue, air supply and hearth designed to Approved Document J and the Building Control Officer's agreement, and open-flued appliances are not to be installed in bedrooms or bathrooms.",
    "New and replacement wet heating systems are to be designed for a maximum flow temperature of 55°C with emitters sized accordingly. Gas boilers to have an ErP seasonal efficiency of not less than 92 per cent, a room-sealed flue with the terminal positioned to Approved Document J and the manufacturer's clearances and not less than 50mm from combustible material, inspection hatches of 300mm x 300mm at every joint and change of direction where a flue runs in a void, and, for a combination boiler, one of the additional efficiency measures in the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide (flue gas heat recovery, weather or load compensation, or a smart thermostat with automation and optimisation). Controls to Approved Document L Volume 1 Section 5: time and temperature control of every heating zone, two zones where the flat exceeds 150m², thermostatic radiator valves to all radiators other than in the room with the thermostat, a cylinder thermostat and time control for hot water independent of the heating, and a boiler or heat pump interlock; primary and accessible pipework, and all pipework outside the heated envelope, insulated to Table 4.4 of Approved Document L Volume 1 and the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide.",
    "Heat pumps to MCS MIS 3005 with the heat loss calculation, emitter and cylinder sizing and the outdoor unit sited to the MCS 020 noise limit at the nearest neighbour's window; any back-up heater interlocked so it never leads. Oil-fired appliances installed by an OFTEC registered technician with the oil tank not less than 1.8m from the building, 760mm from the boundary, on a fire-resisting base extending 300mm beyond the tank, bunded to 110 per cent where there is a pollution risk, and fitted with a fire valve. Electric storage heaters with automatic charge control and panel heaters with time and temperature control in each room; electric underfloor heating on a dedicated circuit with 30mA RCD protection and a floor sensor thermostat to each zone, with continuity and insulation tests recorded before the floor is covered.",
    "Wet underfloor heating designed to BS EN 1264 with the floor surface temperature limited to 29°C (27 with resilient finishes), insulation beneath the pipes of not less than ten times the resistance of the floor finish (R 0.75 m²K/W minimum on intermediate floors), 25mm edge insulation, a 65mm minimum screed over the pipes with movement joints at doorways, zone boundaries and bays over 40m² or 8m long, the system pressure tested before the screed is placed and commissioned to BS EN 1264-4 with the manifold zones labelled. Unvented hot water storage to BS EN 12897 with the temperature and pressure relief valve, expansion vessel and non-self-resetting thermal cut-out, a tundish within 500mm of the valve, the discharge pipe one size larger than the valve outlet routed as D1/D2 to a safe visible termination, and a warning label, installed and notified as the Water & Sanitary note. The commissioning certificate and the Benchmark record for every appliance to be given to building control and the occupier.",
    "All new fixed internal lighting to have a luminous efficacy of not less than 75 lumens per circuit-watt (Approved Document L Volume 1, 2021 edition, Section 6 and the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide), with LED luminaires or lamps throughout; fittings rated at less than 5 circuit-watts are excluded from the count. Downlighters in a separating ceiling are not permitted other than as a tested fire-rated and acoustic-rated fitting within a lowered secondary ceiling below the separating construction."]},
  {c:"Sound Insulation (Part E)", t:"Performance Standards", p:[
    "Requirements E1, E2 and E3 apply. Separating walls and separating floors between flats are to achieve the performance standards for dwelling-houses and flats formed by material change of use given in Approved Document E.",
    "Separating walls: airborne sound insulation not less than 43 dB DnT,w + Ctr. Separating floors and stairs: airborne not less than 43 dB DnT,w + Ctr, and impact not more than 64 dB L'nT,w."]},
  {c:"Sound Insulation (Part E)", t:"Pre-Completion Sound Testing", p:[
    "Pre-completion sound testing is mandatory for flats formed by material change of use (Regulation 41) and is to be carried out when the separating elements, doors and windows are complete but before carpets and soft floor coverings are laid, by a tester accredited by UKAS or registered under the Association of Noise Consultants scheme, at the rate of not less than one set of tests for every ten dwellings of each construction type (a set being two airborne wall tests, two airborne floor tests and two impact floor tests where both elements exist). Testing is to be arranged in good time before completion and the results submitted to building control; a failed test is to be followed by remedial work agreed with the tester and a retest of the failed element and any others building control requires.",
    "Robust Details are not available for conversions. The exemption from pre-completion testing applies only to new dwelling-houses and buildings containing newly constructed flats. Any programme or fee proposal that assumes a Robust Details route on a conversion is wrong.",
    "NOTE — This is the most commonly misunderstood point on flat conversions and it has programme and cost consequences. Build testing into the programme from the outset, and tell the client at fee stage that a failed test means remedial work and a retest."]},
  {c:"Sound Insulation (Part E)", t:"Flanking Transmission", p:[
    "Flanking transmission through the surrounding construction will defeat an otherwise adequate separating element. Junctions between separating walls and floors, external walls, internal walls and ceilings are to be detailed to interrupt the flanking path.",
    "Existing floor joists are not to run continuously through a separating wall; where they do, they are to be cut and supported independently either side.",
    "All service penetrations, sockets, switches and recessed fittings in separating construction are to be sealed and are not to be positioned back to back. Pipework and ductwork crossing a separating element is to be resiliently sleeved and boxed with a lined casing.",
    "Existing chimney breasts, flues and voids within separating walls are to be identified, sealed and, where redundant, filled. Open flues are a direct airborne sound path between flats."]},
  {c:"Sound Insulation (Part E)", t:"Requirement E2 — Internal Walls and Floors", p:[
    "New internal walls between a bedroom or a room containing a WC and other rooms within the same flat, and new internal floors within a flat, are to achieve a minimum laboratory sound reduction of 40 dB Rw in accordance with Requirement E2."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Compartmentation and Periods of Fire Resistance", p:[
    "Each flat is to be a separate compartment. For a building containing flats the period of fire resistance for the compartment walls and floors between flats and between flats and the common areas, and for the elements of structure, is set by Approved Document B Volume 1 Table B4 from the height of the top storey above the lowest adjoining ground: 30 minutes (REI 30) where the top storey is not more than 5m above ground (a two-storey house converted to two flats), and 60 minutes (REI 60) where it is more than 5m and not more than 18m (every three-storey house and most four-storey houses). The separating wall to a neighbouring building is 60 minutes in both cases. Flat entrance doorsets are specified in the Means of Escape note.",
    "The height is measured from the lowest ground level adjoining the building to the top floor surface of the highest storey containing a flat; a three-storey house on a sloping site is measured from the low side. Where the existing floor is a timber floor to be upgraded to 60 minutes, the ceiling is to be a tested 60 minute system, normally two layers of 15mm fire-resisting board on resilient bars or an independent ceiling, and the floor deck above may need a layer of board to protect the joists from a fire in the flat above.",
    "Compartment walls are to be carried full height to the underside of the roof covering, or to the underside of a fire resisting ceiling where that arrangement is adopted, and fire stopped at the junction. All openings and service penetrations through compartment construction are to be fire stopped with a proprietary system tested for the substrate and service concerned.",
    "NOTE — Confirm the required period from Table B4 for the measured height of the top storey before the separating elements are detailed. Do not assume 30 or 60 minutes: the difference changes the construction of every separating element in the building."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Means of Escape", p:[
    "Means of escape to comply with Approved Document B, Volume 1, Section 3. The common escape route is to be enclosed in fire resisting construction throughout, discharging to a final exit, with an FD30S self-closing doorset to each flat entrance as the Flat Entrance Doors note.",
    "Where a flat is arranged so that escape is through a protected entrance hall, that hall is to be enclosed in fire resisting construction and all doors from habitable rooms onto it are to be FD30S fire doorsets (E 30 Sa, with intumescent strips and cold smoke seals) fitted with a self-closing device, adopted as the practice standard in place of the E 20 (FD20) minimum in Approved Document B Volume 1. Travel distances within each flat and within the common areas are to be checked against Approved Document B and shown on the fire plans.",
    "Where full compliance with the escape provisions cannot be achieved within the constraints of the existing building, an alternative approach — a residential sprinkler or water mist system designed and installed to BS 9251, or a fire engineered solution — is to be agreed with building control at design stage and before the drawings are finalised.",
    "NOTE — On a conversion of an existing house the escape strategy is the item most likely to drive the whole design. Establish it with the Building Control Officer before the layouts are fixed, not after. Retrofitting a protected route into a plan drawn without one usually costs a unit."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Fire Detection and Alarm", p:[
    "Each flat is to be provided with a fire detection and alarm system to BS 5839-6 of not less than Grade D1 Category LD2 — mains powered, interlinked smoke alarms to BS EN 14604 with an integral tamper-proof standby supply — in the entrance hall and every circulation space, in the living room and in any room in which a fire could start that opens onto the escape route, with a heat alarm to BS 5446-2 in the kitchen. This exceeds the Grade D2 Category LD3 minimum given in Approved Document B Volume 1 (paragraphs 1.1 and 1.10) and is adopted as this practice's standard for every flat formed by conversion. A studio flat with the cooking area in the room is to have a heat alarm in the cooking area and a smoke alarm in the sleeping area. A carbon monoxide alarm to BS EN 50291 is to be fitted in every room containing a fixed combustion appliance, 1m to 3m from the appliance.",
    "Alarms to be ceiling mounted not less than 300mm from any wall or light fitting, within 7.5m of the door to every habitable room and within 3m of every bedroom door, with no point in a hall more than 7.5m from an alarm, and supplied from an independent circuit at the flat's consumer unit or from a regularly used local lighting circuit as BS 5839-6 permits for Grade D1.",
    "A communal alarm is not normally provided in a stay-put building; where building control requires detection in the common areas it is to be designed to BS 5839-1 and is not to sound in the flats. A design, installation and commissioning certificate for every system is to be issued to building control on completion."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Common Stair, Smoke Ventilation and Emergency Lighting", p:[
    "The common stair is to be a protected stairway in fire-resisting construction of the compartment period, with no cupboards, meters, gas pipes or combustible storage within it, FD30S self-closing doors from every flat and from any lobby, and a final exit door opening directly to the outside. Where the stair serves a small building of not more than three storeys with the top storey not more than 11m above ground, the flats may open directly onto the stair provided every flat entrance door is FD30S and the stair has an openable or automatic vent of not less than 1m² at its head for smoke clearance; otherwise a lobby or corridor with its own vent is to be provided between the flat entrance doors and the stair.",
    "Emergency escape lighting to BS 5266-1 is to be provided to the common stair and any common corridor where the route is not lit by borrowed light, and in every case where the building has a floor more than 4.5m above ground or the stair has no external window; fittings to give three hours' duration and be tested monthly and annually with the results recorded. Exit signs to BS ISO 3864 where the way out is not obvious. A maisonette with its own internal stair and a floor more than 4.5m above ground is to have a protected internal stair with fire doors to the rooms off it, or an alternative exit from the upper floor, as Approved Document B Volume 1 Section 3."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Linings, Roofs and Fire Service Access", p:[
    "Wall and ceiling linings to achieve the classification required by Approved Document B for the room or circulation space concerned, with the higher standard applied to the common escape route.",
    "Requirement B4(2) applies: the roof covering is to meet the external fire spread classification for its distance from the boundary as the External Fire Spread note.",
    "Access for a fire service pumping appliance is to be provided in accordance with Approved Document B5. Where the arrangement cannot be achieved it is to be agreed with building control before submission."]},
  {c:"Ventilation", t:"Ventilation — Approved Document F", p:[
    "Requirement F1 applies to the building as converted and each flat is to be provided with ventilation in accordance with Approved Document F Volume 1. Intermittent mechanical extract ventilation to BS EN 13141-4 at not less than 30 litres per second from a cooker hood over the hob (fitted 650mm to 750mm above it, to BS EN 13141-3) or 60 litres per second elsewhere in the kitchen, 30 litres per second from a utility room, 15 litres per second from a bathroom and 6 litres per second from a WC. Every extract fan is to discharge directly to the outside air, not into a roof void or a cavity. Fans may be wired to the light switch and are to have a 15 minute overrun where the room has no openable window. Ductwork rigid wherever possible with flexible duct limited to 1.5m at final connections and run straight, installed to BSRIA BG 43, insulated where it passes through unheated voids and discharging directly to outside air through a terminal with a back-draught shutter. Ducts serving one flat are not to pass through another flat or the common stair unless enclosed in fire-resisting construction of the compartment period, and are to be fitted with fire dampers where they pass through compartment construction.",
    "Background ventilators for natural ventilation (System 1) to give an equivalent area of not less than 10,000mm² in every habitable room and kitchen of a single-storey flat, 8000mm² in a maisonette on two or more storeys, and 4000mm² in every bathroom, with not fewer than four ventilators in a one-bedroom flat and five otherwise, set not less than 1700mm above floor level (trickle ventilators in the window heads ordered as part of the window units), with the equivalent area marked or recorded and noise-attenuating ventilators used on facades exposed to traffic or other noise. Purge ventilation to every habitable room by an openable area of not less than one twentieth of the floor area where the window opens more than 30 degrees, or one tenth where it opens between 15 and 30 degrees, or a mechanical system giving four air changes per hour. Internal doors undercut 10mm above the floor finish (7600mm² for a 760mm door).",
    "Where a flat is single-aspect, has a design air permeability below 5 m³/(h·m²), or has a room with no external wall, intermittent extract with background ventilators will not achieve the whole-dwelling rate and continuous mechanical extract (System 3) is to be used with 4000mm² background ventilators to each habitable room and not fewer than the number of bedrooms plus two, or mechanical ventilation with heat recovery (System 4) with no background ventilators, supply terminals placed to avoid draughts and wet-room air not recirculated; continuous rates not less than 13 litres per second from the kitchen, 8 from each bathroom and utility room and 6 from a WC, and the whole-dwelling rate the greater of 0.3 litres per second per square metre of floor area and the bedroom-count rate (19, 25, 31, 37 and 43 litres per second for one to five bedrooms), with the unit accessible for filter changes from within the flat. Every fixed mechanical ventilation system to be commissioned with the flow rates measured to BS EN 13141-6 or 13141-7 and the results given to building control within five days of the final test and to the occupier in the Home User Guide.",
    "NOTE — Check the purge openable areas against the window schedule and the trickle ventilator equivalent areas against the window order before issue."]},
  {c:"Condensation (C2)", t:"Surface and Interstitial Condensation", p:[
    "Requirement C2 applies. Reasonable precautions are to be taken to prevent surface and interstitial condensation, in accordance with Approved Document C2 and BS 5250.",
    "Where an existing external wall is insulated internally, the risk of interstitial condensation within the existing masonry is increased. The external face is to be maintained weathertight, rainwater goods checked and repaired, and a condensation risk analysis carried out where the wall is exposed or of solid construction.",
    "Provide a continuous vapour control layer on the warm side of insulation, sealed at laps, junctions and service penetrations. Ventilate all roof voids. Extract ducts passing through unheated voids to be insulated."]},
  {c:"Drainage & Waste", t:"Rainwater and Drains Under the Building", p:[
    "Existing rainwater goods to be cleared, checked for falls and repaired, with any new downpipe to a new roof or terrace connected to the surface water or combined drain through a trapped gully and never to the foul drain; where a connection is not available a soakaway to BRE Digest 365 not less than 5m from the building is to be provided. Rainwater is not to discharge onto a neighbour's land.",
    "Existing drains passing beneath the building are to be located by a CCTV survey before the ground floor is renovated, and any drain under a new slab, new foundation or new internal wall is to be protected as the drainage detail note below, with rodding access maintained at both ends. Defective drains found by the survey are to be repaired or relined before the floor is laid."]},
  {c:"Drainage & Waste", t:"Foul Drainage and Solid Waste", p:[
    "Requirement H1 applies. Existing drainage is to be surveyed and its capacity and condition confirmed as adequate for the number of dwellings proposed. New branches and stacks to comply with Approved Document H1, with all appliances trapped and rodding access provided.",
    "Soil and vent pipes to terminate not less than 900mm above any opening into the building within 3m, with air admittance valves only as the drainage detail note below. Stacks passing through compartment construction are to be fire stopped.",
    "Requirement H6 applies: adequate provision is to be made for the storage of solid waste and for access from the flats to the storage point and from the storage point to the collection point. Capacity and location to be agreed with the local authority's waste collection service.",
    "NOTE — Solid waste storage is easy to overlook on a conversion and it is expressly brought in by Regulation 6. Show the bin store on the site plan with the capacity stated, and confirm the arrangement with the collection authority before submission."]},
  {c:"Water & Sanitary", t:"Water Supply, Water Efficiency and Hot Water Safety (Part G)", p:[
    "Requirements G1, G2, G3, G4, G5 and G6 apply. Each flat is to be provided with a wholesome cold water supply, sanitary conveniences and washing facilities, a bathroom and a kitchen or food preparation area in accordance with Approved Document G, with a wash hand basin in or adjacent to every room containing a WC and all new fittings and pipework to the Water Supply (Water Fittings) Regulations 1999.",
    "Requirement G2 applies: the whole dwelling potable water consumption of each flat is not to exceed 125 litres per person per day (Regulation 36), calculated by the Approved Document G methodology or the fittings approach, with the calculation submitted to building control and the fittings specified accordingly.",
    "Hot water storage vessels to Approved Document G3: the cylinder to be capable of being stored at not less than 60°C to control legionella, with a thermostat and a non-self-resetting energy cut-out so that the stored water cannot exceed 100°C, vented cylinders with an open vent pipe of not less than 19mm bore over the cold water cistern, and the vessel marked with its installer, capacity, pressure and the location of the discharge. Unvented systems to BS EN 12897 installed by a person registered with a competent person scheme for unvented hot water and notified to building control. The hot water supplied to every bath is to be limited to not more than 48°C by a thermostatic mixing valve to BS EN 1111 or BS EN 1287 fitted as close to the bath as practicable and set so that it cannot readily be altered by the building user (Requirement G3(4), which Regulation 6(1)(h) applies to a house converted into flats).",
    "NOTE — The optional tighter limit of 110 litres per person per day applies only where the planning permission imposes it by condition. Check the permission before calculating.",
    "Waste water heat recovery: where the SAP assessment relies on it, or the client wants it, a waste water heat recovery system is to be fitted to the shower waste — a vertical or horizontal heat exchanger that passes the warm drain water past the incoming cold feed to the shower mixer, the combination boiler or the cylinder — as a product listed in the SAP Product Characteristics Database so that its saving can be claimed, installed with the connections, falls and access for cleaning the manufacturer requires, shown on the drainage drawings, and recorded in the as-built SAP and in the information handed to the occupier."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Combustion Appliances and Electrical Installation", p:[
    "Requirements J1 to J4 apply. Combustion appliances, flues, hearths and fuel storage to comply with Approved Document J. Existing flues serving retained appliances are to be tested and swept, and flues shared between flats are not permitted. All gas work to be carried out by a Gas Safe registered engineer.",
    "Requirement P1 applies. All electrical work to comply with Approved Document P and BS 7671, designed, installed, inspected and tested by a competent person registered with an approved Part P scheme, with separate metered supplies and consumer units to each flat. Certification to be issued to building control on completion."]},
  {c:"Consultations & Party Wall", t:"Consultations Before Work Starts", p:[
    "Party Wall etc. Act 1996: works to a party wall or party floor structure, including cutting in to insert beams, joist hangers or a damp proof course, raising or underpinning the wall, and cutting away chimney breasts, are notifiable under Section 2 with two months' notice to the adjoining owner; new foundations within 3m and deeper than the neighbour's are notifiable under Section 6 with one month's notice. Fixing an independent lining to a party wall without cutting into it is not normally notifiable, but a schedule of condition is prudent. No notifiable work is to start until the Act has been complied with.",
    "Sewerage undertaker: a new connection to the public sewer needs the undertaker's consent under Section 106 of the Water Industry Act 1991, and building over or within 3m of a public sewer, over a manhole, or where the sewer is more than 3m deep or larger than 225mm needs a build-over agreement with a CCTV survey before and after; the new supplies to each flat need the water undertaker's agreement to separate meters. Gas and electricity suppliers are to be asked for separate metered supplies to each flat before the design of the meter positions is fixed.",
    "Fire and rescue authority: the common parts of a building containing two or more flats fall within the Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005, so building control consults the fire and rescue authority on the plans and the fire safety information for the building is to be handed to the responsible person under Regulation 38 at completion, with a fire risk assessment of the common parts commissioned by the owner before occupation.",
    "Local housing authority: where the flats are let, the authority's minimum space and amenity standards, licensing (where any flat is not self-contained or is shared) and the Housing Health and Safety Rating System apply alongside the Building Regulations, and the authority's private sector housing team is to be consulted where a flat is small.",
    "NOTE — The party wall notices and the sewer connection consent are the two consultations that most often delay a flat conversion. Serve the notices when the structural design is settled, not when the contractor is booked."]},
  {c:"Windows & External Doors", t:"Windows, Doors and Glazing", p:[
    "Replacement windows and external doors are controlled fittings and are to have a whole-window U-value not exceeding 1.4 W/m²K and doors 1.4 W/m²K in accordance with Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.2, with the work notified or carried out by a registered competent person. Where existing windows are retained they are to be overhauled, draught stripped and made to open and close. New windows in an existing opening are to provide background ventilation no worse than the existing and not less than 10,000mm² equivalent area to a habitable room in a single-storey flat (8000mm² in a maisonette) or 4000mm² to a wet room, as the Ventilation note.",
    "Glazing in critical locations, being any glazing within 800mm of floor level, within 1500mm of floor level in a door or within 300mm of a door edge, to be safety glass to BS EN 12600 Class 2 or better and marked. Windows with a sill lower than 800mm above floor level to be guarded or restricted to a 100mm opening, and any window opening onto a common stair or corridor restricted so it cannot obstruct the escape route.",
    "Where a bedroom or habitable room on a floor not more than 4.5m above ground is an inner room or relies on a window for escape, the window is to be an emergency egress window with an unobstructed openable area of not less than 0.33m² and not less than 450mm in both height and width, with the bottom of the openable area between 800mm and 1100mm above floor level.",
    "Flat entrance doorsets are specified in the Means of Escape and Security sections and are a single doorset tested for both fire and security. Communal entrance doors to have a door closer, access control and a viewer or camera.",
    "NOTE — Requirement O1 overheating does not apply to a material change of use, but a single-aspect south or west facing flat with large glazing will overheat and some authorities impose an overheating assessment by planning condition. Where glazing is replaced, a low g-value glass and external or internal shading cost little and are to be considered."]},
  {c:"Security (Part Q)", t:"Secure Doors and Windows (Requirement Q1)", p:[
    "Requirement Q1 applies to dwellings formed by a material change of use under Regulation 6(1)(j). All easily accessible doors and windows, being every door and window at ground level, at basement level, and any above whose sill or threshold is within 2m of an accessible flat roof, balcony, bay roof or other climbable feature, together with the communal entrance door and every flat entrance door, are to be secure doorsets and windows shown by test to meet PAS 24:2016 or an equivalent standard, with the certificates supplied to building control.",
    "Where an existing window or door in an easily accessible position is retained, it is to be upgraded so far as practicable with laminated glass, key-operated locks and hinge bolts, or replaced; the Approved Document allows retained doors and windows to be assessed against Appendix B for a bespoke timber door where a tested doorset is not appropriate to a listed building or conservation area. Letter plates to have a flap and restricted aperture, doorsets to have a chain or viewer, and frames to be fixed to the structure with the fixings the test specifies.",
    "NOTE — Part Q on a conversion is often missed because the practice assumes it is a new-build requirement. It is listed in Regulation 6 for a change of use creating a dwelling, and building control will ask for the PAS 24 certificates at completion."]},
  {c:"EV Charging (Part S)", t:"Electric Vehicle Charging (Requirement S2)", p:[
    "Requirement S2 and Regulation 44E apply where a building undergoing a material change of use to dwellings has associated parking and the building work includes the car park or the electrical infrastructure serving it. An electric vehicle charge point is to be installed for each dwelling with an associated parking space, being a minimum 7 kW untethered charge point with a Type 2 socket to BS EN 62196, a universal socket and smart functionality on a dedicated circuit, positioned so a vehicle in the space can be charged. Where the incoming electrical supply cannot serve every space without an upgrade, charge points are to be installed for as many spaces as the supply allows and cable routes provided to the remainder; in a covered car park, charge points go to the uncovered spaces first with cable routes to the covered spaces.",
    "The exemptions for a change of use are for listed buildings, buildings in a conservation area and scheduled monuments where compliance would unacceptably alter the character or appearance of the building; the £3,600 grid connection cost cap applies to new buildings and not to a change of use. Where the conversion has no associated parking the requirement does not arise, and that is to be recorded on the drawings so building control can close the point.",
    "NOTE — Confirm the supply capacity with the distribution network operator at design stage; a house converted to flats often needs a new incoming supply for the separate meters in any case, and the charge point loads are to be included in that application."]},
  {c:"Extra Notes & External Works", t:"External Works, Stores and Access", p:[
    "Refuse and recycling storage for each flat as the Drainage & Waste note, within 30m of each flat entrance and 25m of the collection point, on a hard level path; cycle storage where the planning permission requires it, covered, secure and accessible without carrying cycles through a flat. External paths, steps and ramps to the flat entrances to be firm, even and lit, with steps having equal rises of not more than 170mm, a handrail, and guarding of 1100mm to any drop of more than 600mm to Approved Document K.",
    "Separate meter boxes for gas and electricity to each flat in positions agreed with the suppliers and, for gas, on an external wall with the required clearances from openings; post boxes at the communal entrance or a letter plate to each flat entrance door. External lighting on presence detection or a photocell with an efficacy of not less than 75 lumens per circuit-watt.",
    "Where gardens are subdivided, boundary treatments are to comply with the planning permission and new hardstanding is to be permeable or drained within the site. Where an external stair is provided as a means of escape it is to be to Approved Document B Volume 1 Section 3 with fire-resisting construction to the wall it passes and guarding of 1100mm.",
    "Balconies and roof terraces formed as part of the conversion to have guarding of not less than 1100mm to Approved Document K, designed for the horizontal loads in BS EN 1991-1-1, not climbable and not passing a 100mm sphere: either a traditional metal or timber balustrade with balusters at not more than 100mm clear, or a glass balustrade of laminated toughened glass to BS 6180 with a handrail unless the glass is a tested free-standing system, fixed with the manufacturer's structural calculation. Balconies within 1m of the boundary, or above a lower roof, are to be checked for fire spread and privacy under the planning permission.",
    "Rainwater harvesting, greywater recovery and waste water heat recovery may be specified where the client or the planning permission requires them: rainwater harvesting to BS EN 16941-1 with the storage sized for the roof area and the non-potable supply clearly labelled and separated from the wholesome supply by a Category 5 backflow arrangement; greywater systems to BS 8525-1; and waste water heat recovery units to the manufacturer's MCS or SAP Appendix Q listing so that they can be entered in the energy assessment."]},
  {c:"Conservation of Fuel & Power", t:"Change to Energy Status", p:[
    "Requirement L1 applies to the building as converted. A material change of use is a change to the energy status of the building, and the guidance in Approved Document L, Volume 1 for existing dwellings applies.",
    "Existing thermal elements that are retained are to be assessed and upgraded where they fall below the threshold for a retained thermal element. Where a thermal element is renovated and the renovated area exceeds 50% of the surface area of that element or 25% of the total building envelope, it is to be upgraded to the standard for a renovated thermal element.",
    "New and replacement windows and doors to meet the standards for replacement elements in Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.2, and new fixed building services the minimum efficiency standards in Approved Document L Volume 1 and the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide.",
    "New and replacement fixed building services are to be commissioned to the manufacturer's procedures and CIBSE guidance with a commissioning notice given to building control within five days of completion and controls as the Heating note. Where new windows and doors are added the total area of openings in each flat is to be kept within the limits for an extension unless the SAP shows compliance, and thermal bridging at the new junctions is to follow the accredited details or be calculated. The occupier of each flat is to be given the operating and maintenance instructions for the heating, hot water and ventilation systems and a plan of the fire safety provisions.",
    "An Energy Performance Certificate is to be produced for each flat on completion.",
    "NOTE — Approved Documents L1 and F1, 2026 editions, come into force on 24 March 2027. Work with a full plans application submitted before that date remains under the current standards provided work commences before 24 March 2028. Confirm the applicable thresholds and target U-values for retained and renovated thermal elements against the edition in force at the date of submission."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Lintels, Beams and Fire Protection", p:[
    "Lintels over openings in masonry to be proprietary steel lintels to BS EN 845-2 with a thermal break where they cross a cavity, or precast prestressed concrete lintels, selected from the manufacturer's tables for the span and the load with not less than 150mm end bearing on whole blocks or bricks (never on cut pieces) and padstones where the manufacturer requires them, the masonry not overhanging the lintel toe by more than 25mm, the toe projecting beyond the frame head, insulation between the lintel and the frame and a separate damp proof course tray with stop ends and weep holes over any external lintel that has no built-in one. Lintels and beams that carry a floor or the fire-resisting enclosure of a stair, and not only the roof, are to be protected to give 30 minutes fire resistance (60 where the element requires it) by encasement in fire-resisting board with staggered joints or an intumescent coating applied to the certified thickness for the section factor, with the certificate given to building control. Steel beams to BS EN 10025 on padstones to the engineer's design, primed, and with timber joists on hangers or a bolted timber plate rather than resting on the flange; internal door openings in non-loadbearing masonry may use a 65mm concrete plank lintel up to 750mm and a 150mm lintel at 900mm with 100mm bearings."]},
  {c:"External Walls, Internal Walls & Roofs", t:"Existing Timber Frame External Wall — Upgrade", opt:1, p:[
    "Where the existing external wall is timber framed, it is retained as a thermal element and upgraded by filling the stud depth with mineral wool or rigid board where the existing insulation is absent or degraded and lining internally with 37.5mm to 62.5mm insulated plasterboard with a vapour control layer to bring the wall to 0.30 W/m²K, or by adding an insulated over-stud layer externally beneath new cladding where the elevation is to be re-clad; the breather membrane and the drained and vented cavity behind the cladding are to be inspected and reinstated, the sole plate and any decayed studs replaced, and a condensation risk analysis to BS EN ISO 13788 provided because the added internal insulation changes the dew point within the frame. Cavity barriers at every floor level and at the separating wall lines are to be provided where absent, as a timber frame cavity can carry fire between flats."]},
  {c:"External Walls, Internal Walls & Roofs", t:"Movement Joints, Wall Ties, Cavity Trays and Damp Proof Courses", p:[
    "Vertical movement joints in the outer leaf at not more than 12m centres in clay brickwork, 7.5m to 9m in calcium silicate brickwork and dense concrete blockwork and 6m in aircrete blockwork, at not more than 1.5m from corners in parapets and at half the spacing in parapets and free-standing walls, and wherever the length of a panel exceeds three times its height or the wall changes thickness or height; joint width not less than 1.3mm per metre between joints in clay brickwork (16mm at 12m) and 10mm in other masonry, formed with a compressible filler and a low-modulus sealant and tied across with debonded ties. Wall ties to BS EN 845-1 of the type and length for the cavity width (PD 6697: 200mm long for a 50–75mm cavity, 225mm for 76–100mm, 250mm for 101–125mm, 275mm for 126–150mm), at 900mm horizontal and 450mm vertical centres staggered (2.5 per m²), at not more than 300mm vertical centres within 225mm of jambs, unbonded edges and movement joints, set level or with a slight fall to the outer leaf and kept free of mortar droppings; ties for cavities over 150mm to the manufacturer's data.",
    "Damp proof courses to BS 6398 in both leaves at not less than 150mm above finished ground, continuous with the floor membrane and with the existing damp proof course where new work meets old, vertical damp proof courses at every reveal and abutment where the cavity is closed, and cavity trays with 150mm upstands, stop ends and not fewer than two weep holes per tray at not more than 900mm centres over every opening, at abutments and at the base of the cavity where it does not extend 225mm below the damp proof course. Vapour control layers to insulated framed walls of not less than 500 gauge polyethylene or a taped foil facing, lapped 100mm onto the floor membrane and sealed."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Flat Entrance Doors and Escape Within Flats — Detail", p:[
    "Flat entrance doorsets to be FD30S fire doorsets with intumescent strips and cold smoke seals, a self-closing device, three hinges of a material with a melting point above 800°C, a lock that can be opened from inside without a key (a thumb-turn) so escape is never impeded, a letter plate with an intumescent liner where fitted, and no glazing other than fire-resisting glazing in a tested frame; the doorset is also a Part Q secure door and the fire and security certification must relate to the same tested doorset. Within a flat with a protected entrance hall the travel distance from the entrance door to the door of every habitable room is not to exceed 9m, no habitable room is to be an inner room off another habitable room, and glazing in the hall enclosure is to be fire-resisting to 1.1m above floor level. In a studio flat the cooking area is to be sited so that the escape route from the sleeping area to the entrance door does not pass through it, and enclosed or fitted with a heat alarm and a sprinkler where the layout cannot achieve that."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"External Fire Spread — Boundaries, Unprotected Areas and Roof Coverings", p:[
    "External walls within 1m of the relevant boundary are to have 30 minutes fire resistance from both sides (60 minutes where they separate buildings), an external surface of Class B-s3, d2 or better, and unprotected areas (windows, doors and combustible cladding) limited to small areas not exceeding 1m² and not less than 4m apart. Walls more than 1m from the boundary may have unprotected areas up to the limits in Approved Document B Volume 1 Section 11 for a residential building: 5.6m² at 1m, 12m² at 2m, 18m² at 3m, 24m² at 4m, 30m² at 5m and unlimited at 6m or more, measured for each side of the building facing that boundary; where cladding is worse than Class B-s3, d2 and more than 1mm thick, half its area counts as unprotected. The relevant boundary is the site boundary or the centre line of an adjoining road, canal or river.",
    "Roof coverings within 6m of the boundary are to be classified BROOF(t4) to BS EN 13501-5, which tiles, slates, felt on a substrate, single-ply membranes, GRP systems and glass with the appropriate classification achieve; thermoplastic rooflights are limited in area and distance as Section 12 and are not to be within 6m of the boundary unless classified. The classification of the roof covering, the rooflights and any cladding is to be stated on the drawings, and the position of the relevant boundary shown on the site plan so building control can check the unprotected area calculation."]},
  {c:"Drainage & Waste", t:"Drainage — Depths, Chambers, Branch Pipes, Building Over Sewers and Pumped Drainage", p:[
    "Below-ground drains to be laid with not less than 600mm cover under gardens and fields and 900mm under drives and roads (1200mm for rigid pipes under roads), or protected by a reinforced concrete slab on a compressible layer with 75mm of granular material between the pipe and the slab where the cover is less; bedded on and surrounded by 100mm of pea gravel, with flexible joints and rocker pipes not more than 600mm long each side of a wall or foundation and 50mm clearance in the opening, or the foundation carried down below the drain and the drain sleeved. Inspection chambers at every change of direction or gradient, at junctions and at not more than 45m centres, 450mm diameter to 1.2m deep, with double-sealed bolt-down covers inside the building and vehicle-rated covers in drives; rodding eyes at the head of each run. Drains passing under a floor slab to have a 1m wide strip of A142 mesh in the slab over the run and a 150mm concrete surround where the crown is within 300mm of the underside of the slab.",
    "Above-ground pipework to BS EN 12056-2 with 75mm seal traps and branch lengths not exceeding 1.7m for a 32mm basin waste (3m at 40mm), 3m for a 40mm bath or shower waste (4m at 50mm) and 6m for a 100mm WC branch, beyond which anti-vacuum traps or a ventilated branch are needed; no branch is to connect within 200mm below a WC branch connection, the stack is to have a long-radius bend at its foot, and an air admittance valve to BS EN 12380 may replace an open vent only where at least one stack on the drain remains open to the air and the valve is above the highest spill-over level in an accessible ventilated position. A pumped WC or macerator is permitted only where a gravity WC is also available in the dwelling, with pumping stations to BS EN 12056-4 and BS EN 12050 with a non-return valve, alarm and 24 hours' storage. All new drains to be tested by air or water before backfilling and again at completion."]},
 ]}
};
